insert into public.questions
  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation)
values
  ($q$ei-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Many assumed the small bookstore would close within a year of the chain store's arrival. ______ it has since expanded to two more locations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bookstore expanding contradicts the expectation that it would close, so a contrast word like 'Instead' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The lecture covered the causes of the recession in great detail. ______ it left many questions about the recovery unanswered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","For example,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause presents a limitation of the lecture, contrasting with the thoroughness described first, so 'However' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The new policy reduced paperwork for small businesses. ______ it introduced a separate reporting requirement that many owners found equally burdensome.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","As a result,","For instance,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a simultaneous downside alongside the benefit mentioned first, which 'At the same time' captures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Researchers first hypothesized that the fish avoided the reef due to pollution. ______ water samples showed pollution levels well within normal range.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","Specifically,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The water sample results undercut the original hypothesis, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The festival relies entirely on volunteer labor. ______ it manages to attract over 10,000 attendees each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Attracting a large crowd despite relying only on volunteers is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The novel's first half follows a single narrator in painstaking detail. ______ the second half jumps between five different perspectives across two decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A single narrator versus five shifting perspectives describes two structurally opposite halves, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city increased funding for public transit by 20%. ______ ridership grew by only 3% over the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A large funding increase producing only a small ridership gain is an unexpected, contrasting result, so 'Nonetheless' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The two researchers disagreed on nearly every methodological point. ______ they arrived at strikingly similar conclusions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence states two separate true facts in tension—disagreement on method, agreement on conclusions—calling for a plain contrast word. 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the agreement replaced the disagreement, and 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct subjects side by side, not one pair's unexpected outcome, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's earnings fell short of projections for the third straight quarter. ______ its stock price rose after the announcement, surprising analysts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Predictably,","Similarly,","As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage itself notes the outcome 'surprised analysts,' so the transition should flag the unexpected result rather than call it predictable.$q$),
  ($q$ei-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The trail is only two miles long. ______ its steep elevation gain makes it one of the park's most difficult hikes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A short trail that is still very difficult is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers made their first powered flight in 1903 near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.
• The flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet.
• Their success followed years of glider experiments.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the brevity of the historic flight relative to its later significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though it lasted only 12 seconds, the Wright brothers' 1903 flight near Kitty Hawk marked the dawn of powered aviation.","The Wright brothers experimented with gliders for several years before 1903.","Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, is known for its consistent winds.","The Wright brothers' flight covered a distance of 120 feet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A combines the flight's short duration (12 seconds) with its outsized historical significance, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.
• It has no bridges crossing its main stem.
• Much of its basin is inaccessible by road.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the river's exceptional water volume. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.","The Amazon River has no bridges crossing its main stem.","Much of the Amazon basin cannot be reached by road.","The Amazon flows through several South American countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly conveys the river's water volume, which is the specific goal stated in the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in both Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911).
• She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.
• She discovered the elements polonium and radium.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the uniqueness of Curie's achievement among Nobel laureates. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.","Marie Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium.","Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize in 1903.","Nobel Prizes are awarded in several different categories."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A states the specific fact about Curie's singular distinction among laureates, matching the stated goal exactly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Barrier Reef spans over 2,300 kilometers.
• It is visible from space.
• It comprises nearly 3,000 individual reefs.$q$, $q$The student wants to convey the reef's enormous scale using a single striking detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Barrier Reef is large enough to be visible from space.","The Great Barrier Reef is home to a wide variety of marine life.","Coral reefs face threats from rising ocean temperatures.","The reef is made up of nearly 3,000 separate smaller reefs."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being visible from space is the single most striking detail conveying scale, better fulfilling the goal than the more technical detail in choice D.$q$),
  ($q$ei-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof.
• It was designed to be an easy-to-learn universal second language.
• Today it has an estimated 2 million speakers worldwide.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce Esperanto by explaining the purpose behind its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof as an easy-to-learn universal second language.","Esperanto currently has an estimated 2 million speakers.","L. L. Zamenhof lived during the 19th century.","Many constructed languages have been invented over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A links the creator, date, and stated purpose (an easy-to-learn universal language), directly explaining why Esperanto was created.$q$),
  ($q$ei-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood to the gills; one pumps blood to the rest of the body.
• The main heart stops beating when the octopus swims.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a surprising consequence of the octopus's unusual circulatory system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because its main heart is the one that pumps blood to the rest of its body, that circulation briefly stops every time the octopus swims.","An octopus's main heart pumps blood to the rest of its body, while its other two hearts serve only the gills.","An octopus has three separate hearts to circulate its blood.","Two of an octopus's three hearts pump blood to its gills."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the main heart's specific role with the fact that it stops during swimming to state the surprising consequence; the others restate isolated notes or describe the hearts' division of labor without naming that consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.
• Its exact destruction date and cause remain debated by historians.
• It is believed to have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize scholarly uncertainty about the library's demise. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians still debate exactly when and how the Library of Alexandria was destroyed.","The Library of Alexandria held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","The library was among the largest in the ancient world.","Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly addresses the ongoing scholarly debate about the library's destruction, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bamboo can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day.
• It is technically classified as a grass, not a tree.
• Some species are used in construction due to their strength-to-weight ratio.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight bamboo's remarkable growth rate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some bamboo species can grow as much as 91 centimeters in a single day.","Bamboo is classified as a grass rather than a tree.","Bamboo's strength-to-weight ratio makes it useful in construction.","Bamboo grows in a variety of climates around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states a growth-rate statistic, which directly accomplishes the stated goal of highlighting bamboo's growth speed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Its surface temperature is hot enough to melt lead.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce a fact about Venus's unusual relationship between its day length and year length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A single day on Venus lasts longer than a full Venusian year.","Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets.","Venus's surface is hot enough to melt lead.","Venus is the second planet from the sun."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that addresses the specific relationship between day length and year length named in the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.
• This period, called the African Humid Period, ended roughly 5,000 years ago.
• Cave paintings in the region depict animals like giraffes and hippos.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that the Sahara was once green. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cave paintings in the region depict animals such as giraffes and hippos, species associated with wetter climates.","Cave paintings have been found throughout the Sahara region.","The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.","The African Humid Period ended roughly 5,000 years ago."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A cites concrete evidence—animals tied to wetter climates—for the claim; the vague mention of cave paintings omits that specific detail, and the other two choices merely restate the claim itself or an indirect date rather than supporting evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if stored properly.
• Archaeologists have found 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that was still edible.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide a concrete historical example that illustrates honey's exceptional shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have discovered 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that remained edible.","Honey's low moisture content helps prevent bacterial growth.","Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar.","Egyptian tombs have been found to contain many preserved goods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A gives the specific historical example (ancient Egyptian honey) requested by the prompt, rather than the general explanation in choice B.$q$),
  ($q$ei-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Eiffel Tower grows about 15 centimeters taller in summer.
• This is due to thermal expansion of the iron.
• It was originally intended to be a temporary structure.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Eiffel Tower's height changes seasonally. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Eiffel Tower's iron structure expands in warmer temperatures, making it about 15 centimeters taller in summer.","The Eiffel Tower was originally meant to be a temporary structure.","The Eiffel Tower is a famous landmark located in Paris.","Iron is a metal that is commonly used in construction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the cause (thermal expansion) to the effect (a 15-centimeter height change), directly explaining the phenomenon.$q$),
  ($q$ei-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea otters hold hands while sleeping to keep from drifting apart.
• They wrap themselves in kelp for the same reason.
• Sea otters are a keystone species in kelp forest ecosystems.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a specific behavior otters use to stay together while resting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sea otters often hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart from one another.","Sea otters are considered a keystone species in their ecosystem.","Kelp forests support a wide diversity of marine life.","Sea otters live primarily along the Pacific coast."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A directly describes the specific resting behavior (holding hands) requested by the prompt.$q$),
  ($q$ei-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for six months. ______ , traffic congestion in the downtown area noticeably worsened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closure caused the congestion, so a result/consequence transition is needed: 'As a result.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two hearts pump blood to the gills.
• One heart pumps blood to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify how many hearts octopuses have. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Octopuses have three hearts: two that pump blood to the gills and one that pumps blood to the rest of the body.","Octopuses are known for their unusual circulatory systems.","Blood is pumped differently in octopuses than in most animals.","Octopuses have gills that allow them to breathe underwater."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific number of hearts (three) and their functions, directly accomplishing the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novelist rarely gives interviews. ______ whenever she does speak publicly, journalists scrutinize every word.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her rarely speaking publicly is the cause of the intense scrutiny when she does — a consequence relationship, signaled by 'Consequently.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• City A implemented a bike-share program in 2015; ridership reached 2 million trips per year by 2020.
• City B implemented a similar program in 2016; ridership reached 800,000 trips per year by 2020.
• Both cities have similar populations and climates.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a key difference in outcomes between the two cities' programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite similar populations and climates, City A's bike-share program achieved far higher ridership than City B's by 2020.","Both cities launched bike-share programs in the mid-2010s.","City A and City B have similar populations and climates.","City B's program began one year after City A's."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A both highlights the ridership gap (2 million vs. 800,000) and uses the similar-conditions detail to show the difference isn't explained by population or climate.$q$),
  ($q$ei-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Proponents argue that the new tax would primarily affect large corporations. ______ , an independent analysis found that over 60% of the tax's burden would ultimately fall on consumers through higher prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","In contrast,","Granted,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The analysis directly rebuts the proponents' claim with contradicting data, which calls for a plain contrast word. 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct, parallel subjects rather than correcting a single claim, and 'Granted' concedes a point before a main argument rather than introducing rebutting evidence, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.
• Energy costs for street lighting fell by 40% over the next two years.
• Some residents complained the new lights were too bright.$q$, $q$The student wants to acknowledge both a benefit and a drawback of the streetlight replacement. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the LED streetlights cut energy costs by 40% within two years, some residents found them uncomfortably bright.","The city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.","Energy costs for street lighting fell significantly after 2018.","Some residents were unhappy with the new streetlights."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines both the benefit (lower energy costs) and the drawback (complaints about brightness) that the prompt asks for.$q$),
  ($q$ei-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals before selecting a finalist. ______ , the selected proposal still required several rounds of revision before approval.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Therefore,","For example,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite being chosen as the finalist, the proposal still needed revision — a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Scientists are unsure exactly why Venus's rotation is reversed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusual Venus's rotation is compared to other planets. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike most planets in the solar system, Venus rotates in the opposite direction, a mystery scientists have yet to fully explain.","Venus is the second planet from the sun.","A day on Venus lasts longer than a Venusian year.","Scientists continue to study the planets in the solar system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A directly emphasizes the unusual reversed rotation and its unexplained cause, matching the student's stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dust and pollen gradually accumulate on solar panels over the summer. ______ their energy output can drop by more than 15% if the panels are never cleaned.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","In contrast,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in output is a direct consequence of the dust buildup described first, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries by different dynasties.
• Its total length, including all branches, exceeds 21,000 kilometers.
• Contrary to popular belief, it is not actually visible to the naked eye from the Moon.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception about the Great Wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often claimed to be visible from the Moon, the Great Wall cannot actually be seen from that distance with the naked eye.","The Great Wall was constructed over the course of many centuries.","The Great Wall's total length exceeds 21,000 kilometers, including all its branches.","Several different Chinese dynasties contributed to building the wall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the popular misconception about visibility from the Moon, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city commissioned dozens of murals to beautify its downtown, expecting the project to reduce vandalism. ______ reports of graffiti and property damage actually increased in the months following the murals' completion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the murals were expected to reduce vandalism but vandalism rose instead, the clause names what actually happened in place of that expectation, which 'Instead' signals. 'Nonetheless' would suggest the increase occurred despite some obstacle rather than as the opposite of a prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The platypus is one of only five species of mammals that lay eggs.
• Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.
• The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how rare it is for a mammal to lay eggs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The platypus is one of just five mammal species that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.","Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.","The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.","Mammals are typically distinguished from other animals by giving birth to live young."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific rarity of egg-laying among mammals, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The archaeological team spent three seasons excavating the site without finding anything of note. ______ in the final week of the dig, they unearthed a cache of coins dating back over a thousand years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three fruitless seasons followed by a sudden discovery is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals; the other choices wrongly suggest the outcome was predictable or a logical consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Satellite data shows Antarctica lost about 40 billion tons of ice per year in the 1980s.
• By the 2010s, the annual ice loss had risen to roughly 250 billion tons per year.
• Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be accelerating the melting of ice shelves from below.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate how dramatically the rate of ice loss has accelerated over time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Antarctica's annual ice loss climbed from about 40 billion tons in the 1980s to roughly 250 billion tons by the 2010s.","Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be melting ice shelves from below.","Antarctica lost about 40 billion tons of ice per year in the 1980s.","By the 2010s, Antarctica's annual ice loss had risen to roughly 250 billion tons per year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A pairs both figures to show the scale of change over time; the others give the cause of melting or just one data point, neither of which demonstrates the acceleration by itself.$q$),
  ($q$ei-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bakery only opened its doors six months ago. ______ it already has a loyal following that lines up before sunrise on weekends.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having a devoted following despite being so new is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press in Europe around 1440.
• Before the press, books were copied by hand, a process that could take months.
• The printing press allowed identical copies of a text to be produced quickly and cheaply.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast book production before and after the printing press. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before Gutenberg's press, copying a single book by hand could take months, but afterward, identical copies could be produced quickly and cheaply.","Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press around 1440.","The printing press is considered one of the most important inventions in history.","Gutenberg's original printing press used movable metal type."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that directly contrasts the slow, laborious hand-copying process with the speed and low cost enabled by the press.$q$),
  ($q$ei-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists at the central bank predicted that inflation would ease significantly by the end of the year. ______ the most recent consumer price data showed inflation holding steady at nearly the same rate as six months earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Nonetheless,","By contrast,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economists' prediction of easing inflation is replaced by the opposite outcome, calling for 'Instead.' 'Nonetheless' implies the steady rate persisted despite some obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, and 'By contrast' would require two distinct things being compared side by side, not one prediction proven wrong, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Arctic tern migrates from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year.
• This round trip covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.
• Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary distance covered by the Arctic tern's migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Arctic tern's annual round-trip migration between the Arctic and Antarctic covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.","Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.","The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic and spends its winters in the Antarctic.","Many bird species undertake long migrations each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific 70,000-kilometer distance, which is the detail needed to emphasize the migration's extraordinary length.$q$),
  ($q$ei-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Public health officials initially projected that a new vaccine would be available to the general public within three months of approval. ______ manufacturing delays pushed the wider rollout back by nearly a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","As anticipated,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy delay contradicts the original three-month projection, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest stands at 8,849 meters, the highest point above sea level on Earth.
• The mountain is officially shared by Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.
• Climbers face extreme risks from low oxygen levels and unpredictable weather near the summit.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific elevation that makes Everest the world's tallest peak above sea level. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At 8,849 meters, Mount Everest is the highest point above sea level on Earth.","Mount Everest sits on the border between Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.","Climbers attempting Everest face serious risks from low oxygen and severe weather.","Many mountaineers dream of one day climbing Mount Everest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A gives the precise elevation figure that directly supports the claim about Everest's status as the tallest peak above sea level.$q$),
  ($q$ei-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest summer in decades. ______ the drought-tolerant garden that the Alvarez family planted last spring stayed green throughout the season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The garden thriving despite the severe drought is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree written in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and Greek.
• It was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.
• Jean-François Champollion used the Greek text to help decipher Egyptian hieroglyphics in 1822.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Rosetta Stone made it possible to decode hieroglyphics. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Rosetta Stone repeated the same decree in Greek alongside hieroglyphics, Champollion was able to use the Greek to decipher the hieroglyphic script in 1822.","The Rosetta Stone was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.","The Rosetta Stone contains a decree written in three different scripts.","Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphics were used for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the stone's three-script format to the specific mechanism by which Champollion deciphered hieroglyphics, fulfilling the goal of explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The factory's new automated assembly line was expected to eliminate most manual labor positions. ______ the company ended up hiring more workers to operate and maintain the new machinery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hiring more workers directly contradicts the expectation of eliminating jobs, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybees perform a "waggle dance" to communicate the location of food sources to other bees.
• The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun.
• The duration of the waggle run indicates the distance to the food source.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain precisely how the waggle dance conveys both direction and distance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through the waggle dance, bees signal a food source's direction by the angle of the dance relative to vertical and its distance by the duration of the waggle run.","Honeybees perform a waggle dance to communicate with other bees.","The angle of a bee's waggle dance relative to vertical indicates a food source's direction relative to the sun.","The duration of a bee's waggle run indicates how far away a food source is."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A specifies both mechanisms—angle for direction and duration for distance—together; B is too vague to explain the mechanism at all, and C and D each capture only one of the two required pieces.$q$),
  ($q$ei-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The violinist injured her hand in a car accident, and doctors doubted she would ever perform again. ______ she returned to the stage eighteen months later to a standing ovation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her triumphant return contrasts with the doctors' doubts, a relationship best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Titanic was, at the time, the largest passenger ship ever built.
• It sank on its maiden voyage in April 1912 after striking an iceberg.
• The ship carried far fewer lifeboats than were needed for all passengers and crew.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many people died when the Titanic sank. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Titanic carried far fewer lifeboats than needed for everyone aboard, a large share of its passengers and crew had no way to escape.","The Titanic was the largest passenger ship built at the time.","The Titanic sank in April 1912 after striking an iceberg on its maiden voyage.","Shipbuilding technology advanced rapidly in the early twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A identifies the specific cause — insufficient lifeboats — that explains the high death toll, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For decades, archaeologists maintained that the stone circle served primarily as a burial ground. ______ recent excavations of nearby postholes suggest it may have functioned as an astronomical calendar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["More recently,","Subsequently,","Eventually,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The newer excavation evidence revises a decades-old belief, which requires a transition marking an updated, present-day view—'More recently.' 'Subsequently' merely marks later timing without signaling revision, and 'Eventually' implies a final outcome of a process rather than a shift in scholarly understanding, so 'More recently' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coast redwoods can grow taller than 350 feet, making them the tallest trees on Earth.
• Their thick, fire-resistant bark can be over a foot thick.
• Redwoods can live for more than 2,000 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the exceptional height of redwood trees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Coast redwoods, which can exceed 350 feet in height, are the tallest trees on Earth.","Redwood bark can grow more than a foot thick, helping the trees survive fires.","Some individual redwood trees are more than 2,000 years old.","Redwood forests are found primarily along the coast of California and southern Oregon."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific height figure that fulfills the goal of emphasizing the redwoods' exceptional height.$q$),
  ($q$ei-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Forecasts called for heavy rain throughout the weekend of the town fair. ______ the skies cleared just in time for the opening ceremony on Saturday morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clearing skies are a welcome, contrasting turn against the rainy forecast, which 'Fortunately' captures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019; its average math scores rose from 68% to 79% by 2022.
• School B did not introduce the program; its average math scores rose from 67% to 70% over the same period.
• Both schools serve similar student populations and have comparable funding levels.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that the tutoring program likely contributed to the improvement in scores. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although School A and School B serve similar populations and have comparable funding, School A's scores rose from 68% to 79% after adopting peer tutoring, far outpacing School B's gain from 67% to 70%.","School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019.","School B's average math scores rose slightly between 2019 and 2022.","Both schools serve similar student populations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-conditions detail with both schools' score changes to suggest the tutoring program, rather than other factors, drove School A's larger gain.$q$),
  ($q$ei-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The author rewrote the opening chapter of her novel eleven times before submitting the manuscript. ______ reviewers later singled out that very chapter as the strongest part of the book.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The repeated revision finally paid off, an eventual outcome that 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying the Roman city of Pompeii in volcanic ash.
• The ash preserved buildings, artifacts, and even plaster casts of victims' final positions.
• Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Pompeii is such a valuable source of information for historians. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because volcanic ash from the 79 CE eruption preserved Pompeii's buildings, artifacts, and even the final positions of its victims, the site offers historians an unusually detailed record of Roman life.","Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying Pompeii in ash.","Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.","Pompeii was a city in the Roman Empire."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains why the site is valuable — the ash's preservation of detailed evidence — directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$After three rounds of talks ended without agreement, both sides publicly stated that a deal was unlikely before the deadline. ______ a last-minute compromise on the disputed clause allowed the agreement to be signed just hours before the deadline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A deal being signed despite the stated pessimism is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'Admittedly' would need to introduce the conceded fact itself rather than follow it, 'Instead' implies the agreement replaced an expectation rather than occurring despite one, and 'As predicted' asserts the opposite of what happened, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Emperor penguins endure Antarctic winters with temperatures as low as -60°C.
• They huddle together in large groups, rotating positions so each bird spends time in the warmer center.
• This behavior can significantly reduce individual heat loss compared to standing alone.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific method penguins use to conserve heat as a group. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Emperor penguins huddle in large groups and rotate positions so that every bird gets a turn in the warmer center, significantly cutting individual heat loss.","Emperor penguins can endure Antarctic winter temperatures as low as -60°C.","Emperor penguins are one of the few animals that breed during the Antarctic winter.","Antarctica is the coldest continent on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Choice A is the only option that specifies the rotating-huddle mechanism, directly answering how penguins conserve heat as a group.$q$),
  ($q$ei-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Light pollution from the nearby city has increased steadily over the past decade. ______ the observatory's astronomers have had to travel farther into the desert to capture clear images of faint galaxies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having to travel farther is a direct consequence of the rising light pollution, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Thomas Edison did not invent the first electric light bulb; earlier inventors had already created working versions.
• Edison's major contribution was developing a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.
• His bulb, using a carbonized filament, could burn for over 1,200 hours.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Edison invented the light bulb. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Edison is widely credited with inventing the light bulb, earlier inventors had already built working versions before he improved the filament.","Edison's carbonized filament allowed his bulb to burn for over 1,200 hours.","Edison developed a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.","Electric lighting transformed daily life in the late nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly addresses and corrects the common misconception about who invented the light bulb, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Vincent van Gogh created more than 2,000 artworks during his lifetime.
• He sold only one painting while he was alive.
• Today his paintings sell for tens of millions of dollars at auction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the contrast between Van Gogh's lack of commercial success during his life and the value of his work today. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though Van Gogh sold only one painting during his lifetime, his works now sell for tens of millions of dollars at auction.","Van Gogh created more than 2,000 artworks over the course of his life.","Van Gogh is considered one of the most influential painters in Western art history.","Auction houses regularly sell paintings by major artists for high prices."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A pairs the fact that Van Gogh sold just one painting in his life with the enormous value of his work today, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Garrett Morgan patented an improved traffic signal in 1923.
• His design added a caution position between "stop" and "go."
• Before his invention, many intersections relied only on manually operated stop signs.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify what made Morgan's traffic signal design innovative. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Garrett Morgan's 1923 traffic signal introduced a caution position between the stop and go signals.","Garrett Morgan patented his traffic signal design in 1923.","Many intersections once relied on manually operated stop signs.","Traffic signals are used to manage the flow of vehicles at intersections."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific innovation, the added caution position, that made Morgan's design new, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Naked mole rats can live more than 30 years, far longer than similarly sized rodents.
• They rarely develop cancer, a trait scientists are studying closely.
• Unlike most mammals, they cannot regulate their own body temperature well.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize a trait that makes naked mole rats especially valuable to medical researchers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Naked mole rats rarely develop cancer, a resistance that scientists are studying closely for its medical value.","Naked mole rats can live more than 30 years, unusually long for a rodent their size.","Unlike most mammals, naked mole rats cannot regulate their own body temperature well.","Naked mole rats are rodents that live in large underground colonies."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A ties the cancer resistance directly to ongoing scientific study, matching the goal of highlighting a trait valuable to researchers.$q$),
  ($q$ei-65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Malcom McLean introduced standardized steel shipping containers in 1956.
• Before containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece, a slow process called "break bulk" shipping.
• Standardization allowed goods to move seamlessly between ships, trains, and trucks.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why standardized containers made global shipping more efficient. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because standardized containers could move seamlessly between ships, trains, and trucks, they replaced the slow, piece-by-piece loading of break bulk shipping.","Malcom McLean introduced standardized steel containers in 1956.","Cargo was once loaded and unloaded piece by piece in a process called break bulk shipping.","Shipping containers are typically made of steel."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the containers' interoperability to the inefficient system they replaced, directly explaining the efficiency gain.$q$),
  ($q$ei-66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Concorde could cross the Atlantic Ocean in under 3.5 hours.
• It was retired from commercial service in 2003.
• Tickets on the Concorde cost far more than tickets on standard flights.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how fast the Concorde could travel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Concorde could cross the Atlantic Ocean in under three and a half hours.","The Concorde was retired from commercial service in 2003.","Tickets for the Concorde cost far more than standard airline tickets.","Air travel changed significantly during the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific travel time that demonstrates the Concorde's exceptional speed, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck near a Greek island in 1901.
• Modern imaging revealed more than 30 interlocking bronze gears inside it.
• Researchers believe it was used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.$q$, $q$The student wants to support the claim that the device was mechanically far ahead of its time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["With more than 30 interlocking bronze gears, the Antikythera mechanism could predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.","The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck near a Greek island in 1901.","The device could predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.","The mechanism's gears were made of bronze."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A pairs the gear count with the device's predictive power to show just how mechanically advanced it was; C states the capability alone without the mechanical complexity, and the others are incidental details.$q$),
  ($q$ei-68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tomatoes were brought to Europe from the Americas in the 16th century.
• Many Europeans believed tomatoes were poisonous because they belong to the nightshade family.
• Tomatoes did not become a common food in Europe until the 18th century.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Europeans were initially reluctant to eat tomatoes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because tomatoes belong to the nightshade family, many Europeans believed the fruit was poisonous when it first arrived from the Americas.","Tomatoes were brought to Europe from the Americas in the 16th century.","Tomatoes did not become a common food in Europe until the 18th century.","Nightshade plants are native to both the Americas and Europe."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific reason, the nightshade connection, that explains European reluctance to eat tomatoes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dead Sea Scrolls were discovered by a shepherd in caves near Qumran in 1947.
• The scrolls include some of the oldest known copies of Hebrew biblical texts.
• Scholars have spent decades translating and studying the roughly 900 manuscripts.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify who first discovered the Dead Sea Scrolls and where. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Dead Sea Scrolls were discovered by a shepherd in caves near Qumran in 1947.","The scrolls include some of the oldest known copies of Hebrew biblical texts.","Scholars have spent decades translating roughly 900 manuscripts.","Ancient texts can reveal a great deal about early religious practices."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names both who found the scrolls and where, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Certain plankton species emit light when disturbed by movement in the water.
• This glow is a defense mechanism meant to startle or attract predators of the plankton's predators.
• The phenomenon is sometimes visible as glowing blue waves at night along certain coastlines.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the biological purpose behind plankton's glowing light. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some plankton glow when disturbed as a defense mechanism, a flash meant to startle predators or attract the predators' own enemies.","Glowing plankton can sometimes be seen lighting up waves along certain coastlines at night.","Plankton are tiny organisms that live throughout the world's oceans.","Many marine organisms are capable of producing their own light."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the defensive purpose of the glow rather than merely describing when it is visible, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.
• By the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, he was almost completely deaf.
• He reportedly felt the vibrations of the orchestra through the floor to sense the music.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to illustrate how Beethoven adapted to his hearing loss while composing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though almost completely deaf by the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, Beethoven reportedly sensed the orchestra's vibrations through the floor.","Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.","By the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, Beethoven was almost completely deaf.","Beethoven reportedly sensed the orchestra's vibrations through the floor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the near-total deafness to the specific adaptation of sensing vibrations through the floor; C states the deafness alone and D states the adaptation alone, neither fully illustrating how he adapted.$q$),
  ($q$ei-72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Greenland sharks are estimated to live more than 300 years, and possibly up to 500.
• Scientists determine their age by analyzing proteins in the lens of the eye.
• They grow extremely slowly, adding less than a centimeter in length per year.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the exceptional lifespan of the Greenland shark. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Greenland sharks are estimated to live more than 300 years, and possibly as long as 500.","Scientists determine a Greenland shark's age by analyzing proteins in its eye lens.","Greenland sharks grow less than a centimeter in length per year.","Sharks are found in oceans throughout the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific lifespan figures, directly accomplishing the goal of emphasizing the shark's exceptional longevity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Swiss engineer George de Mestral invented Velcro in the 1940s.
• He got the idea after noticing burrs stuck to his dog's fur during a walk.
• Velcro consists of two strips, one with tiny hooks and one with tiny loops.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what inspired de Mestral to invent Velcro. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["George de Mestral was inspired to invent Velcro after noticing burrs stuck to his dog's fur during a walk.","Velcro consists of two strips, one with tiny hooks and one with tiny loops.","George de Mestral was a Swiss engineer working in the 1940s.","Many household inventions were developed by observing patterns in nature."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific observation, burrs on his dog's fur, that inspired the invention, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Several major cities installed pneumatic tube networks in the early 1900s to speed up mail delivery.
• Capsules carrying letters were propelled through underground tubes by compressed air.
• The systems were largely abandoned by the mid-20th century as trucks became cheaper and more flexible.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why cities eventually stopped using pneumatic tube mail systems. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cities largely abandoned their pneumatic tube mail systems by the mid-20th century as trucks became a cheaper, more flexible alternative.","Pneumatic tube systems propelled mail capsules through underground tubes using compressed air.","Several major cities installed pneumatic tube networks in the early 1900s.","Mail delivery has changed considerably over the past century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific reason, cheaper and more flexible trucks, that explains why the tube systems were abandoned.$q$),
  ($q$ei-75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between North America and central Mexico.
• No single butterfly completes the entire round trip; the journey takes three or four generations.
• Scientists still debate exactly how later generations know the route their ancestors traveled.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to highlight what makes the monarch migration especially puzzling to scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because no single monarch completes the full 3,000-mile round trip, scientists are still unsure how butterflies several generations removed from their ancestors know which route to follow.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between North America and central Mexico.","The monarch migration takes three or four generations to complete, since no single butterfly makes the full round trip.","Scientists still debate exactly how later generations know the route their ancestors traveled."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the multigenerational relay to the specific mystery of route transmission; C states the relay without the mystery, and D states the mystery without explaining why it's puzzling in the first place.$q$),
  ($q$ei-76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Panama Canal was completed in 1914 after a French attempt failed decades earlier.
• Thousands of workers died from yellow fever and malaria during construction.
• Discovering that mosquitoes spread these diseases allowed engineers to control outbreaks through sanitation measures.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how disease control efforts allowed construction of the canal to succeed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Once engineers discovered that mosquitoes spread yellow fever and malaria, sanitation measures to control the insects helped construction of the canal finally succeed.","Construction of the Panama Canal was completed in 1914.","Thousands of workers died from yellow fever and malaria during construction.","An earlier French attempt to build the canal had failed decades before."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the mosquito discovery to the sanitation measures that allowed construction to succeed, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers have used 3D printers to create simple tissue structures using living cells as "ink."
• Fully functional, transplantable organs have not yet been successfully bioprinted.
• Scientists hope the technology could eventually reduce reliance on organ donor waiting lists.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the current limitation of bioprinting technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fully functional, transplantable organs have not yet been successfully created using bioprinting technology.","Researchers have used 3D printers to create simple tissue structures from living cells.","Scientists hope bioprinting could eventually reduce reliance on organ donor waiting lists.","3D printing has been applied to many different fields in recent years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the current limitation directly, that full organs cannot yet be bioprinted, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coffee cultivation spread from Ethiopia to the Arabian Peninsula by the 15th century.
• Coffeehouses became popular gathering places for discussion and business in the Ottoman Empire.
• European traders later introduced coffee cultivation to colonies in the Americas and Asia.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how coffeehouses contributed to the drink's cultural significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Coffeehouses in the Ottoman Empire became popular gathering places where people met to discuss ideas and conduct business.","Coffee cultivation spread from Ethiopia to the Arabian Peninsula by the 15th century.","European traders introduced coffee cultivation to colonies in the Americas and Asia.","Coffee is one of the most widely traded commodities in the world today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the cultural role of coffeehouses as gathering places, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• City A introduced mandatory curbside composting in 2017; its landfill waste fell by 25% by 2022.
• City B introduced a similar program in 2018 but made participation optional; its landfill waste fell by only 6% over the same period.
• Both cities have comparable populations and household sizes.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that mandatory participation was likely responsible for City A's greater reduction in landfill waste. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although City A and City B have comparable populations, City A's mandatory composting program cut landfill waste by 25% by 2022, far more than City B's optional program, which achieved only a 6% reduction.","Both City A and City B introduced curbside composting programs in the late 2010s.","City B made participation in its composting program optional.","City A and City B have similar populations and household sizes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-conditions detail with both cities' outcomes to suggest mandatory participation, not population differences, drove the gap.$q$),
  ($q$ei-80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, on October 4, 1957.
• The satellite was about the size of a beach ball and weighed 83.6 kilograms.
• Its launch triggered the United States to accelerate funding for science education and space research.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the effect Sputnik's launch had on the United States. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sputnik's launch prompted the United States to accelerate funding for science education and space research.","The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957.","Sputnik 1 was about the size of a beach ball and weighed 83.6 kilograms.","The Space Race was a period of intense competition between two nations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific effect on the United States, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A popular blender model was found to have a faulty switch that could overheat. ______ the manufacturer recalled more than 200,000 units within a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recall was a direct consequence of the faulty switch, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'As a result,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The marathon runner tore a ligament in her ankle during the race's ninth mile. ______ she pushed through the pain and crossed the finish line under her goal time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finishing under her goal time despite a serious injury is a surprising outcome, which 'Remarkably' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics gave the newly opened restaurant lukewarm reviews in its first month. ______ within a year it was fully booked most weekends.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becoming fully booked despite an unenthusiastic start is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The young chess player lost every match at his first major tournament, drawing ridicule from commentators who doubted his talent. ______ within two years he became the youngest grandmaster in his country's history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","Because of this,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becoming a grandmaster after a ridiculed start is a direct contrast between two facts about the same person, which plain 'Yet' signals. 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the early losses never happened, and 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two separate subjects, not one person's turnaround, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The museum spent two years digitizing its entire photo archive and posting it online for free public access. ______ website traffic increased more than tenfold within the first six months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The traffic increase directly resulted from the digitization effort, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small mountain town had no access to high-speed internet for over a decade. ______ its residents relied on satellite connections that were slow and unreliable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Relying on slow satellite connections was a direct consequence of lacking high-speed internet, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Meteorologists predicted an unusually mild winter for the region based on ocean temperature patterns. ______ the area experienced record snowfall and weeks of below-freezing temperatures.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The record snowfall contradicts the prediction of a mild winter, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$City officials initially assured taxpayers that the new library wing would be completed within its original budget. ______ internal documents later revealed that costs had exceeded projections by nearly 40 percent before construction even began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Instead,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The internal documents don't just contrast with the officials' assurance—they expose the concealed truth behind it, which the intensifying transition 'In fact' best captures. Plain 'However' would only mark contrast without that revealing force, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the cost overrun replaced the assurance rather than exposing it as false, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city council banned single-use plastic bags at all retail checkouts last year. ______ sales of reusable canvas totes at local stores have tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in reusable tote sales is a direct result of the plastic bag ban, so 'Accordingly' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The neighborhood bakery closed its walk-in storefront and switched to online-only orders. ______ many longtime customers say they miss browsing the display case in person.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The customers' complaints contrast with the bakery's business decision, so 'However' correctly signals that contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup's first product update was plagued by bugs and drew harsh criticism from early users. ______ after months of fixes, the app became one of the most downloaded productivity tools of the year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The app's rise to popularity after a rocky start describes a final outcome, which 'Eventually' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$After a test rocket exploded during a routine engine trial, engineers warned that a successful launch was likely years away. ______ the redesigned rocket completed a flawless orbital launch just eight months later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Contrary to expectations,","Surprisingly,","Nonetheless,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase must explicitly tie the flawless launch back to the engineers' specific warning, which 'Contrary to expectations' does directly. 'Surprisingly' marks only the writer's reaction without referencing the stated prediction, and 'Nonetheless' suggests the launch succeeded despite a general obstacle rather than refuting a specific forecast, so 'Contrary to expectations' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The farmer switched his entire wheat crop to organic growing methods, despite warnings from neighbors that yields would suffer. ______ his harvest that year was the largest he had ever recorded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A record harvest directly contradicts the neighbors' warning of reduced yields, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The airline routinely overbooked its most popular routes to offset last-minute cancellations. ______ it ended up paying millions of dollars in compensation to bumped passengers each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The compensation costs were a direct consequence of the overbooking practice, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The volcano had shown no signs of activity for over three centuries and was widely considered extinct. ______ it erupted without warning, forcing the evacuation of nearby villages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An eruption after centuries of dormancy is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals correctly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For most of her career, the painter was celebrated for meticulously realistic portraits. ______ her final decade of work embraced bold abstraction that alienated many longtime collectors before eventually reshaping her critical reputation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","However,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence explicitly sets two distinct career phases side by side—realistic portraits versus bold abstraction—which calls for the parallel-comparison transition 'By contrast.' 'However' would mark contrast too generally without emphasizing the two-phase comparison, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the later style erased or replaced the earlier one rather than simply differing from it, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Dozens of free language-learning apps are available online. ______ a paid subscription app with no free version has attracted millions of loyal users.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Attracting millions of paying users despite abundant free competition is a concession, which 'Nonetheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The publisher printed only 2,000 copies of the debut novel, expecting modest interest given its unconventional structure. ______ word-of-mouth recommendations drove the book to sell over a million copies within its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The runaway sales directly replace the publisher's expectation of modest interest, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would suggest success happened despite some stated obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, and 'As predicted' asserts the opposite of what occurred, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$For over a century, a live keeper reliably tended the town's lighthouse each night. ______ the harbor authority replaced the position with an automated light sensor in 2005.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite the keeper's long, reliable service, the position was still eliminated — a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Marine biologists expected the bleached reef to take decades to recover. ______ coral cover rebounded to pre-bleaching levels within just six years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The quick rebound contradicts the biologists' expectation of a decades-long recovery, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The public library eliminated late fees on overdue books last year. ______ the number of returned items increased noticeably in the following months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in returns is a direct consequence of eliminating late fees, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A single saguaro cactus can store hundreds of gallons of water in its stem after a rainstorm. ______ it can survive more than a year without any additional rainfall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surviving a year without rain is a direct consequence of the cactus's water storage, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Developers promised the new wind farm would power every home in the county. ______ it currently supplies electricity to barely a third of local households.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Similarly,","For this reason,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reveals the actual, disappointing reality behind the developers' promise, which 'In fact' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company advertised the new update as a minor bug fix. ______ it introduced a complete redesign of the user interface that confused many longtime users.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A complete redesign contradicts the description of a minor fix, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$City planners promised the new rail line would open within two years. ______ permitting disputes and funding shortfalls delayed the opening by nearly a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy delay contradicts the planners' two-year promise, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city council passed one of the strictest noise ordinances in the state last year. ______ complaints about late-night noise from the entertainment district have barely declined.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Granted,","Instead,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Complaints persisting despite a newly strict ordinance is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'Granted' would need to introduce a concession before a main claim rather than follow one, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the complaints replaced the ordinance rather than continuing despite it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A nonprofit installed beehives on dozens of city rooftops to help pollinator populations. ______ local honey production in the city has increased sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in honey production is a direct consequence of the rooftop hives, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Snow leopards are notoriously difficult to spot in the wild due to their camouflage and remote habitat. ______ a wildlife photographer managed to capture video of three leopards together in a single week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Capturing three elusive leopards in one week is a striking, unexpected feat, which 'Remarkably' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The renowned conductor fell ill just hours before the sold-out performance. ______ his twenty-four-year-old assistant stepped in and led the orchestra through a flawless rendition of the symphony.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","Because of this,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A flawless performance despite the conductor's sudden illness is a direct, single-storyline contrast, which plain 'Yet' signals. 'In contrast' is for comparing two separate subjects side by side, not one crisis-and-recovery story, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the illness never actually happened, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A shipping delay cut off the roastery's usual supply of green coffee beans for nearly two months. ______ the owners began sourcing beans from three new regional farms they had never worked with before.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sourcing new farms was a direct response to the supply disruption, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For decades, historians believed the village was abandoned before the famine struck. ______ pottery shards unearthed at the site in 2019 suggest residents remained for at least another generation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["More recently,","Subsequently,","Eventually,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New 2019 evidence revises a long-held historical belief, which requires the updating transition 'More recently.' 'Subsequently' marks mere sequence without signaling a revision of prior belief, and 'Eventually' implies the final stage of a single process rather than a shift in scholarly consensus, so 'More recently' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city spent millions reinforcing its seawalls after the last major hurricane. ______ the barriers held firm when an even stronger storm struck three years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The seawalls holding firm is a direct consequence of the earlier reinforcement, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The fitness company predicted its new training app would attract a million users within the first month. ______ fewer than 50,000 people had downloaded it by the end of that period.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The low download number contradicts the company's prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The Hendersons installed a drip irrigation system on their small farm during a severe drought. ______ their crop yields held steady even as neighboring farms without the system suffered losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Steady yields during the drought were a direct consequence of the irrigation system, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The pianist did not begin formal lessons until she was sixteen, far later than most concert musicians. ______ she won a major international competition just four years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Winning a major competition despite such a late start is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The recycling plant's engineers claimed their new sorting system would cut contamination rates in half. ______ independent audits found contamination rates had barely changed one year after installation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Independent audit data directly refutes the engineers' claim, a straightforward contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' would suggest the claim is being replaced by a parallel alternative rather than disproven by evidence, and 'Nonetheless' implies the claim held true despite some obstacle, which misstates the relationship, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy rains delayed construction on the park renovation for most of the spring. ______ the project was still completed before the summer festival opened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finishing on time despite the delays is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The submersible was designed only to survey known shipwreck sites at moderate depths. ______ during one dive it detected an entirely uncharted vent ecosystem nearly five kilometers down.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Detecting an uncharted ecosystem far outside the sub's intended purpose is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$State transportation officials built a wildlife overpass above the busiest stretch of the highway. ______ animal-vehicle collisions in that section dropped by more than 90 percent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in collisions is a direct consequence of building the overpass, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many schools phased out handwriting instruction in favor of typing lessons over the past decade. ______ several districts have recently begun reintroducing cursive writing to the curriculum.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reintroducing cursive reverses the earlier trend away from handwriting, a contrast signaled by 'However.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The mechanic estimated the vintage car's restoration would take about six months. ______ sourcing original parts for the engine stretched the project to nearly three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Similarly,","For this reason,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reveals what actually happened, far exceeding the original estimate, which 'In fact' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Ticket sales at the century-old theater had declined steadily for a decade as streaming services grew popular. ______ a single viral clip of a local performance sent sales soaring by 300 percent within a month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The transition must capture just how startling a 300% jump is after a decade of decline, which 'Remarkably' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' implies the rise happened despite the decline continuing, and 'Instead' merely marks replacement without conveying the dramatic scale of the reversal, so 'Remarkably' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A sharp decline in wild bee populations has worried farmers across the region for years. ______ one orchard began renting managed honeybee hives each spring and has seen pollination rates recover fully.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The orchard's full recovery contrasts with the region-wide decline described first, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Landslides cut off the mountain village's only road for several weeks each spring. ______ residents have grown accustomed to stockpiling supplies well before the rainy season begins.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Stockpiling supplies is a direct response to the recurring road closures, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers warned that a failure to deploy the probe's solar panels would end the mission within days. ______ ground controllers remotely triggered a backup mechanism that unfolded the panels and saved the mission.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The backup mechanism's success is framed as a lucky, welcome break from the engineers' warning, which 'Fortunately' captures through its positive evaluation. 'Remarkably' notes surprise without that favorable framing, and 'Nonetheless' implies success despite a persisting obstacle rather than a rescue from one, so 'Fortunately' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The vacant lot had been an eyesore filled with debris for over a decade. ______ neighborhood volunteers transformed it into a thriving community garden within a single summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Transforming the lot despite its decade of neglect is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The manufacturer advertised that the new phone's battery would last two full days on a single charge. ______ most reviewers found it barely lasted through a single day of normal use.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The reviewers' findings directly contradict the manufacturer's claim, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The ice cream shop had guarded its signature flavor recipe for over thirty years. ______ a former employee published the full recipe online last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recipe becoming public despite decades of secrecy is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The regional almanac predicted an unusually mild, frost-free spring for the area. ______ an unexpected cold snap in late April destroyed nearly a third of the season's fruit blossoms.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The devastating cold snap replaces the almanac's prediction of a mild spring with the opposite reality, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would suggest the damage happened despite some obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tardigrades can survive temperatures ranging from nearly -272°C to 150°C.
• They can survive more than a decade without water by entering a dried-out state called desiccation.
• Tardigrades have survived direct, unprotected exposure to the vacuum of space.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how extreme the range of conditions tardigrades can survive is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tardigrades can withstand temperatures from nearly -272°C to 150°C and even survive unprotected exposure to the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades can survive more than a decade without water by entering a dried-out state.","Tardigrades are often studied by scientists interested in extreme biology.","Many tiny organisms can survive in harsh environments."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the extreme temperature range with survival in the vacuum of space, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the range of conditions tardigrades endure.$q$),
  ($q$ei-131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Nikola Tesla developed and promoted alternating current (AC) electrical systems in the late nineteenth century.
• Thomas Edison was a major proponent of direct current (DC) and worked to discredit AC.
• AC eventually became the standard for long-distance electricity transmission because it loses less energy over long distances.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why AC ultimately won out over DC for long-distance power transmission. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["AC became the long-distance standard because it loses less energy than DC over long distances.","Nikola Tesla developed alternating current systems in the late nineteenth century.","Thomas Edison worked to discredit alternating current in favor of direct current.","Electricity transmission has changed considerably since the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific technical reason — lower energy loss — that explains why AC became the long-distance standard.$q$),
  ($q$ei-132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Komodo dragons are the largest living species of lizard.
• Their saliva contains venom that prevents a victim's blood from clotting.
• A single bite can incapacitate prey many times the dragon's own size.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Komodo dragons are able to take down prey much larger than themselves. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because a Komodo dragon's venomous saliva stops blood from clotting, a single bite can incapacitate prey many times its own size.","Komodo dragons are the largest living species of lizard.","Many large predators rely on a powerful bite to subdue their prey.","Large lizards are found in a variety of habitats around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the venom's anti-clotting effect to the specific outcome of incapacitating much larger prey, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Stonehenge's smaller "bluestones" were transported from quarries in Wales, roughly 200 kilometers away.
• Some of the stones weigh as much as 4 tons.
• Researchers believe the stones were moved using sledges and timber rails rather than wheeled vehicles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how builders are believed to have transported the massive stones without wheels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers believe builders used sledges and timber rails, rather than wheeled vehicles, to move stones weighing up to 4 tons from quarries 200 kilometers away.","Stonehenge's bluestones came from quarries in Wales, roughly 200 kilometers from the site.","Some of Stonehenge's stones weigh as much as 4 tons.","Ancient monuments like Stonehenge continue to intrigue archaeologists."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific transport method, sledges and timber rails, that answers how the wheel-less builders moved the massive stones.$q$),
  ($q$ei-134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Ada Lovelace worked with Charles Babbage on his proposed Analytical Engine in the 1840s.
• She wrote a detailed algorithm for calculating Bernoulli numbers for the machine.
• Many historians consider her notes to contain the first published computer program.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific reason Ada Lovelace is often called the first computer programmer. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians often call Ada Lovelace the first computer programmer because her notes contain what is considered the first published algorithm written for a computing machine.","Ada Lovelace worked with Charles Babbage on his Analytical Engine in the 1840s.","The Analytical Engine was a mechanical computing device proposed in the 1840s.","Computing technology has advanced dramatically since the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific reason for Lovelace's title — her published algorithm — directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990 with a flawed primary mirror.
• Images from the telescope were initially blurrier and far less sharp than expected.
• A 1993 space shuttle mission installed corrective optics that fixed the problem.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how astronauts corrected the flaw in Hubble's original design. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Astronauts fixed the flaw in Hubble's 1990 mirror by installing corrective optics during a 1993 space shuttle mission.","The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990 with a flawed primary mirror.","Images from Hubble were initially blurrier than expected.","Space telescopes allow astronomers to observe without atmospheric interference."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific corrective action, installing new optics in 1993, that fixed the original mirror flaw.$q$),
  ($q$ei-136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A 2021 volcanic eruption in Iceland occurred in a relatively accessible, unpopulated valley.
• Lava flows were slow-moving and posed little danger to visitors who kept a safe distance.
• Tourist visits to the eruption site topped 100,000 within the first two months.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why this eruption drew unusually large crowds of visitors rather than triggering a mass evacuation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the eruption occurred in an accessible, unpopulated valley with slow-moving, low-danger lava, more than 100,000 visitors flocked to see it within two months rather than being evacuated.","A volcanic eruption occurred in Iceland in 2021.","Lava flows from the eruption moved slowly and posed little danger to visitors.","The eruption occurred in a relatively accessible, unpopulated valley."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines accessibility, low danger, and the visitor count to fully explain why crowds came rather than evacuees fled; C and D each give only one piece of that explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Silk Road was a network of trade routes connecting China to the Mediterranean.
• Traders carried silk, spices, and precious metals along the routes.
• The routes also facilitated the spread of religions, technologies, and diseases between distant regions.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the Silk Road spread more than just physical goods. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Beyond carrying goods like silk and spices, the Silk Road's routes also spread religions, technologies, and diseases across distant regions.","The Silk Road connected China to the Mediterranean through a network of trade routes.","Traders along the Silk Road carried silk, spices, and precious metals.","Many ancient trade routes connected distant civilizations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly contrasts physical goods with the non-material things the Silk Road spread, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Leonardo da Vinci filled thousands of notebook pages with sketches, scientific observations, and inventions.
• Much of his writing was done in mirror script, readable only when held up to a mirror.
• Many of his engineering designs, such as flying machines, were never built during his lifetime.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the unusual way da Vinci recorded many of his notes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Da Vinci recorded much of his writing in mirror script, text that is legible only when reflected in a mirror.","Da Vinci filled thousands of notebook pages with sketches and scientific observations.","Many of da Vinci's engineering designs were never built during his lifetime.","Leonardo da Vinci is remembered as both an artist and an inventor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the unusual mirror-script method itself, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The U.S. unemployment rate reached roughly 25% at the height of the Great Depression in 1933.
• Thousands of banks failed in the early 1930s, wiping out many families' savings.
• The New Deal introduced programs intended to create jobs and stabilize the economy.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific scale of joblessness during the worst year of the Great Depression. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the depth of the Great Depression in 1933, roughly one in four American workers was unemployed.","Thousands of banks failed in the early 1930s, wiping out many families' savings.","The New Deal introduced programs meant to create jobs and stabilize the economy.","The Great Depression was one of the most severe economic downturns in American history."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific unemployment figure, directly fulfilling the goal of describing the scale of joblessness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The 1986 Chernobyl disaster forced the permanent evacuation of the surrounding area.
• Human absence has allowed wolf, lynx, and elk populations in the exclusion zone to rebound.
• Some radiation levels in the zone remain too high for long-term human habitation.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to support the claim that wildlife has thrived in the absence of people. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Since humans were evacuated after the 1986 disaster, wolf, lynx, and elk populations within the Chernobyl exclusion zone have rebounded.","The 1986 Chernobyl disaster forced the permanent evacuation of the surrounding area.","Some radiation levels within the exclusion zone remain too high for long-term human habitation.","Without people around, wildlife in the exclusion zone has generally thrived."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific species that rebounded and ties that rebound to the human evacuation; D makes the same general claim without the concrete evidence, and B and C describe the evacuation or radiation without mentioning wildlife at all.$q$),
  ($q$ei-141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Axolotls are a species of salamander that can regenerate entire lost limbs.
• They can also regrow parts of their heart, spinal cord, and even portions of their brain.
• Unlike most amphibians, axolotls typically remain in an aquatic, larval-like form their entire lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the range of body parts axolotls are able to regenerate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Axolotls can regenerate not only entire limbs but also parts of their heart, spinal cord, and brain.","Axolotls are a species of salamander that lives underwater.","Unlike most amphibians, axolotls remain in a larval-like form for their entire lives.","Salamanders are amphibians known for their varied life cycles."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A lists multiple regenerated body parts, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the range of axolotl regeneration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Venus flytrap has small trigger hairs inside each of its hinged leaves.
• The trap only snaps shut if two hairs are touched within about 20 seconds of each other.
• This two-touch requirement helps the plant avoid wasting energy on false alarms like raindrops.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Venus flytrap avoids closing in response to non-prey stimuli like rain. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Venus flytrap only closes when two trigger hairs are touched within about 20 seconds of each other, it avoids wasting energy snapping shut for false alarms like raindrops.","The Venus flytrap has small trigger hairs inside each of its hinged leaves.","Venus flytraps are carnivorous plants that capture insects.","Many plants have evolved specialized structures to capture prey."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the two-touch mechanism and connects it to avoiding false alarms, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Inca Empire built a road network spanning more than 30,000 kilometers across the Andes.
• The roads connected the empire without the use of wheeled vehicles.
• Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the roads at high speed.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the scale of the Inca road network. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Inca Empire's road network stretched more than 30,000 kilometers across the Andes.","The Inca roads connected the empire without the use of wheeled vehicles.","Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the Inca roads.","The Inca Empire was one of the largest empires in pre-Columbian America."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific length of the network, directly fulfilling the goal of conveying its scale.$q$),
  ($q$ei-144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.
• The mold, later identified as Penicillium, appeared to be killing the surrounding bacteria.
• It took over a decade before penicillin was mass-produced as an antibiotic.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Fleming's discovery was accidental rather than the result of a deliberate search. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming discovered penicillin's antibacterial effect by chance, after noticing that mold had contaminated and was killing bacteria on one of his culture plates.","Alexander Fleming made his discovery in 1928.","It took more than a decade before penicillin was mass-produced as an antibiotic.","Antibiotics have saved countless lives since their introduction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A frames the discovery as a chance observation of contamination, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its accidental nature.$q$),
  ($q$ei-145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest known point in the world's oceans, reaching nearly 11,000 meters.
• Water pressure at the bottom exceeds 1,000 times the pressure at sea level.
• Specialized submersibles are required to withstand the crushing pressure at that depth.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme water pressure found at the bottom of the trench. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the bottom of the Mariana Trench, water pressure exceeds 1,000 times what it is at sea level.","The Mariana Trench reaches a depth of nearly 11,000 meters.","Specialized submersibles are needed to explore the trench's depths.","Because it is so deep, the Mariana Trench requires specialized submersibles for exploration."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the actual pressure figure—1,000 times sea-level pressure—that the goal asks to emphasize; the others describe the trench's depth or the need for submersibles without quantifying the pressure itself.$q$),
  ($q$ei-146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jane Goodall began observing wild chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.
• She documented chimpanzees stripping leaves from twigs to fish termites out of mounds.
• This was among the first documented instances of tool use by a non-human animal.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the significance of Goodall's termite-fishing observation for the scientific understanding of animal behavior. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Goodall's observation of chimpanzees stripping twigs to fish termites from mounds was among the first documented cases of tool use in a non-human animal.","Jane Goodall began observing wild chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.","Chimpanzees are closely related to humans among primates.","Many animals exhibit complex social behaviors in the wild."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the specific observation to its scientific significance as early evidence of non-human tool use, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Terracotta Army was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974.
• It consists of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers buried to guard the tomb of China's first emperor.
• No two soldiers' faces are exactly alike.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify who first discovered the Terracotta Army and how. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Terracotta Army was discovered in 1974 by farmers who were digging a well.","The Terracotta Army consists of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers.","No two soldiers in the Terracotta Army have exactly the same face.","China's first emperor is remembered for several large construction projects."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names both who made the discovery and the circumstances of how, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Namib desert beetle lives in one of the driest deserts on Earth, which receives almost no rainfall.
• Its back is covered in bumps that collect tiny water droplets from the morning fog.
• Grooves between the bumps channel the collected water directly to the beetle's mouth.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how this beetle is able to survive with almost no rainfall in its habitat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because bumps on its back collect fog droplets, which grooves then channel directly to its mouth, the beetle can get water even though its desert habitat sees almost no rainfall.","The Namib desert beetle lives in one of the driest deserts on Earth, which receives almost no rainfall.","Bumps on the beetle's back collect tiny water droplets from the morning fog.","Grooves between the bumps channel collected water to the beetle's mouth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the full fog-harvesting mechanism, from collection to delivery; C and D each give only half of that mechanism, and B states the problem without any solution.$q$),
  ($q$ei-149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed Morse code in the 1830s and 1840s.
• Common letters like "E" and "T" were assigned the shortest codes.
• This design allowed telegraph operators to transmit everyday messages more quickly.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why assigning short codes to common letters made the telegraph system more efficient. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By assigning the shortest codes to common letters like \"E\" and \"T,\" Morse code let operators transmit everyday messages more quickly.","Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed Morse code in the 1830s and 1840s.","Telegraph operators used Morse code to send messages over long distances.","Communication technology advanced rapidly in the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the short-code design for common letters to the resulting gain in transmission speed, directly explaining the efficiency.$q$),
  ($q$ei-150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Norse explorers established a settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows in present-day Newfoundland around 1000 CE.
• This was roughly 500 years before Columbus's voyages to the Americas.
• The settlement was abandoned after only a few years, likely due to conflicts with Indigenous peoples.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much earlier Norse explorers reached North America compared to Columbus. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Norse explorers reached North America at L'Anse aux Meadows around 1000 CE, roughly 500 years before Columbus's voyages.","The Norse settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows was abandoned after only a few years.","Conflicts with Indigenous peoples likely contributed to the settlement's abandonment.","Many explorers have sailed across the Atlantic Ocean over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific 500-year gap between the Norse arrival and Columbus, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mantis shrimp have among the most complex eyes in the animal kingdom, with up to 16 types of photoreceptors.
• Each eye can move independently and perceive depth on its own.
• Some species can also detect polarized light, which most animals cannot see.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusual the mantis shrimp's visual system is compared to most animals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["With up to 16 types of photoreceptors and the ability to detect polarized light invisible to most animals, the mantis shrimp has one of the most unusual visual systems in the animal kingdom.","Each of a mantis shrimp's eyes can move independently of the other.","Mantis shrimp are known for their powerful, fast-striking claws.","Many marine animals have evolved specialized sensory systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the photoreceptor count with the polarized-light ability, together showing just how unusual the mantis shrimp's vision is.$q$),
  ($q$ei-152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Bermuda Triangle is a region of the Atlantic Ocean associated with supposed mysterious disappearances.
• Insurance records, such as those of Lloyd's of London, show no unusual rate of shipping losses in the area.
• The region sees heavy marine traffic and seasonal tropical storms, both of which can account for ordinary accidents.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that the Bermuda Triangle has an unusually high rate of disappearances. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the Bermuda Triangle is famous for supposed mysterious disappearances, insurance records show no unusual rate of shipping losses in the area.","The Bermuda Triangle is a region of the Atlantic Ocean.","The region experiences heavy marine traffic and seasonal tropical storms.","Many legends have grown up around dangerous stretches of ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the popular misconception using the insurance-record evidence, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute during flight.
• Hummingbirds are the only birds capable of sustained hovering flight.
• They must feed on nectar every ten to fifteen minutes while awake to maintain their energy.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how fast a hummingbird's heart beats during flight. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["During flight, a hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute.","Hummingbirds are the only birds capable of sustained hovering flight.","Hummingbirds must feed on nectar every ten to fifteen minutes while awake.","A hummingbird's heart beats very quickly during flight."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific rate—more than 1,200 beats per minute—that the goal asks to emphasize; D makes the same general claim without a number, and B and C are true but unrelated to heart rate.$q$),
  ($q$ei-154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis Braille began developing his raised-dot reading system in the 1820s, after losing his sight as a child.
• Each Braille character is formed from a grid of up to six raised dots.
• The system was initially resisted by some educators before gaining widespread adoption decades later.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what personal experience motivated Braille to invent the system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Louis Braille, who had lost his sight as a child, began developing his raised-dot system in the 1820s.","Each Braille character is formed from a grid of up to six raised dots.","The Braille system was initially resisted by some educators.","Braille eventually gained widespread adoption among educators."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects Braille's own childhood vision loss to his motivation for developing the system, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cattle produce methane during digestion, a gas with a much stronger short-term warming effect than carbon dioxide.
• Some ranchers have begun adding seaweed supplements to cattle feed.
• Early trials show certain seaweed additives can cut cattle methane emissions by over 80%.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that diet changes can meaningfully reduce cattle's climate impact. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Early trials show that adding certain seaweed supplements to cattle feed can cut methane emissions from digestion by over 80%.","Cattle produce methane during digestion, a gas with a strong short-term warming effect.","Some ranchers have begun adding seaweed supplements to cattle feed.","Because cattle produce a strong-warming gas during digestion, some ranchers have begun adding seaweed to their feed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A cites the trial result—an 80% emissions cut—that actually supports the claim; C mentions the intervention without evidence it works, and B and D explain the problem or the attempted fix without the outcome data.$q$),
  ($q$ei-156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman aqueducts carried water across long distances using only gravity, with no pumps.
• Engineers maintained a gradual, precise downward slope over many kilometers to keep water flowing.
• Some aqueducts included underground sections to protect water quality and avoid obstacles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Roman aqueducts moved water without any mechanical pumps. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roman engineers moved water for kilometers without pumps by maintaining a gradual, precise downward slope that let gravity do the work.","Some Roman aqueducts included underground sections to protect water quality.","Roman aqueducts carried water across long distances.","Ancient Romans built many large public works projects."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the precise-slope technique that allowed gravity alone to move the water, directly answering how pumps were avoided.$q$),
  ($q$ei-157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cleopatra VII is believed to have been fluent in at least seven languages.
• She reportedly used these language skills to negotiate directly with foreign dignitaries without translators.
• She was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Cleopatra's linguistic ability gave her a diplomatic advantage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because she was fluent in at least seven languages, Cleopatra could negotiate directly with foreign dignitaries without relying on translators.","Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt.","Cleopatra VII is believed to have been fluent in at least seven languages.","Cleopatra reportedly negotiated with foreign dignitaries without using translators."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the specific skill (fluency in seven languages) to its specific diplomatic payoff (no need for translators); C and D each state only one half of that cause-and-effect link.$q$),
  ($q$ei-158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Town A installed community-owned wind turbines in 2016; local electricity costs fell by 18% by 2022.
• Town B installed turbines owned by an outside energy company in 2016; local electricity costs fell by only 4% over the same period.
• Both towns have similar populations and wind conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that community ownership, rather than wind conditions, explains Town A's larger drop in electricity costs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite having similar populations and wind conditions, Town A's community-owned turbines cut electricity costs by 18% by 2022, far more than the 4% drop in Town B, where an outside company owned the turbines.","Town A and Town B both installed wind turbines in 2016.","Town B's turbines were owned by an outside energy company.","Town A and Town B have similar wind conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-conditions detail with both towns' cost changes to suggest ownership structure, not wind conditions, drove the gap.$q$),
  ($q$ei-159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Region A invested heavily in public fast-charging stations starting in 2019; EV adoption reached 22% of new car sales by 2023.
• Region B made no comparable investment; EV adoption reached only 7% of new car sales over the same period.
• Both regions offered similar purchase tax credits for EVs.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that charging infrastructure, rather than tax incentives, drove the difference in EV adoption. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although both regions offered similar EV tax credits, Region A's heavy investment in fast-charging stations coincided with EV adoption reaching 22% of new car sales by 2023, compared to just 7% in Region B.","Region A began investing in public fast-charging stations in 2019.","Both regions offered similar purchase tax credits for electric vehicles.","Electric vehicle sales have grown in many parts of the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the matching tax-credit detail with both regions' adoption rates to suggest infrastructure, not incentives, explains the gap.$q$),
  ($q$ei-160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Colony collapse disorder refers to the sudden, unexplained disappearance of most worker bees from a hive.
• Affected colonies are often found with a live queen and plenty of food but almost no worker bees.
• Researchers suspect a combination of pesticides, parasites, and habitat loss may contribute to the disorder.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific pattern that defines colony collapse disorder. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Colony collapse disorder is marked by hives that still have a live queen and ample food but have lost nearly all of their worker bees.","Researchers suspect pesticides, parasites, and habitat loss may all contribute to colony collapse disorder.","Many pollinator species face population declines around the world.","Honeybee colonies play an important role in pollinating crops."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific defining pattern — a surviving queen and food supply alongside missing workers — rather than possible causes or unrelated facts.$q$),
  ($q$ei-161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city installed brighter LED streetlights along the riverfront path. ______ reports of nighttime bicycle accidents there fell sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in accidents is a direct consequence of the brighter lighting, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small hardware store has no website or online ordering system. ______ it continues to outsell every big-box competitor in the county.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Outselling larger competitors despite lacking an online presence is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers expected the new battery chemistry to double the phone's battery life. ______ independent testing found almost no improvement over the previous model.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The test results contradict the engineers' expectation, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's ticket prices rose sharply ahead of the gala season. ______ subscription renewals fell to their lowest level in a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in renewals was a direct result of the price increase, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Several small businesses in the district have struggled since the highway bypass opened. ______ the century-old hardware store closed permanently last spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hardware store's closure is offered as a specific case of the broader struggle, so 'For example' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The negotiations between the two unions dragged on for fourteen months without a breakthrough. ______ both sides reached a contract that satisfied neither side completely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Subsequently,","Gradually,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contract capped fourteen months of stalled talks, so the transition must signal a long-awaited final outcome—'Eventually.' 'Subsequently' marks mere sequence without that sense of a drawn-out resolution, and 'Gradually' describes a slow process itself rather than its eventual result, so 'Eventually' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Critics predicted the remake would tarnish the beloved original film's legacy. ______ it was praised by longtime fans as a faithful and inventive tribute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Fan praise directly replaces critics' prediction of damage to the film's legacy, which 'Instead' captures precisely. 'Nonetheless' would imply the praise occurred despite some stated obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy snowfall collapsed the roof of the community ice rink last winter. ______ the rink has remained closed for repairs ever since.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The prolonged closure is a direct consequence of the roof collapse, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The pharmaceutical company's lead drug candidate showed promising results in early animal trials. ______ human trials revealed a serious side effect that halted development.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The halted development directly contradicts the earlier promising results, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The remote village has no paved roads connecting it to the nearest city. ______ it has become an unlikely hub for a thriving online handicraft business.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A thriving online business despite total road isolation is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nevertheless' signals. 'Admittedly' would need to introduce the conceded limitation itself rather than follow it, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the isolation was replaced rather than simply not preventing success, so 'Nevertheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Streaming services have changed how many musicians earn income. ______ some independent artists now rely primarily on royalties from online plays rather than album sales.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific case illustrating the broader change described first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The airline switched to lighter aircraft seats to cut fuel costs. ______ it was able to reduce ticket prices on several popular routes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lower ticket prices are a direct consequence of the fuel savings, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The mayor's office claimed the new parking garage would be completed on budget. ______ city records later showed the project had run $12 million over its original estimate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Instead,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$City records don't just contrast with the mayor's office's claim—they expose the concealed truth behind it, which the revealing transition 'In fact' captures. Plain 'However' would mark contrast without that exposing force, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The university extended library hours during final exam week. ______ student visits to the library nearly tripled compared to a typical week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The surge in visits is a direct consequence of the extended hours, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The weather service forecast clear skies for the entire week of the air show. ______ a sudden storm grounded every performance on opening day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The storm grounding the show contradicts the forecast of clear skies, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small nonprofit operates with only two full-time staff members. ______ it has distributed meals to over 50,000 families since its founding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Serving so many families with such a small staff is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Sales of the board game had declined every year since its release in the 1990s. ______ a viral video review sent sales soaring to an all-time high last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Predictably,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A sudden, record-breaking reversal after decades of decline is a genuinely surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' captures. 'Nonetheless' implies the rise happened despite the decline continuing rather than reversing it, and 'Predictably' asserts the opposite of what happened, so 'Unexpectedly' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The factory switched to biodegradable packaging for all its products. ______ shipping costs rose slightly due to the new material's extra weight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in shipping costs is a direct consequence of switching materials, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup rejected dozens of acquisition offers over five years, insisting on remaining independent. ______ it accepted a buyout from a much larger competitor last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The buyout describes the eventual, final outcome after years of refusals, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The island's economy depends heavily on a handful of industries. ______ tourism alone accounts for nearly half of all local jobs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tourism statistic is a specific example supporting the general claim made first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The panel of judges unanimously predicted the underdog team would be eliminated in the first round. ______ the team advanced all the way to the championship match.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The team's championship run replaces the judges' prediction of early elimination with the opposite outcome, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would imply the advance happened despite a stated obstacle rather than contradicting a prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The hospital introduced a new scheduling system to reduce patient wait times. ______ average wait times fell by nearly 40 percent within three months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in wait times is a direct consequence of the new scheduling system, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The violin had been stored in an attic for over sixty years and showed signs of neglect. ______ once restored, it produced a tone that rivaled instruments costing ten times as much.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Producing such a fine tone despite decades of neglect is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's product recall cost tens of millions of dollars and drew widespread media criticism. ______ customer loyalty surveys taken a year later showed little lasting damage to the brand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The brand suffering little lasting damage despite the costly, criticized recall is a concession, which 'Nonetheless' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Geologists long assumed the canyon had formed gradually over millions of years of erosion. ______ new sediment analysis suggests a single catastrophic flood carved most of it in a matter of weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New sediment analysis directly overturns geologists' long-standing assumption, a straightforward contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' fits when a stated plan or expectation is replaced by what actually happened, but here geologists hold a belief about the past that new evidence revises, and 'Nonetheless' implies the gradual-erosion view held true despite some obstacle, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city planted thousands of trees along its main boulevards over the past decade. ______ summer temperatures downtown have measurably dropped compared to nearby areas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in temperatures is a direct consequence of the tree planting, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Several countries have begun taxing single-use plastics to curb waste. ______ one European nation reduced plastic bag use by 90 percent within two years of introducing such a tax.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nation's experience is offered as a specific case of the broader trend described first, so 'For example' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The app's founders launched it expecting a niche audience of hobbyist gardeners. ______ it was downloaded more than ten million times within its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","However,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The founders' plan for a niche audience is replaced by the opposite reality of mass downloads, which calls for 'Instead' rather than the belief-correcting 'However' or the obstacle-despite framing of 'Nonetheless.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new noise-canceling headphones use a stronger microphone array than earlier models. ______ they block significantly more ambient sound than their predecessor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Blocking more sound is a direct consequence of the stronger microphone array, so 'As a result' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The contract required the construction crew to finish the renovation within sixty days. ______ unexpected structural damage extended the project to nearly five months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy extension contradicts the sixty-day contractual deadline, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small-town diner has kept the same menu for forty years without a single addition. ______ it remains one of the most popular restaurants in the entire region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Remaining popular despite never updating the menu is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876.
• His assistant, Thomas Watson, heard the first intelligible words transmitted over the device from another room.
• Bell was also deeply involved in deaf education throughout his life.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the first successful demonstration of Bell's telephone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1876, Bell's assistant Thomas Watson heard the first intelligible words ever transmitted over Bell's telephone, from another room.","Alexander Graham Bell was deeply involved in deaf education throughout his life.","Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876.","Communication technology changed rapidly in the late nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific first successful demonstration, Watson hearing words from another room, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Frederick Banting and Charles Best isolated insulin from dog pancreases in 1921.
• Before this discovery, a diagnosis of diabetes was often fatal within a few years.
• The first human patient treated with insulin showed rapid improvement within hours.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the significance of the 1921 discovery for diabetes patients. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before Banting and Best isolated insulin in 1921, a diabetes diagnosis was often fatal within a few years, but the first patient treated with it improved within hours.","Frederick Banting and Charles Best isolated insulin from dog pancreases.","Diabetes remains a serious chronic illness today.","Medical research in the early twentieth century led to many new treatments."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A contrasts the earlier fatal prognosis with the rapid improvement insulin produced, directly explaining the discovery's significance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937 after four years of construction.
• It was the longest suspension bridge span in the world at the time.
• Its distinctive orange color was chosen partly for visibility in San Francisco's fog.$q$, $q$The student wants to state why the bridge's color was chosen. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Golden Gate Bridge's orange color was chosen in part to make it more visible in San Francisco's frequent fog.","The Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937 after four years of construction.","The Golden Gate Bridge was the longest suspension bridge span in the world when it opened.","San Francisco is known for its foggy weather."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific reason behind the bridge's color, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Severe drought combined with poor farming practices stripped topsoil across the Great Plains in the 1930s.
• Massive dust storms, called "black blizzards," sometimes blocked out the sun for hours.
• Hundreds of thousands of families migrated west, many to California, seeking work.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the severity of the 1930s dust storms with a specific detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["So severe were the 1930s dust storms that these \"black blizzards\" could block out the sun for hours at a time.","Hundreds of thousands of families migrated west during the Dust Bowl.","Poor farming practices contributed to the stripping of topsoil in the 1930s.","The Great Plains experienced a severe drought during the 1930s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific, vivid detail about the sun being blocked out, directly illustrating the storms' severity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Manhattan Project employed over 130,000 people at its peak.
• Many workers did not know the ultimate purpose of the facilities where they worked.
• The project's total cost exceeded $2 billion, an enormous sum at the time.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how compartmentalized and secretive the project's workforce was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite employing more than 130,000 people, the Manhattan Project kept many workers unaware of the ultimate purpose of their own facilities.","The Manhattan Project's total cost exceeded $2 billion.","The Manhattan Project employed more than 130,000 people at its peak.","Many workers did not know the ultimate purpose of the facilities where they worked."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the massive workforce with workers' ignorance of the project's purpose to show how compartmentalized it was; C and D each give only one half of that picture, and B is unrelated to secrecy.$q$),
  ($q$ei-197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Whitcomb Judson patented an early slide fastener in 1893.
• It frequently jammed and was not commercially successful.
• Gideon Sundback redesigned the device in 1913, creating the modern, more reliable zipper.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Judson's original design failed to catch on. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Judson's original 1893 slide fastener frequently jammed, which kept it from becoming commercially successful.","Gideon Sundback redesigned the slide fastener in 1913.","Whitcomb Judson patented an early slide fastener in 1893.","Zippers are now used in countless everyday products."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific reason, frequent jamming, that explains why the original design did not catch on.$q$),
  ($q$ei-198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Peregrine falcons can reach speeds over 240 miles per hour during a diving attack.
• This makes them the fastest animals on Earth in terms of top speed.
• They fold their wings into a streamlined shape to achieve these speeds.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the falcon's status as the fastest animal on Earth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Diving at speeds over 240 miles per hour, the peregrine falcon is the fastest animal on Earth.","Peregrine falcons fold their wings into a streamlined shape while diving.","Peregrine falcons are skilled hunters found on every continent except Antarctica.","Many birds of prey rely on speed to catch their prey."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific speed figure and the falcon's status as fastest animal, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Electric eels can generate electric discharges of up to 600 volts.
• They use these shocks both to stun prey and to defend themselves.
• Electric eels are not true eels but are more closely related to catfish.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how powerful the electric eel's discharge is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Electric eels can produce electric discharges of up to 600 volts.","Electric eels use electric shocks to stun prey and to defend themselves.","Electric eels are more closely related to catfish than to true eels.","Many aquatic animals have evolved unusual defense mechanisms."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific voltage figure, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the discharge's power.$q$),
  ($q$ei-200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Chameleons change color primarily through specialized cells with pigment and light-reflecting crystals.
• Rather than mainly for camouflage, color change is largely used to signal mood or communicate with other chameleons.
• Temperature also influences a chameleon's coloration.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that chameleons change color mainly to hide from predators. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often assumed to change color mainly to hide from predators, chameleons primarily use color shifts to signal mood and communicate with other chameleons.","Chameleons change color using specialized cells containing pigment and light-reflecting crystals.","Temperature can influence a chameleon's coloration.","Many reptiles have evolved ways to blend into their surroundings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the common misconception about camouflage, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A blue whale's heart can weigh as much as 400 pounds.
• Its heartbeat can be detected from over a mile away using specialized equipment.
• Blue whales are the largest animals known to have ever lived.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to illustrate the enormous size of a blue whale's heart. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A blue whale's heart can weigh as much as 400 pounds, large enough that its heartbeat can be detected from over a mile away.","Blue whales are the largest animals known to have ever lived.","A blue whale's heartbeat can be detected using specialized equipment.","A blue whale's heart can weigh as much as 400 pounds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the heart's weight with the mile-away detectable heartbeat to fully illustrate its enormous size; C and D each give only one of those two supporting details, and B describes the whale's overall size, not its heart.$q$),
  ($q$ei-202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Chicago Fire of 1871 destroyed roughly 3.3 square miles of the city.
• It killed an estimated 300 people and left about 100,000 residents homeless.
• The fire led to major reforms in building codes and firefighting equipment.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the scale of destruction caused by the fire. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Chicago Fire of 1871 destroyed about 3.3 square miles of the city and left roughly 100,000 residents homeless.","The Great Chicago Fire led to major reforms in building codes and firefighting equipment.","The Great Chicago Fire occurred in 1871.","The fire killed an estimated 300 people."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the area destroyed with the number left homeless to state the fire's full scale; D gives only the death toll, and B and C describe a later consequence or the date rather than the scale of destruction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Amelia Earhart was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean, in 1932.
• In 1937, she disappeared over the Pacific Ocean while attempting to circumnavigate the globe.
• No confirmed wreckage of her plane has ever been found.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the unresolved mystery surrounding Earhart's disappearance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite extensive searches, no confirmed wreckage of Amelia Earhart's plane has ever been found since she vanished over the Pacific in 1937.","Amelia Earhart was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean.","Amelia Earhart was attempting to circumnavigate the globe in 1937.","Aviation in the early twentieth century involved considerable risk."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly addresses the unresolved mystery, the unfound wreckage, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jacques Cousteau co-developed the Aqua-Lung breathing device in 1943.
• It allowed divers to breathe underwater for extended periods without being tethered to the surface.
• Cousteau later used underwater film to bring ocean exploration to a wide public audience.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what made the Aqua-Lung an improvement over earlier diving equipment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cousteau's 1943 Aqua-Lung let divers breathe underwater for long stretches without being tethered to the surface.","Jacques Cousteau used underwater film to bring ocean exploration to a wide audience.","Jacques Cousteau co-developed the Aqua-Lung in 1943.","Diving equipment has continued to improve since the mid-twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific improvement, untethered breathing, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Olmec and Maya civilizations were among the first to cultivate cacao, over 3,000 years ago.
• Early preparations of cacao were usually bitter, unsweetened drinks, often mixed with chili.
• Sugar was not commonly added to chocolate until Europeans began producing it in the 16th and 17th centuries.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that chocolate has always been a sweet treat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although chocolate is associated with sweetness today, early cacao drinks prepared by the Olmec and Maya were typically bitter and unsweetened, often spiced with chili.","The Olmec and Maya civilizations cultivated cacao over 3,000 years ago.","Europeans began adding sugar to chocolate in the 16th and 17th centuries.","Chocolate is made from the cacao plant, which grows in tropical regions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception, contrasting chocolate's sweet reputation with its bitter origins.$q$),
  ($q$ei-206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Giant squid have the largest eyes of any known animal, up to 10 inches across.
• They were not filmed alive in their natural habitat until 2012.
• Scientists believe their large eyes help them detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why giant squid likely evolved such large eyes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Scientists believe giant squid evolved eyes up to 10 inches across, the largest of any animal, to detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean.","Giant squid were not filmed alive in their natural habitat until 2012.","Giant squid have the largest eyes of any known animal, up to 10 inches across.","Scientists believe large eyes help giant squid detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the eyes' record size to their specific function to explain why they evolved that way; C and D each state only the size or only the function, and B is unrelated to the question of why the eyes evolved.$q$),
  ($q$ei-207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Pistol shrimp snap a specialized claw shut fast enough to create a popping bubble.
• The collapsing bubble produces a shockwave that can stun or kill small prey.
• The sound produced can briefly reach louder levels than a gunshot.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how loud the pistol shrimp's claw snap can be. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A pistol shrimp's claw snap can briefly produce a sound louder than a gunshot.","A pistol shrimp's claw snap creates a shockwave that can stun or kill small prey.","Pistol shrimp use a specialized claw to capture their prey.","Many marine animals rely on sound to communicate or hunt."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific comparison to a gunshot, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the snap's loudness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Pierre de Coubertin helped revive the Olympic Games in 1896 after more than 1,500 years of dormancy.
• The first modern Games featured only 14 nations and roughly 241 athletes.
• Women were not permitted to compete until the 1900 Games in Paris.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how small the first modern Olympics were compared to today. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The first modern Olympics in 1896 drew only about 241 athletes from 14 nations, a tiny fraction of the thousands who compete today.","Pierre de Coubertin helped revive the Olympic Games in 1896.","Women were not permitted to compete in the Olympics until 1900.","The Olympic Games have a long and storied history."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific small numbers of athletes and nations, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the first Games' modest scale.$q$),
  ($q$ei-209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• László Bíró patented a reliable ballpoint pen design in 1938.
• Earlier ballpoint attempts often leaked or clogged due to inconsistent ink flow.
• Bíró's design used a rotating ball to regulate ink flow evenly.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what made Bíró's design more reliable than earlier attempts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Bíró's 1938 design used a rotating ball to regulate ink flow evenly, solving the leaking and clogging that plagued earlier ballpoint pens.","László Bíró patented his ballpoint pen design in 1938.","Earlier ballpoint pen attempts often leaked or clogged.","Writing instruments have changed considerably over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the specific mechanism, the rotating ball regulating ink flow, that solved earlier pens' problems.$q$),
  ($q$ei-210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Black Death killed an estimated one-third of Europe's population between 1347 and 1351.
• The resulting labor shortage gave surviving peasants more bargaining power over wages.
• Some historians argue this shift contributed to the eventual decline of the feudal system.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the plague's death toll led to changes in peasants' economic position. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Black Death wiped out roughly a third of Europe's population, the resulting labor shortage gave surviving peasants new bargaining power over their wages.","The Black Death killed an estimated one-third of Europe's population.","Some historians link the Black Death to the eventual decline of feudalism.","The Black Death spread across Europe between 1347 and 1351."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the population loss to the labor shortage and the resulting wage leverage, directly explaining the economic shift.$q$),
  ($q$ei-211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Joseph Nicéphore Niépce created the earliest surviving photograph in 1826 or 1827.
• The exposure process took approximately eight hours to complete.
• The image shows a view from his window and remains preserved today.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how long the exposure took for the earliest surviving photograph. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Niépce's earliest surviving photograph, taken in 1826 or 1827, required an exposure time of roughly eight hours.","Niépce's earliest surviving photograph shows a view from his window.","Niépce's photograph remains preserved today.","Photography has advanced considerably since the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific eight-hour exposure time, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Scientists first identified a severe seasonal thinning of ozone over Antarctica in 1985.
• The Montreal Protocol, signed in 1987, phased out many ozone-depleting chemicals worldwide.
• By the 2020s, satellite data showed signs that the ozone layer had begun slowly recovering.$q$, $q$The student wants to use specific evidence to support the claim that international action helped repair the ozone layer. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["After the 1987 Montreal Protocol phased out ozone-depleting chemicals, satellite data by the 2020s showed the ozone layer slowly recovering.","Scientists first identified severe seasonal ozone thinning over Antarctica in 1985.","The Montreal Protocol, signed in 1987, phased out many ozone-depleting chemicals worldwide.","By the 2020s, satellite data showed signs that the ozone layer had begun slowly recovering."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the treaty's chemical phase-out to the later recovery evidence, showing the action actually helped; C and D each give only the action or only the result, not the causal connection the claim requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Percy Spencer discovered the heating effect of microwaves in 1945 after noticing a chocolate bar melted in his pocket near radar equipment.
• The first commercial microwave ovens, sold in the early 1950s, were nearly six feet tall.
• Home versions did not become affordable and common until the 1970s.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the accidental observation that led to the microwave oven's invention. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Spencer invented the microwave oven after noticing, by chance, that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted near radar equipment in 1945.","The first commercial microwave ovens, sold in the early 1950s, were nearly six feet tall.","Home microwave ovens did not become common until the 1970s.","Kitchen appliances have become more compact and affordable over time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific accidental observation, the melted chocolate bar, that led to Spencer's discovery.$q$),
  ($q$ei-214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Despite its name, the Panama hat is traditionally woven in Ecuador, not Panama.
• The hats gained the misleading name after being shipped through the Panama Canal and worn by canal workers.
• Weaving a fine Panama hat can take skilled artisans several months.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Panama hats originate from Panama. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite its name, the Panama hat is traditionally woven in Ecuador, having gained its misleading name after being shipped through the Panama Canal.","Weaving a fine Panama hat can take skilled artisans several months.","The Panama Canal connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.","Many traditional crafts require months of skilled labor to complete."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception about the hat's origin, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895 while experimenting with cathode ray tubes.
• One of his first X-ray images was of his wife's hand, showing her bones and wedding ring.
• Within months, X-ray machines were being used in hospitals to examine broken bones.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to illustrate how quickly X-rays were put to practical medical use. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Just months after Röntgen's 1895 discovery, hospitals were already using X-ray machines to examine patients' broken bones.","Röntgen discovered X-rays while experimenting with cathode ray tubes.","One of Röntgen's first X-ray images showed his wife's hand and wedding ring.","Medical imaging technology has continued to advance since the 1890s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific timeframe, within months, showing how quickly X-rays reached practical hospital use.$q$),
  ($q$ei-216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Factory A automated its assembly line in 2015; worker injury rates fell by 45% by 2021.
• Factory B delayed automation until 2020; worker injury rates fell by only 10% over the same period.
• Both factories produce similar goods and employ similarly sized workforces.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that earlier automation, rather than workforce differences, explains Factory A's greater drop in injuries. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Factory A and Factory B employ similarly sized workforces, Factory A's earlier automation was followed by a 45% drop in injuries by 2021, compared to only a 10% drop at Factory B.","Factory A automated its assembly line in 2015.","Factory B delayed automating its assembly line until 2020.","Factory A and Factory B produce similar goods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-workforce detail with both factories' injury-rate changes to suggest automation timing, not workforce size, drove the gap.$q$),
  ($q$ei-217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Leaning Tower of Pisa began in 1173 and took nearly 200 years to complete.
• The tower began tilting even before construction was finished due to soft, unstable soil.
• Engineers completed a major stabilization project in the 1990s and 2000s that reduced the tilt.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the tower began leaning in the first place. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Leaning Tower of Pisa began tilting during construction because it was built on soft, unstable soil.","Construction of the Leaning Tower of Pisa took nearly 200 years to complete.","Engineers stabilized the Leaning Tower of Pisa in the 1990s and 2000s.","The Leaning Tower of Pisa is a popular tourist attraction today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific cause, unstable soil, that explains why the tower began leaning during construction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Hindenburg was a hydrogen-filled airship that caught fire while landing in New Jersey in 1937.
• Of the 97 people aboard, 35 died, along with one worker on the ground.
• The disaster effectively ended the era of passenger airship travel.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the lasting effect the Hindenburg disaster had on airship travel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1937 Hindenburg disaster effectively brought the era of passenger airship travel to an end.","The Hindenburg was a hydrogen-filled airship that caught fire in New Jersey in 1937.","Thirty-five of the 97 people aboard the Hindenburg died in the disaster.","Air travel has changed considerably since the early twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the disaster's lasting consequence, the end of passenger airships, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Hiram Bingham was guided to Machu Picchu by local residents in 1911.
• The site had never been completely unknown to people living in the region.
• Bingham's accounts introduced the site to a wide international audience.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Bingham discovered a site unknown to anyone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Bingham's 1911 visit introduced Machu Picchu to an international audience, local residents who guided him there already knew of the site.","Hiram Bingham visited Machu Picchu in 1911.","Machu Picchu later became known to a wide international audience.","Many ancient sites in South America attract researchers and tourists."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception, noting that local residents already knew of the site before Bingham's visit.$q$),
  ($q$ei-220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jonas Salk developed an injectable polio vaccine, announced successful in 1955.
• Salk refused to patent the vaccine, saying it belonged to the public.
• Mass vaccination campaigns sharply reduced polio cases in the United States within a few years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Salk's decision not to profit personally from his discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Jonas Salk refused to patent his 1955 polio vaccine, insisting that it belonged to the public rather than to him.","Jonas Salk's polio vaccine was announced successful in 1955.","Mass vaccination campaigns sharply reduced polio cases in the United States.","Vaccines have eliminated or greatly reduced many once-common diseases."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly states Salk's refusal to patent the vaccine, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing his decision not to profit from it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• District A extended public library hours to include weekends starting in 2018; childhood literacy test scores rose 12 points by 2023.
• District B kept traditional weekday-only hours; literacy scores rose just 3 points over the same period.
• Both districts have comparable school funding and student populations.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to argue that expanded library access, not funding differences, likely drove District A's larger literacy gains. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite comparable funding and student populations, District A's literacy scores rose 12 points after extending library hours to weekends, compared to just a 3-point rise in District B, which kept traditional hours.","District A extended its public library hours to include weekends starting in 2018.","District B kept its traditional weekday-only library hours.","District A and District B have comparable school funding."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the comparable-funding detail with both districts' score changes to suggest library access, not funding, drove the gap.$q$),
  ($q$ei-222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The sandwich is popularly named after John Montagu, the 4th Earl of Sandwich.
• According to legend, he requested meat between bread slices so he could keep eating while gambling.
• Historians note that similar bread-and-filling meals likely existed in various cultures long before Montagu.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Montagu invented the first-ever sandwich. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the sandwich is popularly named for the Earl of Sandwich, historians note that similar bread-and-filling meals likely existed in other cultures long before him.","John Montagu was the 4th Earl of Sandwich.","According to legend, Montagu requested meat between bread slices while gambling.","Sandwiches are a popular food eaten in many cultures today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception, noting that similar meals predated Montagu, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The QWERTY layout was designed in the 1870s for early mechanical typewriters.
• One common explanation is that it helped reduce jamming by separating frequently paired letters.
• Despite newer layouts claiming greater efficiency, QWERTY remains the dominant standard today.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain one theory behind why the QWERTY layout was originally designed the way it was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["One common explanation for QWERTY's original design is that separating frequently paired letters helped reduce jamming in early mechanical typewriters.","QWERTY remains the dominant keyboard standard despite newer, competing layouts.","The QWERTY layout was designed in the 1870s.","Typewriters were eventually replaced by computer keyboards."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific jam-reducing rationale behind QWERTY's original design, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small vineyard suffered its worst grape harvest in twenty years after an unexpected spring frost. ______ the wine produced from that very harvest went on to win a national award.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Consequently,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A ruined harvest producing an award-winning wine is an unexpected contrast, which 'Yet' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Edward Jenner tested his smallpox vaccine in 1796 using material from a cowpox sore.
• He inoculated a young boy, who then proved immune to smallpox exposure.
• His method is considered the foundation of modern vaccination.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the biological source Jenner used to create his smallpox vaccine. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1796, Edward Jenner created his smallpox vaccine using material taken from a cowpox sore.","Edward Jenner inoculated a young boy to test his vaccine.","Jenner's method is considered the foundation of modern vaccination.","Smallpox was once a widespread and deadly disease."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific source, a cowpox sore, that the prompt asks for, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city's new bus rapid transit line cost nearly twice its original budget to build. ______ average commute times across the city dropped by almost 20 minutes once it opened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Specifically,","In other words,","As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite the costly overrun, the line still delivered a real benefit, a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Hoover Dam was completed in 1936 on the Colorado River.
• At the time, it was the largest concrete structure ever built.
• It took about five years and employed thousands of workers during the Great Depression.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that building the dam provided jobs during a period of economic hardship. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Built during the Great Depression, Hoover Dam provided jobs for thousands of workers at a time when employment was scarce.","Hoover Dam was completed in 1936 on the Colorado River.","At the time of its completion, Hoover Dam was the largest concrete structure ever built.","The dam took approximately five years to construct."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the dam's construction to the economic hardship of the Great Depression, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The ceramics studio's kiln malfunctioned just two days before the holiday craft fair. ______ the owner spent three sleepless nights firing pieces in a borrowed kiln across town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scrambling to use a borrowed kiln is a direct consequence of the malfunction, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Pluto was discovered by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930 and classified as the ninth planet.
• In 2006, the International Astronomical Union reclassified it as a "dwarf planet."
• The reclassification was based on a new, stricter definition of what counts as a planet.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception that Pluto is still officially classified as a planet. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although many people still think of Pluto as the ninth planet, astronomers reclassified it as a dwarf planet in 2006.","Pluto was discovered by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930.","The International Astronomical Union adopted a stricter definition of what counts as a planet.","Pluto orbits the sun from the outer edge of the solar system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception that Pluto remains officially classified as a planet.$q$),
  ($q$ei-230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The university's physics department predicted its new telescope would detect only faint, distant galaxies. ______ the telescope's very first image captured a previously unknown comet much closer to home.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Likewise,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Capturing a nearby comet rather than distant galaxies contradicts the prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Higgs boson was first proposed theoretically in 1964.
• It took nearly 50 years before scientists at CERN confirmed its existence experimentally.
• The discovery was announced in 2012 using data from the Large Hadron Collider.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the long gap between the theoretical prediction and its experimental confirmation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Nearly 50 years passed between the Higgs boson's theoretical proposal in 1964 and its experimental confirmation at CERN in 2012.","The Higgs boson was first proposed theoretically in 1964.","CERN scientists used data from the Large Hadron Collider to confirm the discovery.","The Large Hadron Collider is located near Geneva, Switzerland."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly states the nearly 50-year gap between prediction and confirmation, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The ski resort invested heavily in snowmaking machines after several winters of disappointingly low snowfall. ______ natural snowfall the following winter broke a decade-old record.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ironically,","Unexpectedly,","Remarkably,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The costly snowmaking investment being followed by a record natural snowfall is a self-undermining irony, not just a surprising fact, so 'Ironically' fits best. 'Unexpectedly' and 'Remarkably' both note surprise or scale but miss the specific paradox of the wasted preparation, so 'Ironically' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• James Watson and Francis Crick published the double-helix model of DNA in 1953.
• Their model relied heavily on X-ray diffraction images produced by Rosalind Franklin.
• Franklin's contribution was not widely credited until years after the discovery.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Watson and Crick alone were responsible for discovering DNA's structure. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Watson and Crick are often credited alone, their 1953 double-helix model relied heavily on Rosalind Franklin's X-ray diffraction images.","James Watson and Francis Crick published their model of DNA's structure in 1953.","Watson and Crick's model relied heavily on X-ray diffraction images.","Rosalind Franklin's contribution went largely uncredited for years after the discovery."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly names Franklin and ties her X-ray images to the model's creation, directly correcting the misconception; C omits her name and D omits her specific contribution, so neither fully corrects it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The puppet theater's lead performer quit just two days before opening night. ______ an understudy who had never performed the role stepped in and delivered a flawless show.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A flawless show despite the sudden loss of the lead performer is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Stephanie Kwolek invented Kevlar in 1965 while researching lightweight materials for tires.
• Kevlar is five times stronger than steel by weight.
• It is now used in bulletproof vests, helmets, and cables.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much stronger Kevlar is relative to its weight. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kevlar, invented by Stephanie Kwolek in 1965, is five times stronger than steel by weight.","Kevlar was originally developed while researching lightweight materials for tires.","Kevlar is now used in products such as bulletproof vests and helmets.","Stephanie Kwolek worked as a chemist for several decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific strength-to-weight comparison that fulfills the goal of emphasizing Kevlar's strength.$q$),
  ($q$ei-236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Regulators cut the region's cod fishing quotas in half to protect the declining population. ______ cod numbers continued to fall even after the stricter limits took effect.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","However,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cod numbers continuing to fall despite stricter quotas is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'However' would fit correcting a mistaken claim rather than describing an action that failed to reverse a trend, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the decline replaced some other expected outcome, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The 1883 eruption of Krakatoa was heard roughly 3,000 miles away.
• The eruption triggered tsunamis that killed tens of thousands of people.
• Volcanic ash from the eruption affected global temperatures for several years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far the sound of the eruption traveled. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1883 eruption of Krakatoa was so loud that it was heard roughly 3,000 miles away.","Tsunamis triggered by the eruption killed tens of thousands of people.","Volcanic ash from Krakatoa affected global temperatures for years.","Krakatoa is a volcanic island located in present-day Indonesia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific distance the sound traveled, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its reach.$q$),
  ($q$ei-238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small studio spent six years developing its debut video game, promoting it as a future genre classic. ______ it sold fewer than a thousand copies in its first month on sale.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The weak sales directly contradict the studio's own marketing claim, a straightforward correction best marked by 'However.' 'Nonetheless' would imply poor sales occurred despite some stated obstacle, and 'Instead' suggests a plan was replaced outright rather than a claim being disproven by the outcome, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Thomas Newcomen built early steam engines in the early 1700s for pumping water out of mines.
• James Watt improved the design in 1776, making it far more fuel-efficient.
• Watt's improvements helped power the factories of the Industrial Revolution.$q$, $q$The student wants to clarify that Watt improved an existing design rather than inventing the steam engine from scratch. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Watt is often credited with inventing the steam engine, he actually improved a design that Thomas Newcomen had built decades earlier.","James Watt's 1776 improvements made the steam engine far more fuel-efficient.","Watt's improved engine helped power factories during the Industrial Revolution.","Thomas Newcomen's engines were primarily used for pumping water out of mines."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly corrects the misconception by naming Newcomen's earlier design and Watt's later improvement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A landslide damaged the national park's most popular summit trail, closing it indefinitely. ______ visitors can still reach the summit by a longer, less direct route.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The alternate route is a welcome piece of good news following the closure, which 'Fortunately' captures.$q$),
  ($q$ei-241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891.
• The completed line stretches more than 9,000 kilometers, linking Moscow to Russia's Pacific coast.
• It remains the longest single railway line in the world.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the railway's exceptional length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Trans-Siberian Railway stretches more than 9,000 kilometers, making it the longest single railway line in the world.","Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891.","The railway links Moscow to Russia's Pacific coast.","Russia spans eleven time zones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific length figure that fulfills the goal of emphasizing the railway's exceptional scale.$q$),
  ($q$ei-242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The public art installation downtown was intended to be temporary, lasting only a single summer. ______ residents petitioned to keep it permanently, and the city agreed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The piece becoming permanent contradicts its original temporary plan, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elisha Otis demonstrated his safety elevator brake at a New York exposition in 1854.
• During the demonstration, he had the elevator's rope cut while he stood on the platform.
• The brake stopped the platform from falling, proving the device worked.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific demonstration Otis used to prove his invention worked. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Otis proved his safety brake worked by having the elevator's rope cut while he stood on the platform, a fall the brake successfully stopped.","Elisha Otis demonstrated his invention at a New York exposition in 1854.","Otis's invention was a brake designed for elevators.","Elevators became common in buildings after Otis's invention."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific rope-cutting demonstration, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The volunteer fire department relies on donations for nearly all of its equipment budget. ______ it maintains some of the newest trucks in the entire county.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having some of the county's newest trucks on donations alone is an impressively surprising fact, which 'Remarkably' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' would frame the donation-reliance as an obstacle merely overcome, understating how striking the outcome is, and 'Instead' implies a replacement rather than an achievement, so 'Remarkably' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount St. Helens erupted violently on May 18, 1980, in Washington State.
• The eruption flattened forests across more than 200 square miles.
• It was the deadliest and most economically destructive volcanic eruption in U.S. history.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extent of the damage the eruption caused to the surrounding landscape. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens flattened forests across more than 200 square miles.","Mount St. Helens erupted on May 18, 1980, in Washington State.","The eruption is considered the deadliest in U.S. history.","Mount St. Helens is part of the Cascade Range."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific scale of forest destruction, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the eruption's damage.$q$),
  ($q$ei-246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The craft brewery's seasonal pumpkin ale became unexpectedly popular on social media. ______ demand outpaced production, and the brewery sold out within a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Selling out is a direct consequence of the surge in demand, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The earliest magnetic compasses were developed in China, possibly as early as the 2nd century BCE.
• They were originally used for divination and geomancy rather than navigation.
• Compasses were not widely adapted for sea navigation until centuries later.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that compasses were invented for navigation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although compasses are associated with navigation, the earliest Chinese compasses were used for divination and geomancy.","Magnetic compasses were first developed in China around the 2nd century BCE.","Compasses were not widely used for sea navigation until centuries after their invention.","Divination practices were common in ancient Chinese culture."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception about the compass's original purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A small studio trained its chess-playing program using only amateur-level games. ______ the program went on to defeat several grandmasters in exhibition matches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Consequently,","For instance,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Defeating grandmasters after training only on amateur games is an unexpected outcome, which 'Surprisingly' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elias Howe patented a lockstitch sewing machine design in 1846.
• Isaac Singer later developed a more practical version and built a commercial company around it.
• The two inventors became rivals and were involved in a lengthy patent lawsuit.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Howe and Singer became rivals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because Singer's commercially successful machine drew on Howe's earlier lockstitch patent, the two became rivals in a lengthy lawsuit.","Elias Howe patented a lockstitch sewing machine design in 1846.","Isaac Singer later developed a more practical version and built a commercial company around it.","The two inventors were involved in a lengthy patent lawsuit."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects Singer's commercial success to Howe's earlier patent to explain the cause of their rivalry; D merely restates that they were rivals without explaining why, and B and C each describe one inventor without connecting them.$q$),
  ($q$ei-250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The farmers' market relocated to a much smaller lot after losing its original parking agreement. ______ vendor turnout and average weekly sales both increased at the new location.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Accordingly,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sales increasing despite the smaller, less convenient location is an unexpected result, which 'Surprisingly' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Galileo improved the design of the telescope in 1609.
• He used it to observe four moons orbiting Jupiter.
• This observation supported the idea that not everything in the universe orbits Earth.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Galileo's observation of Jupiter's moons was significant. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By observing four moons orbiting Jupiter, Galileo provided evidence that not everything in the universe orbits Earth.","Galileo improved the design of the telescope in 1609.","Galileo observed four moons orbiting the planet Jupiter.","Telescopes allow distant objects to be viewed more closely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the observation to its broader significance, fulfilling the goal of explaining its importance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The opera house's board voted to cancel the entire season after a severe budget shortfall. ______ an anonymous donor stepped forward with enough funding to save every scheduled performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","Specifically,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The donor's rescue directly contradicts the board's decision to cancel, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• For centuries, many scholars believed the city of Troy was purely legendary.
• In the 1870s, Heinrich Schliemann excavated a site in modern Turkey that matched descriptions from ancient texts.
• His excavation uncovered multiple layers of ancient settlement at the site.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Troy was purely legendary. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although long considered legendary, Troy was shown to be a real place when Schliemann excavated a matching site in the 1870s.","Heinrich Schliemann excavated a site in modern Turkey in the 1870s.","Schliemann's excavation revealed multiple layers of ancient settlement.","Ancient texts contain descriptions of the city of Troy."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception that Troy was purely legendary.$q$),
  ($q$ei-254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$An early prototype of the toy required four different batteries and constant recharging to operate. ______ the final retail version runs on a single battery for over a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The retail version's efficiency is the opposite of the power-hungry prototype, so a contrast transition, 'By contrast,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Natural rubber supplies were disrupted for the United States during World War II.
• Chemists rapidly developed synthetic rubber as a substitute for military and civilian use.
• Production of synthetic rubber scaled from almost nothing to hundreds of thousands of tons within a few years.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why synthetic rubber production expanded so quickly during the war. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because wartime disruptions cut off natural rubber supplies, chemists scaled synthetic rubber production to hundreds of thousands of tons within a few years.","Natural rubber supplies were disrupted during World War II.","Synthetic rubber was used for both military and civilian purposes.","Rubber is used in a wide range of manufactured products."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the wartime cause, a disrupted supply, to the specific effect, rapid scale-up, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers were thrilled to discover a new species of frog in a remote rainforest. ______ habitat loss in the same region threatens to drive the species to extinction before it can be fully studied.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The looming extinction threat undercuts the researchers' excitement, a direct contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the threat replaces the discovery itself, and 'Nonetheless' awkwardly implies the threat persists despite the thrill rather than undercutting it, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Erie Canal opened in 1825, connecting the Hudson River to Lake Erie.
• It reduced the cost of shipping goods between the Midwest and New York City by more than 90%.
• The canal helped establish New York City as a major commercial port.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much the canal reduced shipping costs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Erie Canal, which opened in 1825, cut the cost of shipping goods between the Midwest and New York City by more than 90%.","The Erie Canal connected the Hudson River to Lake Erie.","The canal helped establish New York City as a major commercial port.","Canals were an important form of transportation in the 19th century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific cost reduction figure, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its economic impact.$q$),
  ($q$ei-258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city expanded its bike lane network by thirty miles over two years. ______ the number of cycling accidents reported at major intersections dropped by nearly half.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in accidents is a direct consequence of the expanded bike lanes, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis Pasteur developed a rabies vaccine in 1885.
• He first tested it on a boy who had been bitten by a rabid dog.
• The boy survived, and the treatment became a landmark in preventive medicine.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the circumstances under which Pasteur's rabies vaccine was first tested on a person. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Pasteur's rabies vaccine was first tested in 1885 on a boy who had been bitten by a rabid dog, and the boy survived.","Louis Pasteur developed a rabies vaccine in 1885.","The treatment became a landmark in preventive medicine.","Rabies is a viral disease that affects the nervous system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific first human test, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Mechanical problems delayed the amusement park's newest roller coaster for over a year past its planned opening. ______ it broke the park's single-day attendance record within its first week of operation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","However,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting an attendance record despite a lengthy delay is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'However' fits correcting a mistaken claim, not describing success following an obstacle, and 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the record replaced the delay rather than following it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Levi Strauss began selling denim work pants to miners during the California Gold Rush.
• Jacob Davis, a tailor, added copper rivets to reinforce the pants' stress points.
• Strauss and Davis patented the riveted design together in 1873.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the innovation that made the riveted jeans design distinctive. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Jacob Davis's addition of copper rivets to reinforce stress points made the jeans he and Strauss patented in 1873 distinctive.","Levi Strauss began selling denim pants to miners during the Gold Rush.","Strauss and Davis patented their design together in 1873.","Denim work pants were popular among manual laborers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific innovation, copper rivets, that made the design distinctive, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The vineyard's hillside plot is too steep and rocky for any harvesting machine to operate safely. ______ every grape on that plot must be picked by hand each autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hand-picking is a direct consequence of the terrain being unsuitable for machinery, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Tasmanian tiger, or thylacine, was a carnivorous marsupial once widespread across Australia.
• Competition with dingoes and hunting by humans drove it to extinction on the mainland.
• The last known thylacine died in captivity in 1936.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the thylacine went extinct on the Australian mainland. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Competition with dingoes, combined with hunting by humans, drove the thylacine to extinction on the Australian mainland.","The thylacine was a carnivorous marsupial once widespread across Australia.","The last known thylacine died in captivity in 1936.","Marsupials are mammals that typically carry their young in a pouch."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A identifies the specific causes of the thylacine's mainland extinction, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A coral nursery program initially struggled to keep transplanted fragments alive in increasingly warm waters. ______ researchers have developed heat-resistant coral strains with much higher survival rates.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Since then,","Similarly,","For example,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The improved strains represent a later development following the initial struggles, a sequence correctly signaled by 'Since then.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Canada geese fly in a V-shaped formation during long migrations.
• Each bird benefits from reduced air resistance created by the bird ahead of it.
• Geese periodically rotate the lead position because it requires the most energy.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why geese rotate which bird leads the formation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Geese rotate the lead position during flight because leading the V-formation requires more energy than drafting behind another bird.","Canada geese fly in a V-shaped formation during long migrations.","Birds flying behind the leader benefit from reduced air resistance.","Canada geese migrate long distances each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the specific reason for the rotation, the higher energy cost of leading, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The debate coach required her students to argue positions they personally disagreed with during practice rounds. ______ several students later said the exercise sharpened their ability to anticipate counterarguments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Nonetheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The students' testimony doesn't just contrast with the exercise's discomfort—it confirms and reinforces its value, which the intensifying 'In fact' captures. Plain 'However' would only mark a contrast, and 'Nonetheless' frames the benefit as happening despite the exercise rather than growing out of it, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis-Sébastien Lenormand demonstrated an early parachute jump from a tower in 1783.
• He coined the word "parachute" from Greek and French roots meaning "against falling."
• His design predated practical aircraft by over a century.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the origin of the word "parachute." Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lenormand coined the term \"parachute\" by combining Greek and French roots meaning \"against falling.\"","Louis-Sébastien Lenormand demonstrated a parachute jump from a tower in 1783.","Lenormand's design predated practical aircraft by over a century.","Lenormand coined a new word to describe his invention."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific Greek and French roots and their meaning, directly explaining the word's origin; D mentions that he coined a word without saying what it meant, and B and C are real but irrelevant to etymology.$q$),
  ($q$ei-268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A city zoo's panda breeding program had failed for over a decade despite consulting international experts. ______ last spring, a female panda gave birth to twins conceived through artificial insemination.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Finally,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The twins' birth is the long-awaited culmination of a decade of failed attempts, an eventual outcome that 'Finally' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Angkor Wat was built in the 12th century by the Khmer Empire in present-day Cambodia.
• It was originally constructed as a Hindu temple before later becoming a Buddhist site.
• It remains one of the largest religious monuments in the world by land area.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the temple's exceptional size. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Angkor Wat remains one of the largest religious monuments in the world by land area.","Angkor Wat was built in the 12th century by the Khmer Empire.","The temple was originally built for Hindu worship before becoming a Buddhist site.","Angkor Wat is located in present-day Cambodia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly states the temple's exceptional scale, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The tech conference's keynote speaker canceled just hours before the event was set to begin. ______ organizers scrambled to find a replacement able to address the same topic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The scramble for a replacement is a direct consequence of the cancellation, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Early chewing gum in the United States was often made from spruce tree resin.
• In the 1870s, Thomas Adams began experimenting with chicle, a natural latex from the sapodilla tree.
• Chicle produced a smoother, longer-lasting chew than resin-based gum.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Adams's chicle-based gum was an improvement over earlier gum. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Adams's chicle-based gum, developed in the 1870s, offered a smoother, longer-lasting chew than earlier gum made from spruce resin.","Early chewing gum in the United States was often made from spruce tree resin.","Chicle is a natural latex derived from the sapodilla tree.","Chewing gum remains a popular product today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific improvement, a smoother, longer-lasting chew, over the earlier resin-based gum, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The urban garden co-op charges members a small annual fee to help maintain shared tools and soil. ______ the co-op has waived that fee entirely for first-time gardeners under eighteen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The fee waiver for young gardeners is an exception to the general fee policy, a contrast correctly signaled by 'However.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Eli Whitney patented the cotton gin in 1794.
• The machine could clean cotton fifty times faster than doing so by hand.
• Its efficiency led plantation owners to expand cotton production and reliance on enslaved labor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain an unintended social consequence of the cotton gin's efficiency. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the cotton gin cleaned cotton fifty times faster than hand labor, plantation owners expanded production and deepened their reliance on enslaved labor.","Eli Whitney patented the cotton gin in 1794.","The cotton gin could clean cotton far faster than doing so by hand.","The gin's efficiency led plantation owners to expand cotton production."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the gin's speed to the specific social consequence of deepened reliance on enslaved labor; D states only the production-expansion half of that consequence, and B and C describe the invention or its speed without any social effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Organizers rerouted the marathon at the last minute after a water main broke along the original course. ______ runners found that the new route included a far steeper hill than organizers had anticipated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finding a steeper hill than organizers anticipated is a surprising discovery, which 'Unexpectedly' captures directly. 'Nonetheless' implies the discovery occurred despite some other obstacle, and 'Instead' suggests a planned outcome was replaced rather than an unplanned complication being discovered, so 'Unexpectedly' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea turtles can detect subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.
• Hatchlings use this sense to navigate the open ocean after leaving their birth beach.
• Many sea turtles return to lay eggs on the same beach where they themselves hatched.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how hatchling sea turtles are able to navigate the open ocean. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hatchling sea turtles navigate the open ocean by detecting subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.","Sea turtles can detect subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.","Many sea turtles return to lay eggs on the beach where they hatched.","Sea turtles face numerous threats throughout their long migrations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly explains the mechanism, magnetic field detection, that hatchlings use to navigate, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The antique watch show typically draws a modest crowd of regional collectors each year. ______ this year's show attracted serious collectors from three different countries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drawing international collectors breaks from the show's usual modest pattern, a contrast correctly signaled by 'However.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Richard Drew worked for 3M as a young engineer in the 1920s.
• He invented masking tape in 1925 after noticing painters struggled with uneven paint lines.
• He later invented transparent cellophane tape, marketed as Scotch tape, in 1930.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what inspired Drew to invent masking tape. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Drew invented masking tape in 1925 after observing painters struggle to create clean, even paint lines.","Richard Drew worked as a young engineer for 3M in the 1920s.","Drew later invented transparent cellophane tape in 1930.","Scotch tape became a widely used household product."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific observation that inspired the invention, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The chemistry teacher had assigned the same lab experiment every year without any changes. ______ this past year she redesigned it entirely after students kept reporting confusing instructions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Finally,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The redesign after years of repeating the same experiment unchanged is a long-delayed action, which 'Finally' correctly signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jesse Reno patented an early escalator design in 1892.
• His version was more like an inclined moving ramp than a staircase.
• It was first installed as a novelty ride at Coney Island in 1896.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how Reno's early escalator was first used. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Reno's early escalator design was first installed not in a building but as a novelty ride at Coney Island in 1896.","Jesse Reno patented an early escalator design in 1892.","Reno's version resembled an inclined moving ramp more than a staircase.","Escalators are now common in shopping malls and transit stations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific, surprising first use of the invention, as an amusement ride, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Analysts predicted that a sudden drop in oil prices would cripple the small nation's economy. ______ earlier investments in tourism and manufacturing cushioned most of the expected losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The economy being cushioned rather than crippled contradicts the analysts' prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Berlin Wall was erected in 1961 to divide East and West Berlin.
• It stood as a physical symbol of the Cold War division of Europe for nearly three decades.
• The wall fell in 1989 after mass protests and political upheaval in East Germany.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly the wall's fall followed decades of division. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["After standing as a symbol of Cold War division for nearly three decades, the Berlin Wall fell within months of the 1989 protests in East Germany.","The Berlin Wall was erected in 1961 to divide East and West Berlin.","The wall fell in 1989 after mass protests in East Germany.","The wall stood as a symbol of Cold War division for nearly three decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A sets the decades of division against the comparatively fast collapse to emphasize the contrast; C and D each state one half of that contrast, and B describes only the wall's construction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The botanical garden's rarest seeds lose viability if left at room temperature for more than a few hours. ______ staff now store every rare seed sample in a climate-controlled vault kept near freezing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For this reason,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The climate-controlled vault is a direct response to the seeds' fragility, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'For this reason,' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roy Plunkett accidentally discovered Teflon in 1938 while researching refrigerant gases.
• He noticed that a sample had polymerized into a slippery, heat-resistant solid.
• Teflon is now best known for its use in non-stick cookware.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Plunkett came to discover Teflon. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Plunkett discovered Teflon by accident in 1938 when a sample he was researching as a refrigerant gas polymerized into a slippery solid.","Roy Plunkett was researching refrigerant gases in 1938.","Teflon is now best known for its use in non-stick cookware.","Teflon is a synthetic material with a wide range of uses."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the accidental circumstances of the discovery, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The two rival chefs had spent years publicly criticizing each other's restaurants in local reviews. ______ they agreed to co-host a single charity dinner, cooking side by side for one night only.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two longtime public rivals cooking together is a genuinely surprising turn given their history, which 'Surprisingly' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' frames the dinner as happening despite an obstacle rather than as an unexpected reversal, and 'Instead' implies a plan was replaced rather than an unlikely event occurring, so 'Surprisingly' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A narwhal's tusk is actually an elongated front tooth that can grow over 8 feet long.
• The tusk is covered in nerve endings connected to the narwhal's brain.
• Scientists believe the tusk may help narwhals sense changes in water conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that a narwhal's tusk is a horn. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often mistaken for a horn, a narwhal's tusk is actually an elongated front tooth that can grow over 8 feet long.","A narwhal's tusk is covered in nerve endings connected to its brain.","Scientists believe the tusk may help narwhals sense changes in water conditions.","Narwhals live primarily in Arctic waters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the misconception that the tusk is a horn.$q$),
  ($q$ei-286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Gray whales migrate between Arctic feeding grounds and warmer breeding lagoons in Mexico.
• The round trip can cover more than 12,000 miles, among the longest of any mammal migration.
• Mother gray whales nurse their calves in the shallow, protected lagoons before heading north.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary length of the gray whale's migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A gray whale's round-trip migration between the Arctic and Mexico can cover more than 12,000 miles, among the longest of any mammal.","Gray whales migrate between Arctic feeding grounds and breeding lagoons in Mexico.","Mother gray whales nurse their calves in shallow, protected lagoons.","A gray whale's migration between the Arctic and Mexico is among the longest of any mammal."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the actual distance—more than 12,000 miles—that quantifies the migration's extraordinary length; D makes the same claim without a number, and B and C describe the migration's route or calf-rearing without addressing its length.$q$),
  ($q$ei-287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The chef spent months refining a single sauce recipe. ______ the dish became the restaurant's signature item.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sauce's refinement directly caused the dish's new status, so a cause-and-effect transition is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new wing was expected to open by spring. ______ construction delays pushed the opening to late fall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Therefore,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The delayed opening contradicts the original expectation, so the contrast word 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra rehearsed the symphony for nearly six weeks. ______ the opening night performance still contained several rough passages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite extensive rehearsal, problems remained, a concession best signaled by 'Even so.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy rains saturated the hillside for nearly a week. ______ a large section of the slope collapsed overnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The saturation directly caused the collapse, so the cause-and-effect transition 'Consequently' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new library branch offers free wifi and several quiet study rooms. ______ it hosts weekly workshops on resume writing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","However,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another service to the one named first, so an addition transition is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many coastal towns have adapted to rising sea levels in unconventional ways. ______ one town built a seawall almost entirely from recycled shipping containers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nevertheless,","Meanwhile,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific example of the general claim in the first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The engineer insisted that the bridge's design met every safety standard. ______ independent inspectors later found several welds that failed to meet code.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The inspectors' findings directly disprove the engineer's claim with contradicting evidence, which 'However' signals. 'Instead' would suit a plan replaced by what actually happened, not a claim corrected by inspection results, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The volcano had shown no signs of activity for nearly three decades. ______ it erupted violently without warning in 1991.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Specifically,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sudden eruption contrasts with decades of apparent dormancy, so 'Nevertheless' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The runner trained through the hottest months of summer. ______ she finished the marathon nearly ten minutes faster than her previous best.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","In contrast,","For example,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her improved time resulted directly from the training, so the cause-and-effect transition fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new vaccine proved highly effective against the original viral strain. ______ it also provided partial protection against a newly emerged variant.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","However,","Instead,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another benefit, so an addition transition like 'Moreover' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The startup's founders predicted rapid growth within the first year. ______ the company struggled to attract even a handful of early customers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Similarly,","For instance,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The company's struggles directly contradict the founders' prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Engineers have proposed several ways to extend smartphone battery life. ______ one team is developing a battery that recharges using ambient light.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Nonetheless,","Therefore,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence offers a specific example of the general proposals mentioned first.$q$),
  ($q$ei-299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest growing season in forty years. ______ crop yields fell by nearly a third.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","By contrast,","For instance,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drought directly caused the drop in yields, so a cause-and-effect transition is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Critics have long argued that online courses cannot replicate the engagement of in-person seminars. ______ a recent study found that online students in small discussion groups participated just as actively as their in-person peers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A study's contradicting findings disprove the critics' long-standing claim, which calls for the belief-correcting 'However.' 'Instead' fits a plan replaced by reality, not a claim overturned by research, so 'However' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The subway line was first proposed in the 1970s. ______ it did not break ground until nearly thirty years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Therefore,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The long delay between proposal and construction is an unexpected contrast, which 'Yet' signals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Migratory songbirds rely on stars to navigate at night. ______ many species also sense the Earth's magnetic field.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","Nevertheless,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another navigational tool to the one named first, so an addition transition fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The sculptor was celebrated during her lifetime for strict, minimalist forms. ______ her private sketchbooks reveal an exuberant, almost chaotic imagination.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","However,","Instead,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence explicitly sets two sides of the same artist—her public minimalist work and her private sketchbooks—side by side, which the parallel-comparison 'By contrast' captures precisely. 'However' would mark contrast more generally, and 'Instead' wrongly implies one erased the other rather than both coexisting, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The translator chose to preserve the poem's unusual line breaks rather than smooth them into conventional sentences. ______ she prioritized the original rhythm over easy readability.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","However,","For example,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence restates the first in different terms, so 'In other words' is the appropriate transition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A software error shut down the telescope's guidance system for six hours. ______ astronomers lost a rare chance to observe the comet's closest approach.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The shutdown directly caused the lost observation opportunity, so the cause-and-effect transition is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists generally agree that raising interest rates slows inflation. ______ the policy also risks slowing economic growth enough to cause a recession.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","However,","Nonetheless,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recession risk is an additional concern that holds alongside the agreed-upon benefit, not a contradiction of it, so the simultaneity marker 'At the same time' fits best. 'However' would wrongly frame the risk as negating the benefit, and 'Nonetheless' implies the risk persists despite some obstacle, so 'At the same time' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Sleep researchers have identified several habits that improve sleep quality. ______ keeping a consistent wake-up time appears to matter more than total hours slept.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nonetheless,","Thus,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific example of a general claim about helpful habits, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The redesigned park added shaded walking paths throughout the grounds. ______ it introduced a splash pad for young children.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Additionally,","Instead,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another feature of the redesign, so an addition transition is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The piano was invented in the early 1700s as a quieter alternative to the harpsichord. ______ by the late 1800s it had become the dominant keyboard instrument in concert halls.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Meanwhile,","For example,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a later outcome in a sequence of events, so 'Eventually' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Adding a small amount of catalyst lowered the reaction's activation energy substantially. ______ the reaction that once took hours now completed in minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","In contrast,","Nonetheless,","Granted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dramatically faster reaction is a direct, logical effect of the lowered activation energy, which 'Consequently' signals. 'In contrast' would require two different reactions being compared side by side, and 'Nonetheless' implies the speedup happened despite an obstacle rather than because of the catalyst, so 'Consequently' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Participants who studied material right before sleeping were expected to remember it poorly. ______ they outperformed participants who studied the same material in the morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Similarly,","For example,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The actual result contradicts the expectation stated first, so the contrast word 'Instead' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The defense conceded that the contract's wording was ambiguous. ______ it argued that the ambiguity should be resolved in its client's favor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite conceding the ambiguity, the defense still pressed its own interpretation, a concession-and-persistence relationship signaled by 'Nonetheless.' 'Admittedly' would redundantly repeat the concession rather than pivot to the argument that follows it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The newsroom cut its print staff by half over the past decade. ______ it tripled the size of its digital team.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Therefore,","For example,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two staffing changes happened over the same period, so the simultaneity transition 'Meanwhile' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Traditional brick construction insulates buildings well but takes months to complete. ______ prefabricated panel construction can be finished in a matter of weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Prefabricated construction is presented as the opposite of traditional brick construction in speed, so 'By contrast' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Regional dialects often preserve words that have vanished from standard usage. ______ some rural communities still use a term for 'icicle' that dates back centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Consequently,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific illustration of the general claim about preserved words, so 'For example' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Visiting the national park in winter means dealing with closed trails and limited services. ______ the crowds that overwhelm the park in summer are entirely absent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","Therefore,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The absence of summer crowds is an upside that contrasts with the winter drawbacks mentioned first, so 'However' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The factory replaced its manual assembly line with robotic arms capable of working around the clock. ______ output nearly doubled within the first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","In contrast,","Admittedly,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The near-doubling of output is a direct, logical effect of switching to round-the-clock robotic automation, which 'As a result' signals. 'In contrast' would require two different factories or systems being compared, and 'Admittedly' would concede a limitation before a main claim, neither of which fits a straightforward cause-and-effect result, so 'As a result' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybees communicate the location of food sources through a "waggle dance."
• The dance encodes both the distance and direction of the source relative to the sun.
• Scout bees perform the dance inside the hive after returning from a successful search.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the dance conveys precise spatial information. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through the waggle dance, scout honeybees encode both the distance and direction of a food source relative to the sun.","Scout bees perform the waggle dance inside the hive after a successful search.","Honeybees are important pollinators of many crops.","Only scout bees that have found food perform the waggle dance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific distance and direction information the dance conveys, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press in Europe around 1440.
• Before the press, books were copied by hand, a process that could take months.
• Within fifty years, printing presses had spread to over 200 European cities.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly the technology spread across Europe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Within just fifty years of its introduction, Gutenberg's printing press had spread to more than 200 cities across Europe.","Before the press, scribes copied books by hand, a process that could take months.","Gutenberg introduced his press in Europe around 1440.","Books became a popular form of entertainment during the Renaissance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the rapid spread of the press across hundreds of cities, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses can change both the color and texture of their skin in under a second.
• Specialized skin cells called chromatophores control the pigment changes.
• Unlike most camouflaging animals, octopuses achieve this despite being colorblind.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the surprising fact that octopuses camouflage effectively despite lacking color vision. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Remarkably, octopuses change skin color and texture in under a second despite being colorblind.","Chromatophores are specialized skin cells that control pigment changes.","Octopuses are among the most intelligent invertebrates.","Octopus skin can change texture as well as color."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines the speed of camouflage with the colorblindness detail, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Rosalind Franklin produced X-ray diffraction images that revealed DNA's helical structure.
• Her photograph, known as "Photo 51," was shown to Watson and Crick without her knowledge.
• Watson and Crick used her data to help build their 1953 model of DNA.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Franklin's contribution was used without her consent. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Franklin's X-ray image, Photo 51, was shown to Watson and Crick without her knowledge and used to help build their 1953 DNA model.","Watson and Crick published their model of DNA's structure in 1953.","Franklin's X-ray diffraction images revealed DNA's helical structure.","DNA carries genetic information in the form of a double helix."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states both the unauthorized sharing of her data and its use, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Iceland sits atop a volcanically active region with abundant underground heat.
• Roughly 90% of Icelandic homes are heated using geothermal energy.
• Geothermal plants also generate about a quarter of the country's electricity.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how widely geothermal energy is used for home heating specifically. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roughly 90% of homes in Iceland rely on geothermal energy for heating.","Iceland sits atop a volcanically active region with abundant underground heat.","Geothermal plants generate about a quarter of Iceland's electricity.","Iceland has a small population relative to its land area."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific heating statistic requested by the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman concrete structures like the Pantheon have survived for nearly 2,000 years.
• Modern concrete structures often begin to crack and deteriorate within decades.
• Researchers found that Roman concrete contains volcanic ash that reacts with seawater to self-heal tiny cracks.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Roman concrete has lasted so much longer than modern concrete. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roman concrete contains volcanic ash that reacts with seawater to self-heal tiny cracks, helping explain why structures like the Pantheon have survived nearly 2,000 years.","Modern concrete structures often crack and deteriorate within decades.","The Pantheon is one of the best-preserved buildings from ancient Rome.","Researchers continue to study ancient Roman building techniques."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the causal mechanism, the self-healing volcanic ash, that explains Roman concrete's durability.$q$),
  ($q$ei-324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Female anglerfish can be over sixty times the size of males.
• Males attach themselves permanently to a female's body shortly after finding her.
• Once attached, a male's eyes and internal organs gradually degrade, and he survives mainly by drawing nutrients from the female's bloodstream.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme physical transformation males undergo after attaching to a female. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["After attaching to a female, a male anglerfish's eyes and internal organs gradually degrade as he comes to survive mainly on nutrients drawn from her bloodstream.","Female anglerfish can be over sixty times the size of males.","Males attach themselves permanently to a female shortly after finding her.","After attaching, a male anglerfish survives mainly by drawing nutrients from the female's bloodstream."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes both the degrading of the male's eyes and organs and his reliance on the female's bloodstream, capturing the full extremity of the transformation; C states only the attachment, and D states only the survival mechanism without the degeneration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Some bamboo species can grow up to 35 inches in a single day.
• Bamboo is technically a type of grass, not a tree.
• Its rapid growth makes it a popular material for sustainable construction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusually fast bamboo can grow. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Certain bamboo species can grow as much as 35 inches in a single day.","Bamboo is technically a grass rather than a tree.","Bamboo's rapid growth makes it popular for sustainable construction.","Many homebuilders are turning to sustainable materials."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific daily growth figure that directly supports the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• English longbowmen at the Battle of Agincourt in 1415 could fire roughly ten to twelve arrows per minute.
• Drawing a war longbow required a pull of up to 150 pounds of force.
• Skeletal remains of longbowmen show enlarged, asymmetrical bones in their drawing arms.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the physical toll that using a longbow took on archers' bodies. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Skeletal remains of longbowmen reveal enlarged, asymmetrical bones in their drawing arms, a result of repeatedly pulling up to 150 pounds of force.","Longbowmen at Agincourt could fire ten to twelve arrows per minute.","The Battle of Agincourt took place in 1415.","Drawing a war longbow required a pull of up to 150 pounds of force."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A pairs the skeletal evidence with the 150-pound pull it resulted from to show the lasting physical toll; D states the strain alone without the evidence of damage, and B and C are unrelated to the archers' bodies.$q$),
  ($q$ei-327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Swiss engineer George de Mestral invented Velcro after noticing burrs clinging to his dog's fur and his clothing during a hike.
• He spent nearly a decade refining the hook-and-loop design before it became commercially viable.
• Velcro later became essential for securing equipment in zero-gravity spacecraft.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Velcro's invention was inspired by observing nature. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["George de Mestral invented Velcro after noticing how burrs clung to his dog's fur and his own clothing during a hike.","De Mestral spent nearly a decade refining the hook-and-loop design.","Velcro later became essential for securing equipment in zero-gravity spacecraft.","Velcro is widely used in clothing and footwear today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the natural observation, burrs clinging to fur, that inspired the invention.$q$),
  ($q$ei-328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest's peak was first accurately measured in 1856 by a survey team using triangulation.
• Modern GPS measurements place its height at 29,031.7 feet.
• The mountain's height changes slightly each year due to tectonic activity and snowfall.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Everest's height is not perfectly fixed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Everest's height actually changes slightly from year to year because of tectonic activity and snowfall.","Everest's peak was first accurately measured in 1856 using triangulation.","Modern GPS measurements place Everest's height at 29,031.7 feet.","Many climbers attempt to summit Everest each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states that the height varies and explains why, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest repositories of knowledge in the ancient world.
• Ships arriving in Alexandria's harbor were sometimes required to surrender scrolls to be copied for the library.
• Much of the library's collection was lost over centuries to fires, neglect, and conflict.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the aggressive methods used to build the library's collection. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ships arriving in Alexandria's harbor were sometimes required to surrender scrolls so that copies could be added to the library's collection.","The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest repositories of knowledge in the ancient world.","Much of the library's collection was lost to fires, neglect, and conflict over the centuries.","Alexandria was an important center of trade in the ancient Mediterranean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the forced surrender of scrolls, the specific acquisition method named in the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coral gets its color from symbiotic algae living in its tissue.
• When water temperatures rise even slightly above normal for extended periods, corals expel these algae.
• Without the algae, coral turns white and loses its main source of nutrients, often leading to death if conditions don't improve quickly.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the mechanism by which warm water causes coral to die. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Prolonged exposure to slightly elevated water temperatures causes coral to expel its algae, leaving it without its main nutrient source and vulnerable to death.","Coral gets its color from symbiotic algae living in its tissue.","Bleached coral turns white when it loses its algae.","Coral can die if it loses its main source of nutrients and conditions don't improve quickly."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A traces the full chain from warm water to algae expulsion to the risk of death, as the mechanism goal requires; B, C, and D each name only one link in that chain.$q$),
  ($q$ei-331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that remained edible after 3,000 years.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity make it inhospitable to most bacteria.
• Bees produce honey by repeatedly regurgitating and evaporating nectar.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how remarkably long honey can remain preserved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that remained edible after 3,000 years.","Honey's low moisture content and acidity make it inhospitable to most bacteria.","Bees produce honey by repeatedly regurgitating and evaporating nectar.","Honey has been used as a natural sweetener for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the striking 3,000-year preservation figure that directly fulfills the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.
• He composed many of his most celebrated works, including his Ninth Symphony, after becoming almost completely deaf.
• He reportedly sawed the legs off his piano so he could feel the vibrations of the notes through the floor.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how Beethoven adapted physically to compose despite his hearing loss. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Beethoven reportedly sawed the legs off his piano so he could feel the vibrations of the notes through the floor.","Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.","Beethoven composed his Ninth Symphony after becoming almost completely deaf.","Beethoven is considered one of the most influential composers in history."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes a concrete physical adaptation Beethoven used to continue composing, matching the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Scientists extract long cylinders of ice, called ice cores, from deep within Antarctic glaciers.
• Trapped air bubbles in the ice preserve samples of the atmosphere from thousands of years ago.
• The oldest continuous ice cores recovered so far record roughly 800,000 years of climate history.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far back in time ice cores allow scientists to study Earth's climate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The oldest continuous ice cores recovered so far allow scientists to study roughly 800,000 years of Earth's climate history.","Scientists extract long cylinders of ice, called ice cores, from Antarctic glaciers.","Trapped air bubbles in the ice preserve samples of ancient atmosphere.","Antarctica is the coldest continent on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific 800,000-year span that fulfills the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In the 1630s, the price of some rare tulip bulbs in the Netherlands rose to many times a skilled worker's annual salary.
• Contracts to buy tulip bulbs were traded on futures markets before the flowers even bloomed.
• The market collapsed abruptly in February 1637, leaving many traders with worthless contracts.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how disconnected tulip prices had become from the flowers' practical value. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the height of the 1630s tulip craze, some rare bulbs sold for prices many times a skilled worker's annual salary.","Contracts to buy tulip bulbs were traded on futures markets before the flowers bloomed.","The tulip market collapsed abruptly in February 1637.","Tulip bulb prices rose to very high levels during the 1630s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific comparison—many times a worker's annual salary—that shows how disconnected prices were from practical value; D makes the same claim without that comparison, and B and C describe speculation or the collapse rather than the price disconnect itself.$q$),
  ($q$ei-335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Great Wall spanned many centuries and several different dynasties.
• The wall is not a single continuous structure but a network of walls and fortifications.
• Popular claims that it is visible from the Moon with the naked eye have been debunked by astronauts.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the common misconception that the Great Wall is visible from the Moon. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite popular claims, astronauts have confirmed that the Great Wall is not actually visible from the Moon with the naked eye.","Construction of the Great Wall spanned many centuries and several dynasties.","The wall is a network of walls and fortifications rather than one continuous structure.","The Great Wall attracts millions of tourists every year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly names and corrects the specific misconception stated in the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The platypus is one of only a few mammals that lay eggs.
• Its bill contains thousands of electroreceptors that detect tiny electrical fields produced by prey muscles.
• Platypuses hunt with their eyes, ears, and nostrils closed, relying entirely on this electrical sense underwater.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how completely the platypus depends on electroreception while hunting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While hunting underwater, platypuses close their eyes, ears, and nostrils and rely entirely on electroreceptors in their bill to detect prey.","The platypus is one of only a few mammals that lay eggs.","The platypus's bill contains thousands of electroreceptors.","Platypuses are found only in Australia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the complete sensory shutdown and total reliance on electroreception, matching the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.
• The mold, Penicillium, had created a zone around itself where no bacteria could grow.
• It took more than a decade after Fleming's observation before penicillin was mass-produced for medical use.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Fleming's discovery was accidental. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming's discovery began almost by accident, when he noticed in 1928 that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.","The mold created a zone around itself where no bacteria could grow.","It took more than a decade before penicillin was mass-produced.","Penicillin is still used to treat bacterial infections today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A frames the discovery as accidental contamination, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Both Voyager probes, launched in 1977, carry a gold-plated phonograph record.
• The record includes greetings in 55 languages, natural sounds like whale calls, and musical selections from around the world.
• The probes are expected to keep traveling through interstellar space long after Earth itself may no longer exist in its current form.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary timescale over which the record might outlast Earth as we know it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Voyager probes are expected to keep carrying their golden records through interstellar space long after Earth itself may no longer exist in its current form.","The record includes greetings in 55 languages and natural sounds like whale calls.","Both Voyager probes were launched in 1977.","The record contains musical selections from around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the extraordinary timescale outlasting Earth, directly matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The now-familiar looped wire paperclip design was patented in 1899 by Norwegian inventor Johan Vaaler.
• Earlier versions of paper fasteners date back to the early 1800s but were far less practical.
• The paperclip's simple design has remained largely unchanged for over a century.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how little the paperclip's design has changed since its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The paperclip's simple looped design has remained largely unchanged for more than a century.","The looped wire paperclip was patented in 1899 by Johan Vaaler.","Earlier versions of paper fasteners date back to the early 1800s.","Paperclips are commonly used to organize loose papers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly states the persistence of the design, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Some desert wildflower seeds can remain dormant in the soil for decades, waiting for enough rainfall to germinate.
• A single unusually wet season can trigger a "superbloom," when millions of dormant seeds sprout at once.
• Superblooms can transform an entire desert landscape with color for just a few weeks.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how long desert seeds can wait before germinating. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some desert wildflower seeds can lie dormant in the soil for decades before enough rainfall finally triggers germination.","A wet season can trigger a \"superbloom\" of millions of seeds at once.","Superblooms can transform a desert landscape with color for a few weeks.","Deserts receive very little annual rainfall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the decades-long dormancy period that fulfills the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Roman Empire built an estimated 50,000 miles of paved roads at its height.
• Roads were constructed with multiple layers, including a foundation of large stones beneath finer gravel and paving.
• Many sections of Roman road remain usable or visible today, nearly 2,000 years after construction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the durability of Roman road construction. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Many sections of Roman road remain usable or visible today, nearly 2,000 years after they were built.","The Roman Empire built an estimated 50,000 miles of paved roads.","Roman roads were constructed with multiple layers, including large foundation stones.","Roman roads connected cities across the empire for trade and military use."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the roads' survival across nearly 2,000 years, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Antikythera mechanism, recovered from a shipwreck in 1901, is an ancient Greek device dated to around 100 BCE.
• It used a complex system of at least 30 bronze gears to predict astronomical positions and eclipses.
• Devices of comparable mechanical complexity would not reappear in Europe for well over a thousand years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far ahead of its time the device's technology was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Devices of comparable mechanical complexity to the Antikythera mechanism would not reappear in Europe for well over a thousand years.","The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck in 1901.","It used at least 30 bronze gears to predict astronomical positions.","The device is dated to around 100 BCE."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A makes the explicit historical comparison showing how far ahead of its time the device was.$q$),
  ($q$ei-343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elephants have the largest number of genes dedicated to smell receptors of any mammal studied.
• Researchers estimate elephants can detect some odors from several kilometers away.
• Elephants use smell to locate water sources and distinguish between individual herd members.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far elephants can detect scent. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers estimate that elephants can detect some odors from several kilometers away.","Elephants have the largest number of smell-receptor genes of any mammal studied.","Elephants use smell to distinguish between individual herd members.","Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific detection distance that fulfills the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Ancient Babylonian mathematicians used a placeholder symbol for zero as early as 300 BCE, but did not treat it as a number itself.
• Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the 7th century, were among the first to define zero as a number with its own arithmetic rules.
• The concept of zero as a true number later spread to the Islamic world and eventually to Europe.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Indian mathematicians were the first to treat zero as a genuine number rather than just a placeholder. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the 7th century, were among the first to define zero as a true number with its own arithmetic rules.","Babylonian mathematicians used a placeholder symbol for zero as early as 300 BCE.","The concept of zero later spread to the Islamic world and then Europe.","Zero is essential to modern mathematics and computing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A credits Indian mathematicians specifically with defining zero as a true number, matching the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between Canada and central Mexico each year.
• No single monarch completes the entire round trip; the journey takes several successive generations.
• Scientists still do not fully understand how later generations find the same overwintering sites their ancestors used.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the puzzling fact that monarchs find overwintering sites they themselves have never visited. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Remarkably, scientists still do not fully understand how monarch generations that have never visited the overwintering sites manage to find them.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between Canada and Mexico each year.","No single monarch completes the entire round trip alone.","Monarch caterpillars feed exclusively on milkweed plants."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the unexplained navigation puzzle across generations, matching the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Challenger Deep, located in the Mariana Trench, is the deepest known point in the ocean at nearly 36,000 feet.
• Water pressure at that depth is more than 1,000 times greater than at sea level.
• Only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have ever been completed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme pressure conditions at the bottom of the Challenger Deep. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the bottom of the Challenger Deep, water pressure is more than 1,000 times greater than it is at sea level.","The Challenger Deep is the deepest known point in the ocean, at nearly 36,000 feet.","Only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have been completed.","Because it is so deep, only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have been completed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the actual pressure figure the goal asks to emphasize; the others describe the trench's depth or the rarity of descents without quantifying the pressure itself.$q$),
  ($q$ei-347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The oldest surviving photograph was taken by Joseph Nicephore Niepce in 1826 or 1827.
• Because the exposure took several hours, moving objects like people and carriages did not appear in the final image.
• The photograph shows the view from an upstairs window of Niepce's estate in France.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the earliest photograph shows no people, even though it was taken from a window overlooking a presumably busy area. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the exposure took several hours, moving objects like people and carriages simply did not appear in the final image.","The photograph was taken by Joseph Nicephore Niepce in 1826 or 1827.","The photograph shows the view from an upstairs window of Niepce's estate.","Photography developed rapidly as a technology throughout the 19th century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the reason, the hours-long exposure, that explains why no moving people appear in the image.$q$),
  ($q$ei-348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cuneiform, developed in ancient Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE, is among the earliest known writing systems.
• Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into soft clay tablets using a reed stylus.
• Clay tablets, unlike papyrus or parchment, survive well over thousands of years, especially when baked or burned.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many cuneiform tablets have survived to the present day. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because clay tablets survive well over thousands of years, especially when baked or burned, many cuneiform texts have endured to the present day.","Cuneiform was developed in ancient Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE.","Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into clay using a reed stylus.","Cuneiform is considered among the earliest known writing systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the material explanation, clay's durability, for why so many tablets have survived.$q$),
  ($q$ei-349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A massive volcanic eruption in 1815 spewed ash high into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight across much of the globe. ______ crops failed in many regions the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The crop failures were a direct consequence of blocked sunlight, so the cause-and-effect transition 'As a result' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$An engineer once predicted that the wooden footbridge would collapse within a decade. ______ it has stood for more than fifty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","In addition,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bridge's long survival contradicts the engineer's prediction, so the contrast transition 'Instead' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum opened a new wing devoted to contemporary sculpture. ______ it extended its weekend hours to accommodate more visitors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","However,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Extending weekend hours is a second, related change alongside the new wing, so the addition transition 'In addition' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The chef built a reputation for unusual flavor pairings. ______ one popular dessert combines dark chocolate with chili powder.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nevertheless,","As a result,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dessert is a specific example of the chef's unusual pairings, so 'For instance' correctly introduces it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Rainfall across the valley fell far below average for three consecutive years. ______ reservoir levels dropped to their lowest point in decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Low reservoir levels are the effect of the sustained low rainfall, so the cause-and-effect transition 'Consequently' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics dismissed the novel as derivative when it was first published. ______ it went on to sell millions of copies worldwide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The novel's commercial success contrasts with its harsh reception, so the concession transition 'Nevertheless' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Finnish and Estonian both evolved from a common Uralic ancestor language. ______ both languages rely heavily on noun case endings rather than prepositions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","However,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence states another point of resemblance between the languages, so 'Similarly' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The biologist first drafted a hypothesis about the bird's migration pattern. ______ she spent two summers tracking tagged birds to test it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Afterward,","However,","In other words,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tracking the birds happened after drafting the hypothesis, so the sequence transition 'Afterward' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city has become known for its vibrant street art scene. ______ one downtown alley is covered wall to wall with murals by local artists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In particular,","Nevertheless,","As a result,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The alley is a specific instance of the broader street art scene, so 'In particular' correctly narrows the focus.$q$),
  ($q$ei-358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company switched to biodegradable packaging for all of its products. ______ its shipping costs rose because the new materials were bulkier than the old plastic wrap.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in shipping costs is a direct effect of the packaging switch, so 'As a result' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists widely expected a recession once interest rates rose sharply. ______ the economy grew faster than it had in the previous three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","As a result,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The economy's unexpected growth directly contradicts the predicted recession, so the contrast transition 'Instead' is needed, not a cause-and-effect or addition word.$q$),
  ($q$ei-360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new sequencing method takes nearly twice as long as the older technique. ______ it produces results with far fewer errors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The accuracy gain holds despite the slower speed, so the concessive transition 'Even so' is needed rather than a cause-and-effect or addition word.$q$),
  ($q$ei-361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Regulators tightened emissions standards for new vehicles. ______ automakers accelerated development of electric models.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","In the same way,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Automakers' response was a direct consequence of the new standards, so 'Accordingly' correctly signals that cause-and-effect relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The artist spent decades perfecting her technique in oil painting. ______ she mastered bronze casting well into her seventies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Instead,","As a result,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mastering bronze casting is an additional, impressive accomplishment alongside painting, so the intensifying addition transition 'Moreover' fits best.$q$),
  ($q$ei-363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Students in the morning discussion section scored consistently well on the exam. ______ those in the afternoon section struggled with the same material.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The afternoon section's performance directly opposes the morning section's, so the contrast transition 'By contrast' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The budget phone costs significantly less than its competitors. ______ its camera and battery life fall noticeably short of industry standards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That said,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The weaker specs qualify the praise of the low price, so the concessive transition 'That said' is needed rather than a result or example word.$q$),
  ($q$ei-365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The ancient manuscript was exposed to damp conditions for centuries. ______ most of its pages have become too brittle to handle without specialized equipment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The brittleness is a direct effect of centuries of dampness, so the cause-and-effect transition 'As a result' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The team lost three starting players to injury midseason. ______ it still managed to qualify for the playoffs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Qualifying for the playoffs happened despite the injuries, so the concessive transition 'Nonetheless' is required.$q$),
  ($q$ei-367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest growing season on record. ______ many farmers had to sell off portions of their livestock to cover their losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a consequence,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Selling livestock was a direct consequence of the severe drought, so 'As a consequence' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The orchestra's new conductor has reshaped its sound dramatically. ______ she slowed the tempo of several classical pieces to emphasize their emotional weight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","However,","In addition,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slowing the tempo explains precisely how the conductor reshaped the sound, so 'Specifically' fits better than a word suggesting a separate added action.$q$),
  ($q$ei-369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$One proposal would fund the repairs through a property tax increase. ______ the alternative plan would rely on a one-time fee charged to new construction projects.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["On the other hand,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second plan is presented as an alternative to the first, so the contrast transition 'On the other hand' is needed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Parents complained for years about the school's rigid dress code. ______ the district revised the policy to allow more flexibility.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In response,","For example,","Similarly,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The policy revision was a reaction to the complaints, so 'In response' correctly signals that cause-and-effect relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers warned repeatedly that the dam's spillway was undersized for a major flood. ______ officials delayed funding the upgrade for another decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even then,","As a result,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Officials ignored the warnings despite them, so the concessive transition 'Even then' is needed rather than a cause-and-effect word.$q$),
  ($q$ei-372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers first identified the compound in a soil sample from a remote rainforest. ______ laboratory tests revealed that it had powerful antifungal properties.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Subsequently,","However,","Similarly,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lab tests happened after the initial identification, so the sequence transition 'Subsequently' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The committee's decision was procedurally sound but substantively flawed. ______ the committee followed every required step yet reached a conclusion unsupported by the evidence it gathered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That is,","Namely,","Specifically,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause restates the same idea in concrete terms—procedurally fine but substantively unsupported—rather than introducing a new example or a particular item, so the restating transition 'That is' fits best. 'Namely' and 'Specifically' both introduce a specific instance rather than paraphrase the whole claim, and 'However' wrongly suggests a contradiction rather than a clarification, so 'That is' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup's app undeniably simplified scheduling for small medical clinics. ______ few of those clinics could justify its steep monthly subscription fee once a free alternative appeared.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Granted,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clinics dropping the app despite its real usefulness is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Even so' signals. 'Granted' would need to introduce the concession itself rather than follow it, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the usefulness was replaced rather than simply not enough to keep subscribers, so 'Even so' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's rough, unfinished appearance is not the result of neglect. ______ the artist deliberately left tool marks visible to emphasize the carving process.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Rather,","Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence rejects one explanation (neglect) and replaces it with the true one (deliberate artistic choice), a corrective relationship signaled by 'Rather.' 'Admittedly' would wrongly concede the neglect explanation as true, and 'Nonetheless' implies the deliberate choice persisted despite an obstacle rather than replacing a false explanation, so 'Rather' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The algorithm's accuracy plateaus once the training set exceeds roughly ten million examples, a point of diminishing returns. ______ beyond that threshold, adding more data barely improves its performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","Namely,","In short,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence just paraphrases the single idea—diminishing returns past a threshold—in simpler words, which 'In other words' signals precisely. 'Namely' would introduce a specific named example rather than a paraphrase, and 'In short' implies condensing several prior points into a brief conclusion rather than restating one idea, so 'In other words' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$In coastal towns, rising sea levels have forced many residents to relocate inland over the past decade. ______ in some inland floodplains, the same decade has seen a surge of new residential development despite the flood risk.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","However,","Instead,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The inland surge in development is the exact opposite of the coastal retreat just described, a parallel reversal that 'Conversely' captures precisely. 'However' marks contrast more generally without that explicit mirror-opposite framing, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the coastal retreat is being replaced rather than mirrored by an opposite trend elsewhere, so 'Conversely' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Historians have long criticized the treaty's vague wording as an invitation to future disputes. ______ the treaty held firm for nearly a century without a single major violation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Nonetheless,","In contrast,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The treaty's long, violation-free durability is a direct contrast with historians' criticism of its vague wording—two coexisting facts in tension—which plain 'Yet' signals. 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct subjects side by side, not one treaty's own track record, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Bypass surgery carries well-documented risks for patients over eighty. ______ for Mr. Alvarez, forgoing treatment carried a far higher risk of sudden cardiac failure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Admittedly,","Nonetheless,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence first concedes a general truth (surgery's documented risk) before pivoting to a specific case where the opposite calculus applies, which the concession-before-claim 'Admittedly' signals. 'Nonetheless' would need to follow the conceded fact with something happening despite it, not pivot to a specific contrasting case, so 'Admittedly' is correct.$q$),
  ($q$ei-380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sourdough starter is a mixture of flour and water that captures wild yeast and bacteria from the surrounding air.
• Lactobacillus bacteria in the starter ferment sugars in the dough and produce lactic acid as a byproduct.
• The lactic acid is responsible for sourdough bread's distinctive tangy flavor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why sourdough bread tastes tangy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lactobacillus bacteria in the starter produce lactic acid, which gives sourdough bread its distinctive tangy flavor.","Sourdough starter is a mixture of flour and water that captures wild yeast and bacteria from the air.","Sourdough bread has been made using natural fermentation for thousands of years.","Bakers must feed a sourdough starter regularly to keep it active."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the lactic acid byproduct that directly explains the tangy flavor; the others describe the starter or its history without explaining the taste.$q$),
  ($q$ei-381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybee scouts perform a figure-eight waggle dance inside the hive after finding a good food source.
• The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun.
• The duration of the waggling portion of the dance indicates how far away the food source is.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how other bees learn the distance to a food source from the waggle dance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The duration of the waggling portion of the dance tells other bees how far away the food source is.","Honeybee scouts perform a figure-eight dance after finding a good food source.","The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the food's direction relative to the sun.","Bees rely on dances to communicate information inside the hive."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A specifically links the dance's duration to communicating distance, which is the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The aurora borealis occurs when charged particles from the sun collide with gases in Earth's atmosphere.
• Oxygen collisions tend to produce green or red light, while nitrogen collisions tend to produce blue or purple light.
• The aurora is most visible near the magnetic poles, where Earth's magnetic field funnels the charged particles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what causes the different colors seen in the aurora borealis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Oxygen collisions tend to produce green or red light, while nitrogen collisions tend to produce blue or purple light.","The aurora borealis occurs when charged particles from the sun collide with atmospheric gases.","The aurora is most visible near Earth's magnetic poles.","Earth's magnetic field funnels charged particles toward the poles."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A identifies which gases produce which colors, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coast redwoods can grow bark more than a foot thick at the base of the trunk.
• The thick bark contains very little resin, which makes it difficult for fire to ignite.
• The bark also provides insulation that protects the tree's living tissue from heat during a forest fire.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why coast redwoods often survive forest fires that kill other trees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Their thick, low-resin bark resists ignition and insulates the tree's living tissue from the heat of a forest fire.","Coast redwoods can grow bark more than a foot thick at the base of the trunk.","Coast redwoods are among the tallest trees in the world.","Forest fires periodically sweep through coastal redwood forests."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A combines both the fire-resistant and insulating properties of the bark to explain the trees' survival.$q$),
  ($q$ei-384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Early clothing fasteners like buttons and hooks required time and dexterity to fasten one at a time.
• The modern zipper, patented in the early twentieth century, uses interlocking teeth that close with a single pull of a slider.
• Zippers quickly became popular for boots, jackets, and later a wide range of clothing.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the zipper was a convenient improvement over earlier fasteners. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike buttons and hooks, which had to be fastened one at a time, the zipper closes an entire seam with a single pull of a slider.","The modern zipper was patented in the early twentieth century.","Zippers quickly became popular for boots and jackets.","Buttons and hooks were used on clothing for centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly contrasts the zipper's one-motion closure with the slower, one-at-a-time fastening it replaced.$q$),
  ($q$ei-385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many bat species are active at night and hunt flying insects in near-total darkness.
• Bats emit high-frequency calls and listen for the echoes that bounce off nearby objects.
• By analyzing the timing and pitch of the returning echoes, a bat can judge an object's distance, size, and movement.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how bats are able to hunt successfully in the dark. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By emitting calls and analyzing the timing and pitch of the returning echoes, bats can judge the distance, size, and movement of nearby objects even in darkness.","Many bat species are active at night and hunt flying insects.","Bats emit high-frequency calls that are often inaudible to humans.","Bats are found on every continent except Antarctica."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the mechanism, echo analysis, that lets bats hunt without relying on sight.$q$),
  ($q$ei-386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming returned from vacation to find a petri dish of bacteria contaminated with mold.
• Fleming noticed that the bacteria near the mold had been killed, while bacteria elsewhere on the dish had grown normally.
• This chance observation led Fleming to isolate the substance, later named penicillin, that the mold produced.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how accidental Fleming's discovery of penicillin was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming's discovery began with an accidental observation: bacteria near a stray mold contamination had died, while bacteria elsewhere on the dish had not.","Alexander Fleming isolated the substance that the mold produced and later named it penicillin.","Fleming returned from vacation in 1928 to examine his petri dishes.","Penicillin became one of the most important medical discoveries of the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A highlights the unplanned contamination and chance observation that make the discovery accidental.$q$),
  ($q$ei-387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic during the northern summer.
• It then migrates to spend the southern summer near Antarctica, following a round-trip route of roughly 60,000 miles.
• This is considered the longest annual migration of any animal.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how extreme the Arctic tern's migration distance is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Arctic tern's round-trip migration covers roughly 60,000 miles each year, the longest annual journey of any animal.","The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic during the northern summer.","The Arctic tern spends the southern summer near Antarctica.","Many bird species migrate long distances between breeding and non-breeding seasons."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific extreme distance and its record-setting status, matching the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before standardized shipping containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece by dockworkers, a slow and labor-intensive process.
• Standardized steel containers, introduced in the mid-twentieth century, could be lifted by crane directly between ships, trains, and trucks.
• This standardization cut loading times from days to hours and sharply reduced shipping costs.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much the standardized shipping container improved the efficiency of global trade. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By allowing cargo to move directly between ships, trains, and trucks, standardized containers cut loading times from days to hours and sharply reduced shipping costs.","Before standardized containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece by dockworkers.","Standardized steel shipping containers were introduced in the mid-twentieth century.","Global trade expanded significantly over the second half of the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific efficiency gains, time and cost, that fulfill the goal of emphasizing improved efficiency.$q$),
  ($q$ei-389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, a slow process that kept them scarce and expensive.
• Gutenberg's movable-type press, developed around 1440, allowed identical pages to be printed quickly and in large numbers.
• Within decades, the price of books fell sharply, and significantly more people across Europe learned to read.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the printing press contributed to rising literacy rates in Europe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the press could print identical pages quickly and in large numbers, book prices fell sharply, and significantly more people learned to read.","Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, a slow and expensive process.","Gutenberg developed his movable-type press around 1440.","Handwritten books were scarce and valuable before the printing press existed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A links the press's output and falling prices directly to the rise in literacy, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest's summit is covered by a layer of snow and ice whose depth can change from year to year.
• Surveyors must also account for slight shifts in the Himalayas caused by ongoing tectonic activity.
• Different surveying expeditions have measured the mountain's height as anywhere from about 29,000 to just over 29,035 feet.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why different surveys have measured slightly different heights for Mount Everest. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the snow and ice layer on the summit varies and the Himalayas shift slightly due to tectonic activity, surveys have measured the mountain's height differently.","Mount Everest's summit is covered by a layer of snow and ice.","Different surveying expeditions have measured the mountain's height as anywhere from about 29,000 to just over 29,035 feet.","The Himalayas were formed by the collision of tectonic plates."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the two reasons, variable snow depth and tectonic shifts, that explain the measurement differences; choice C merely restates the discrepancy without explaining it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Scientists drill long cylindrical cores of ice from the Antarctic ice sheet, where layers of snow have compacted into ice over hundreds of thousands of years.
• Tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in each layer preserve a sample of the atmosphere at the time the snow fell.
• By measuring carbon dioxide levels in these trapped air bubbles, researchers can reconstruct how the atmosphere's composition has changed over time.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how ice cores allow scientists to study the ancient atmosphere. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in each ice layer preserve a sample of the atmosphere from when the snow fell, letting researchers measure past carbon dioxide levels.","Scientists drill long cylindrical cores of ice from the Antarctic ice sheet.","Layers of snow in Antarctica have compacted into ice over hundreds of thousands of years.","Antarctica holds the majority of the world's fresh water in its ice sheet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the trapped-air mechanism that lets scientists study the ancient atmosphere, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Flamingos are often seen standing on a single leg for extended periods, including while asleep.
• Researchers found that flamingos use less muscular effort to balance on one leg than on two, likely due to how their leg joints lock in place.
• Standing on one leg also allows a flamingo to keep the other leg tucked against its body, reducing heat loss in cold water.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a functional benefit of the one-legged stance beyond balance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tucking one leg against the body while standing on the other helps a flamingo reduce heat loss in cold water.","Flamingos are often seen standing on a single leg for extended periods.","Flamingos use less muscular effort to balance on one leg than on two.","Flamingo leg joints can lock in place while the bird stands."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal asks for a benefit beyond balance; only choice A names the heat-conservation benefit, while the other relevant-sounding choices concern balance, not warmth.$q$),
  ($q$ei-393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone, discovered in 1799, contains the same decree inscribed in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek.
• Scholars at the time could already read ancient Greek fluently.
• By comparing the Greek text to the hieroglyphic text word by word, researchers were gradually able to decode the meanings of Egyptian hieroglyphs.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Rosetta Stone allowed scholars to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because scholars could already read the stone's Greek inscription, comparing it word by word to the hieroglyphic text let them gradually decode the hieroglyphs' meanings.","The Rosetta Stone was discovered in 1799.","The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree in three different scripts.","Ancient Greek was already a well-understood language among scholars of the time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the comparative method, using known Greek to decode unknown hieroglyphs, that fulfills the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The SR-71 Blackbird reconnaissance jet cruised at speeds above Mach 3, generating extreme frictional heat on its skin.
• Engineers coated the aircraft's titanium skin with a special black paint.
• The black paint helped the skin radiate away excess heat more effectively and also reduced the aircraft's radar visibility.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the SR-71's black paint was functionally useful, not merely a stylistic choice. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The black paint helped the Blackbird's skin radiate away the extreme heat generated at high speed more effectively.","The SR-71 Blackbird cruised at speeds above Mach 3.","Engineers coated the aircraft's titanium skin with a special black paint.","The SR-71 Blackbird was used for reconnaissance missions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A ties the paint to a specific functional benefit, heat radiation, rather than simply restating that the paint was applied.$q$),
  ($q$ei-395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venice is built on a cluster of marshy islands in a lagoon, and its foundations rest on wooden piles driven into soft sediment.
• Decades of groundwater pumping in the twentieth century caused the sediment beneath the city to compact and settle.
• At the same time, global sea levels have been rising, increasing the frequency of flooding in the city's lowest-lying squares.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain that Venice's flooding problem results from two separate factors. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Venice's flooding stems both from the city's own sediment settling due to past groundwater pumping and from the broader rise in global sea levels.","Venice is built on a cluster of marshy islands in a lagoon.","Venice's foundations rest on wooden piles driven into soft sediment.","Global sea levels have been rising in recent decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names both contributing factors, local subsidence and global sea-level rise, as the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cats commonly purr when content, such as while being petted or resting near their owners.
• Cats have also been observed purring while injured, giving birth, or near death.
• Researchers have proposed that purring may aid healing through vibration, but no single explanation has been confirmed for all cases.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that scientists do not fully understand why cats purr. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although purring may aid healing through vibration, researchers have not confirmed a single explanation that accounts for all the situations in which cats purr.","Cats commonly purr when content, such as while being petted.","Cats have been observed purring while injured or giving birth.","Purring may help cats heal through the vibrations it produces."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly states that no confirmed explanation exists, matching the goal of emphasizing scientific uncertainty.$q$),
  ($q$ei-397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many forest trees are connected underground by networks of mycorrhizal fungi attached to their roots.
• Through these fungal networks, a tree can send sugars to a neighboring tree that is shaded or struggling.
• Some researchers have also found evidence that trees send chemical warning signals through the same network when attacked by insects.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a specific way trees use the fungal network to help a struggling neighbor. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A tree can send sugars through the fungal network to a neighboring tree that is shaded or struggling.","Many forest trees are connected underground by networks of mycorrhizal fungi.","Trees may send chemical warning signals through the fungal network when attacked by insects.","Mycorrhizal fungi attach themselves to the roots of forest trees."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A describes the specific nutrient-sharing behavior that helps a struggling neighboring tree, as the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many people assume the Leaning Tower of Pisa was designed to lean.
• In fact, the tower began tilting during construction in the twelfth century because its foundation was laid in unstable, soft soil.
• Engineers in the twentieth century installed counterweights and extracted soil from beneath the foundation to stabilize the tilt without making the tower perfectly straight.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the common misconception that the tower's lean was intentional. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Contrary to popular belief, the tower began tilting during construction because its foundation was laid in unstable, soft soil.","Engineers in the twentieth century installed counterweights to stabilize the tower.","Soil was extracted from beneath the tower's foundation to reduce its tilt.","The Leaning Tower of Pisa was built in the twelfth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly states that the lean was accidental, correcting the misconception named in the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman aqueducts carried water for miles using a continuous, gentle downhill slope, often elevated on stone arches.
• Maya water systems in cities like Palenque instead used covered, pressurized channels that could run water uphill over short distances.
• Both civilizations built their systems primarily to supply cities with drinking water and support agriculture.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a key engineering difference between the Roman and Maya water systems. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While Roman aqueducts relied on a continuous downhill slope, Maya systems used covered, pressurized channels that could move water uphill.","Roman aqueducts carried water for miles, often elevated on stone arches.","Maya water systems were used in cities such as Palenque.","Both civilizations built water systems to supply their cities with drinking water."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A directly contrasts the two engineering approaches, slope-based versus pressurized, as the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Most flying insects beat all of their wings using a shared set of muscles, limiting independent control.
• Dragonflies have separate muscles controlling each of their four wings individually.
• This independent control lets dragonflies hover, fly backward, and change direction abruptly while chasing prey.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how dragonflies achieve such precise aerial maneuverability. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because separate muscles control each of their four wings individually, dragonflies can hover, fly backward, and change direction abruptly.","Most flying insects beat all of their wings using a shared set of muscles.","Dragonflies have four wings, unlike many other flying insects.","Dragonflies often chase prey while in flight."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A ties the independent wing-muscle control to the specific maneuvers, explaining the mechanism the goal asks for.$q$),
  ($q$ei-401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Atacama Desert in Chile receives less than half an inch of rainfall per year, making it one of the driest places on Earth.
• Dry air contains little water vapor, which would otherwise absorb and distort incoming light and radio signals.
• Several major astronomical observatories, including large radio telescope arrays, have been built in the Atacama because of these conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why astronomers chose the Atacama Desert as a site for major observatories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the desert's extremely dry air contains little water vapor to distort incoming light and radio signals, astronomers have built major observatories there.","The Atacama Desert in Chile receives less than half an inch of rainfall per year.","The Atacama Desert is one of the driest places on Earth.","Several major astronomical observatories have been built in the Atacama Desert."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explains the causal link between dry air and clearer observations, matching the goal of explaining why astronomers chose the site.$q$),
  ($q$ei-402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many people assume that international internet traffic travels mainly through satellites.
• In reality, a vast network of fiber-optic cables laid along the ocean floor carries the large majority of international data.
• These submarine cables can transmit far more data, more quickly and cheaply, than satellite links can.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that satellites carry most international internet traffic. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Contrary to popular belief, a vast network of fiber-optic cables laid along the ocean floor, not satellites, carries the large majority of international internet traffic.","Submarine cables can transmit data more quickly and cheaply than satellite links can.","Many people assume that international internet traffic travels mainly through satellites.","Fiber-optic cables are laid along the ocean floor to connect continents."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly states the correct fact in contrast to the misconception, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Korean printers cast movable metal type and used it to print the Jikji, a Buddhist text, in 1377.
• Johannes Gutenberg's movable-type press in Europe is often credited as the first of its kind, beginning around 1440.
• The Korean system used individual characters for a script with thousands of characters, making large-scale printing more difficult than it was for the European alphabet.$q$, $q$The student wants to challenge the common assumption that Gutenberg's press was the first movable-type printing technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Korean printers were already using movable metal type to print the Jikji in 1377, more than sixty years before Gutenberg's press appeared in Europe.","Johannes Gutenberg's movable-type press is often credited as the first of its kind.","The Korean printing system had to account for a script with thousands of characters.","European printing relied on an alphabet with a comparatively small number of characters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific date showing Korean movable type predated Gutenberg's press, directly challenging the assumption named in the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before the Panama Canal opened in 1914, a ship traveling between New York and San Francisco had to sail around the southern tip of South America.
• That route added roughly 8,000 miles compared with crossing through the Panama Canal.
• The canal uses a system of locks to raise and lower ships between sea level and a man-made lake roughly 85 feet above sea level.$q$, $q$The student wants to quantify how much distance the Panama Canal saved on a specific shipping route. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By allowing ships to avoid sailing around South America, the canal shortened the New York-to-San Francisco route by roughly 8,000 miles.","The Panama Canal opened in 1914.","The canal uses a system of locks to raise and lower ships to a lake roughly 85 feet above sea level.","Ships traveling between the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans once had to sail around South America."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A states the specific route and the exact distance saved; the last choice describes the old route without the quantified comparison the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Carbon dating measures the decay of carbon-14, a radioactive isotope, to estimate the age of organic material.
• Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years, meaning half of it decays in that span.
• Because so little carbon-14 remains after roughly 50,000 years, the method becomes unreliable for dating objects older than that.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a specific limitation of carbon dating as a method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because so little carbon-14 remains after roughly 50,000 years, carbon dating becomes unreliable for objects older than that.","Carbon dating measures the decay of carbon-14 to estimate an object's age.","Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.","Because carbon-14 decays at a fixed, known rate, scientists can use it to estimate an object's age."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names the specific age threshold beyond which the method becomes unreliable, directly stating a limitation; the others explain how carbon dating works or cite a related fact without identifying where it breaks down.$q$),
  ($q$ei-406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1971, computer engineer Ray Tomlinson sent what is widely considered the first email between two separate computers on a network.
• Tomlinson later said he could not remember the exact content of the message, though he believed it was something unremarkable like a string of letters.
• The @ symbol Tomlinson chose to separate the username from the computer's name is still used in email addresses today.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the first email's content was unremarkable compared to its lasting significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tomlinson could not even recall the exact content of his 1971 message, likely an unremarkable string of letters, yet the system he built still underlies email today.","In 1971, Ray Tomlinson sent what is widely considered the first email between two computers.","Tomlinson chose the @ symbol to separate the username from the computer's name.","The @ symbol is still used in email addresses today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly pairs the forgettable content with the system's lasting significance, matching the specific contrast the goal asks for.$q$),
  ($q$ei-407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Leonardo da Vinci often blended colors and tones gradually, a technique called sfumato, to soften outlines and create a hazy depth.
• Sandro Botticelli, by contrast, favored crisp, clearly defined outlines and flat areas of color with little blending.
• Both painters worked in Florence during the Italian Renaissance and were influenced by some of the same patrons.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a specific stylistic difference between da Vinci's and Botticelli's painting techniques. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While da Vinci blended colors gradually to create a hazy depth, Botticelli favored crisp outlines and flat areas of color with little blending.","Leonardo da Vinci's technique of blending colors gradually is called sfumato.","Both painters worked in Florence during the Italian Renaissance.","Da Vinci and Botticelli were influenced by some of the same patrons."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A names both painters' contrasting techniques side by side, fulfilling the specific stylistic-contrast goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Gothic cathedrals were built with tall stone walls containing large stained-glass windows, which left less stone to bear the roof's weight.
• Flying buttresses are external stone arches that lean against the upper walls from outside the building.
• By transferring some of the roof's outward thrust to the ground through these external arches, flying buttresses allowed the walls themselves to be thinner and support larger windows.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the specific structural problem that flying buttresses solved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Flying buttresses transferred the roof's outward thrust to the ground, allowing walls weakened by large stained-glass windows to remain stable even though they were thinner.","Flying buttresses are external stone arches that lean against the upper walls from outside the building.","Gothic cathedrals were built with tall stone walls containing large stained-glass windows.","Flying buttresses transferred some of a roof's outward thrust to the ground."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A connects the buttresses' thrust-transferring function to the specific problem of walls weakened by large windows; C states the problem alone, D states the mechanism too generally, and B just describes what buttresses are.$q$),
  ($q$ei-409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dead Sea's water is roughly ten times saltier than ordinary ocean water, which is why almost no fish or plants can survive in it.
• The Dead Sea earned its name because of this near-total absence of aquatic life.
• Despite its name, the Dead Sea is a popular tourist destination, largely because its extreme salinity makes swimmers float with unusual ease.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight an ironic contrast between the Dead Sea's name and its appeal to tourists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite being named for its near-total absence of life, the Dead Sea draws tourists precisely because its extreme salinity lets them float with unusual ease.","The Dead Sea's water is roughly ten times saltier than ordinary ocean water.","Almost no fish or plants can survive in the Dead Sea.","The Dead Sea earned its name because of its near-total absence of aquatic life."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A explicitly juxtaposes the 'dead' name with the lively tourist appeal, capturing the irony the goal asks for.$q$),
  ($q$ei-410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Pantheon in Rome, completed around 128 CE, still has the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.
• Roman concrete included volcanic ash, which modern researchers have found reacts with seawater and rainwater to grow new mineral crystals that fill in tiny cracks over time.
• Modern concrete, by contrast, typically weakens and cracks further once water penetrates it.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Roman concrete structures like the Pantheon have survived for nearly two thousand years. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Volcanic ash in Roman concrete reacts with water to grow new mineral crystals that fill in cracks over time, unlike modern concrete, which tends to weaken as water penetrates it.","The Pantheon in Rome was completed around 128 CE.","The Pantheon still has the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.","Modern concrete typically weakens and cracks further once water penetrates it."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only choice A gives the specific self-healing mechanism, and contrasts it with modern concrete, explaining the longevity the goal asks about.$q$),
  ($q$ei-411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The hiking trail was closed after a landslide. ______ the park service posted detour signs for visitors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","For example,","Similarly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The signs are a consequence of the closure, so 'As a result' is logical.$q$),
  ($q$ei-412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Mia studied for weeks. ______ she earned the highest score in her class.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Meanwhile,","Nevertheless,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her high score follows from her studying, so a cause-effect transition fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dogs have been domesticated for thousands of years. ______ cats were domesticated much later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Therefore,","Likewise,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentences contrast the long history of dogs with the later history of cats.$q$),
  ($q$ei-414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Many birds migrate south in winter. ______ the Arctic tern travels from pole to pole each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Otherwise,","For example,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The Arctic tern is an example of a migrating bird.$q$),
  ($q$ei-415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The restaurant is famous for its desserts. ______ its lunch menu is also very popular.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","In contrast,","Instead,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence adds further praise, so an additive transition fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The team practiced in the rain all week. ______ they were ready for Saturday's game.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Rather,","Previously,","Alternatively,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Practice led to readiness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Solar panels have become much cheaper over the past decade. ______ the share of homes that use them has risen steadily.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","In other words,","Instead,","Regardless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lower cost plausibly explains the rise in adoption.$q$),
  ($q$ei-418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novel received mixed reviews on its release. ______ it has since become one of the most widely taught books in the country.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For example,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The later success contrasts with the mixed initial reception.$q$),
  ($q$ei-419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Most plants need sunlight to grow. ______ some fungi thrive in complete darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Similarly,","For instance,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Fungi that thrive in the dark contrast with plants needing sunlight.$q$),
  ($q$ei-420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Researchers suspected that the new medication would help patients sleep. ______ the trial showed no improvement over a placebo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Specifically,","However,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The trial result contradicted the expectation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city introduced a bike-sharing program last year. ______ it added 40 miles of protected bike lanes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Additionally,","Nevertheless,","Conversely,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding bike lanes is another step in the same direction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Octopuses can squeeze through tiny openings because they have no bones. ______ a large octopus can pass through a hole no bigger than its eye.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Rather,","Meanwhile,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence strengthens the first with a striking example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was designed to withstand strong winds. ______ it was not tested against earthquakes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Likewise,","However,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The wind design contrasts with the lack of earthquake testing.$q$),
  ($q$ei-424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Early telescopes produced blurry images of distant objects. ______ improvements in lens grinding eventually yielded much sharper views.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Instead,","Conversely,","Over time,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The improvement happened gradually.$q$),
  ($q$ei-425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some students prefer to study in silence. ______ many others concentrate better with background music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","For example,","By contrast,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentences contrast two preferences.$q$),
  ($q$ei-426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The study found no link between screen time and academic performance. ______ the authors caution that the study measured only total hours, not the content viewed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Similarly,","For this reason,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The caution qualifies the null finding, so a concessive transition fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's budget has shrunk by a third. ______ it has cut the number of concerts it offers rather than reducing the pay of its musicians.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","In contrast,","Even so,","To cope,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Cutting concerts is the orchestra's response to the budget cut.$q$),
  ($q$ei-428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics initially dismissed the painter as a mere imitator of her teacher. ______ her mature style, with its bold geometry, shows little trace of that influence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In sum,","As a result,","Similarly,","In truth,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the earlier dismissal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Salt lowers the freezing point of water, which is why it is spread on icy roads. ______ it becomes ineffective when temperatures fall far below zero.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise","Hence","Thus","Yet"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The limitation contrasts with the effectiveness just described.$q$),
  ($q$ei-430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The mayor promised to reduce commute times. ______ the plan she proposed, which adds only one express bus line, would likely affect a small share of commuters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Subsequently,","In practice,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contrasts the promise with the likely real-world effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum was crowded on Saturday. ______ on Tuesday it was nearly empty.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By comparison,","As a result,","In particular,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Saturday and Tuesday attendance are being contrasted.$q$),
  ($q$ei-432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Fewer than a dozen copies of the book survive. ______ it is considered one of the most valuable books in private hands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","Unsurprisingly,","On the other hand,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The scarcity logically explains the value.$q$),
  ($q$ei-433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$I forgot my umbrella. ______ I got soaked on the walk home.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Meanwhile,","As a result,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Getting wet is the consequence of forgetting the umbrella.$q$),
  ($q$ei-434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company offered employees a four-day work week. ______ productivity did not decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Notably,","Instead,","Otherwise,","Alternatively,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence adds a noteworthy result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Archaeologists had long assumed that the settlement was abandoned suddenly. ______ new radiocarbon dates suggest that residents left gradually over several decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","In addition,","Accordingly,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The new dates contradict the long-held assumption.$q$),
  ($q$ei-436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$First, whisk the eggs in a bowl. ______ add the milk and stir gently.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Otherwise,","Next,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentences describe sequential steps.$q$),
  ($q$ei-437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Satellite data show that the glacier has retreated about 2 kilometers since 1990. ______ the retreat has accelerated in the past decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Moreover,","Otherwise,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence adds further information in the same direction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author's early essays are widely anthologized. ______ her later work, which is more experimental, has received far less attention.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Similarly,","Specifically,","By comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two bodies of work are contrasted in how much attention they receive.$q$),
  ($q$ei-439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some reptiles can survive months without food. ______ the Galápagos tortoise can go a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Instead,","Alternatively,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tortoise's year is a more extreme example of the same capability.$q$),
  ($q$ei-440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Local farmers were worried about the drought. ______ they began planting crop varieties that need less water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Likewise,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Planting drought-tolerant crops is a response to the worry.$q$),
  ($q$ei-441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A bridge may appear to be under little stress when it carries no traffic. ______ the weight of the structure itself places enormous loads on its cables.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For instance,","Likewise,","In reality,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the appearance described in the first.$q$),
  ($q$ei-442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The red panda is a small mammal native to the Himalayas.
• Its diet consists mainly of bamboo.
• It spends most of its time in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce the red panda to readers unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Red pandas spend time in trees, which are tall plants.","The red panda, a small mammal native to the Himalayas, spends most of its time in trees and eats mainly bamboo.","The red panda eats bamboo.","Many mammals live in the Himalayas and eat plants."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice introduces the animal with its location, habitat, and diet.$q$),
  ($q$ei-443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China stretches over 13,000 miles.
• Construction began more than 2,000 years ago.
• Parts of it were built to repel invaders.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the age of the Great Wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Parts of the Great Wall were built to repel invaders.","The Great Wall of China stretches over 13,000 miles.","Construction of the Great Wall of China began more than 2,000 years ago.","The Great Wall is in China."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice highlights how old the wall is.$q$),
  ($q$ei-444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Nile is about 4,130 miles long.
• The Amazon's length is estimated at about 4,000 miles.
• Measuring a river's length depends on where one places its source.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why rankings of the world's longest river are disputed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Nile is about 4,130 miles long.","The Amazon is nearly as long as the Nile.","Because a river's length depends on where its source is placed, the Nile and the Amazon, at roughly 4,130 and 4,000 miles, are both claimed as the longest.","Rivers can be measured."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice links the measurement ambiguity to the dispute.$q$),
  ($q$ei-445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Jane Goodall began studying chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.
• She observed chimpanzees making and using tools.
• Her discovery challenged the belief that only humans use tools.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the significance of Goodall's discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Goodall began her work in Tanzania in 1960.","Goodall studied chimpanzees for many years.","Chimpanzees live in Tanzania.","By observing chimpanzees make and use tools, Goodall overturned the belief that only humans do so."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice states the discovery and its importance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the world's oceans.
• Its deepest point is nearly 36,000 feet below the surface.
• Mount Everest is about 29,000 feet tall.$q$, $q$The student wants to make a comparison that conveys the trench's depth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Mariana Trench's deepest point, nearly 36,000 feet below the surface, lies farther down than Mount Everest, at about 29,000 feet, rises up.","Mount Everest is about 29,000 feet tall.","The ocean is deep.","The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice makes the comparison using both numbers.$q$),
  ($q$ei-447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Researchers studied 200 households that installed smart thermostats.
• The households reduced heating costs by an average of 8%.
• Savings were largest in homes with irregular occupancy schedules.
• The study lasted one winter.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a finding of the study and a limit on it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The study lasted one winter and involved heating.","Smart thermostats reduced costs in homes with irregular occupancy.","Researchers studied 200 households.","Households with smart thermostats cut heating costs by an average of 8%, although the study covered only a single winter."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice presents the main result and a limitation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies travel up to 3,000 miles during migration.
• No single butterfly makes the entire round trip.
• It takes several generations to complete the cycle.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the monarch migration is remarkable. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Generations of butterflies live in many places.","Monarchs travel up to 3,000 miles.","Because no single monarch completes the round trip, the species' 3,000-mile migration is carried out across several generations.","Monarch butterflies are insects."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice explains what makes the migration remarkable.$q$),
  ($q$ei-449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The violin has four strings.
• It is tuned in perfect fifths.
• It is played with a bow.$q$, $q$The student wants to state how the violin is played. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The violin has four strings.","The violin is tuned in perfect fifths.","The violin is played with a bow.","The violin is an instrument."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice states how it is played.$q$),
  ($q$ei-450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor.
• They support about 25% of marine species.
• Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that protecting reefs is disproportionately valuable. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor, they support about 25% of marine species, so protecting them benefits a vast share of ocean life.","About 25% of marine species live in the ocean.","Coral reefs cover very little of the ocean floor.","Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice contrasts the reefs' small area with their outsized role.$q$),
  ($q$ei-451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Sojourner Truth was born into slavery in New York around 1797.
• She escaped in 1826.
• In 1851 she delivered a famous speech on women's rights in Akron, Ohio.$q$, $q$The student wants to trace her path from slavery to public advocacy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Born into slavery around 1797, Sojourner Truth escaped in 1826 and later became a leading speaker for women's rights.","Sojourner Truth was born in New York.","Sojourner Truth gave a speech in Akron.","Sojourner Truth escaped in 1826 and gave speeches."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice covers the progression from slavery through escape to advocacy.$q$),
  ($q$ei-452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Wind turbines generate no emissions during operation.
• Manufacturing turbines requires energy and materials.
• A turbine typically offsets the emissions from its manufacture within a year.$q$, $q$The student wants to acknowledge a drawback of wind turbines while emphasizing their environmental benefit. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Wind turbines generate no emissions.","Manufacturing turbines requires energy and materials.","A turbine can offset emissions.","Although manufacturing a wind turbine requires energy and materials, a typical turbine offsets those emissions within a year and then operates without emissions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice includes both the drawback and the offsetting benefit.$q$),
  ($q$ei-453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Library of Congress holds more than 170 million items.
• It was established in 1800.
• It is the largest library in the world by collection size.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the size of the Library of Congress. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Library of Congress was established in 1800.","Libraries hold books and other items.","The Library of Congress is in the United States.","The Library of Congress, which holds more than 170 million items, is the largest library in the world by collection size."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice emphasizes its size.$q$),
  ($q$ei-454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Octopuses have eight arms.
• They can change color to match their surroundings.
• They are found in all the world's oceans.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how octopuses blend in. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Octopuses are found in all oceans.","Octopuses can change color to match their surroundings.","Octopuses have eight arms.","Octopuses live in the sea."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice describes camouflage.$q$),
  ($q$ei-455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• In 2019, the town replaced its streetlights with LED lamps.
• The change cut the town's lighting energy use by 60%.
• The new lamps also cost more up front than the old ones.
• The upfront cost was repaid through energy savings within four years.$q$, $q$The student wants to persuade readers that the LED switch was a sound investment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The new LED lamps cost more up front.","LED lamps use less energy than old lamps.","The town replaced its streetlights in 2019.","Although the LED lamps cost more up front, the 60% reduction in the town's lighting energy use repaid that cost within four years."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice acknowledges the cost and shows it was recovered through savings.$q$),
  ($q$ei-456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter.
• Males incubate a single egg on their feet for about two months.
• During this time the males do not eat.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the males' endurance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Emperor penguins live in Antarctica.","During the Antarctic winter, male emperor penguins incubate an egg on their feet for about two months without eating.","Penguins have eggs.","Emperor penguins breed once a year."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice stresses the long, fasting incubation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• A 2020 survey found that 62% of commuters in the city drive alone.
• The city's transit agency wants to raise ridership.
• Surveyed drivers most often cited unreliable bus schedules as their reason.$q$, $q$The student wants to recommend a course of action to the transit agency. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because most drivers cited unreliable schedules, the transit agency could raise ridership by making its bus service more reliable.","62% of commuters drive alone.","Buses can be unreliable.","The city's transit agency wants to raise ridership."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice recommends an action grounded in the survey's finding.$q$),
  ($q$ei-458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if it is sealed.
• Archaeologists found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.
• Honey is made by bees.$q$, $q$The student wants to give an example of honey's long shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Honey never spoils.","Honey is sweet.","Archaeologists have found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.","Honey is made by bees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice gives a specific example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The printing press was invented around 1440 by Johannes Gutenberg.
• Before it, books were copied by hand.
• By 1500, millions of printed books had been produced across Europe.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the press's effect on book production. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Books were copied by hand.","Gutenberg invented the printing press.","The press was invented around 1440.","After Gutenberg's invention of the printing press around 1440, millions of books were printed across Europe by 1500, replacing laborious copying by hand."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice combines the before-and-after effect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Regular exercise lowers the risk of heart disease.
• A study of 5,000 adults found that those who walked 30 minutes a day had a 20% lower risk.
• The study did not include adults over 65.$q$, $q$The student wants to report the study's result while noting its limited scope. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In a study of 5,000 adults, walking 30 minutes a day was linked to a 20% lower risk of heart disease, although the study did not include adults over 65.","Adults who walk are healthier.","Exercise lowers risk of heart disease.","The study of 5,000 adults did not include older people."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice states the result and the limitation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Frida Kahlo painted 55 self-portraits.
• She said she painted herself because she was often alone.
• Many of the portraits show her pain from a bus accident.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain her reason for painting self-portraits. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kahlo was an artist.","Kahlo, who painted 55 self-portraits, explained that she painted herself because she was often alone.","Kahlo was in a bus accident.","Kahlo painted self-portraits of her pain."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice states her stated reason.$q$),
  ($q$ei-462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.
• It covers about 3.6 million square miles.
• It spans parts of eleven countries.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the Sahara's size. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Sahara is hot.","The Sahara spans eleven countries.","The Sahara covers about 3.6 million square miles.","The Sahara is a desert."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice gives the size.$q$),
  ($q$ei-463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Dr. Aliyu studies how bilingual children switch between languages.
• Her team found that bilingual children outperform monolingual peers on tasks requiring attention control.
• The researchers suggest that frequent language switching trains this ability.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a proposed reason for the observed advantage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Dr. Aliyu's team suggests that bilingual children outperform monolingual peers on attention-control tasks because frequent language switching trains this ability.","Bilingual children outperform monolingual peers.","Dr. Aliyu studies bilingual children.","Switching languages is common."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice pairs the finding with the proposed explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
