  ($q$ei-544$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The study found no link between screen time and test scores. ______ the researchers cautioned that the sample was small.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","For instance,","Even so,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The caution tempers the finding; 'Even so' signals the qualification.$q$),
  ($q$ei-545$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author's first novel sold poorly. ______ her second became an international bestseller.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","In other words,","For example,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second novel's success contrasts with the first's failure.$q$),
  ($q$ei-546$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Honeybees communicate the location of flowers through a dance. ______ the angle of the dance indicates the direction relative to the sun.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence gives specific detail about the dance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-547$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company cut its prices by twenty percent. ______ sales rose by nearly a third.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Meanwhile,","As a result,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The rise in sales is a consequence of the price cut.$q$),
  ($q$ei-548$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some historians argue the war was inevitable. ______ others contend that diplomacy could have prevented it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","For example,","Similarly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two views are in opposition.$q$),
  ($q$ei-549$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee reviewed the proposal carefully. ______ it recommended approval with minor changes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Ultimately,","Conversely,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'Ultimately' marks the final outcome of the review.$q$),
  ($q$ei-550$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The film received mixed reviews from critics. ______ it was a hit with audiences, earning over a hundred million dollars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","Nevertheless,","Therefore,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Audience success contrasts with the critics' mixed reviews.$q$),
  ($q$ei-551$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Volcanic soil is rich in minerals. ______ regions near volcanoes often support intensive agriculture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","In contrast,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Farming prospers as a result of mineral-rich soil.$q$),
  ($q$ei-552$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dr. Alvarez published her results in March. ______ three other labs reported similar findings by June.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Instead,","Subsequently,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The later reports followed in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-553$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The first experiment failed to produce the expected reaction. ______ the team adjusted the temperature and tried again.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","For example,","Next,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Next' signals the next step in the sequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-554$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The artist rarely sold her paintings during her lifetime. ______ they now sell for millions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","Therefore,","Today,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Today' marks the shift from then to now.$q$),
  ($q$ei-555$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Most plants need sunlight to grow. ______ certain fungi thrive in complete darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Therefore,","Similarly,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Fungi differ from plants in this respect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-556$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The chef praised the dish's flavor. ______ she suggested reducing the salt slightly.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Previously,","Likewise,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The suggestion slightly qualifies the praise.$q$),
  ($q$ei-557$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Renewable energy has grown quickly in recent years. ______ it still accounts for a minority of global electricity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Nevertheless,","Therefore,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The fact that it is still a minority contrasts with the rapid growth.$q$),
  ($q$ei-558$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The treaty was widely celebrated at its signing. ______ within a decade, both nations had violated its central provisions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Yet","Moreover","Likewise"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The violations contrast sharply with the celebration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-559$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The researchers expected the drug to lower blood pressure. ______ patients who took it showed no significant change.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Instead,","Specifically,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Instead signals the unexpected result replaced the expected one.$q$),
  ($q$ei-560$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Many assume that glass is a slow-moving liquid. ______ glass is an amorphous solid, and old windows are thicker at the bottom because of how they were made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Subsequently,","In fact,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'In fact' introduces a correction of the common assumption.$q$),
  ($q$ei-561$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The mayor promised to reduce traffic. ______ she funded a new commuter rail line and expanded bus service.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Otherwise,","To that end,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The actions were taken in service of the promise.$q$),
  ($q$ei-562$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novel's first half is a conventional detective story. ______ its second half abandons mystery for philosophical reflection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","For instance,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two halves differ in genre.$q$),
  ($q$ei-563$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The hypothesis predicted that warmer temperatures would increase growth. ______ the data showed a decline above 30 degrees Celsius.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Similarly,","However,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The data contradicted part of the prediction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-564$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company's earlier products were largely unremarkable. ______ its latest device has redefined the market.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Therefore,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The latest device is contrasted with the earlier products.$q$),
  ($q$ei-565$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The wetlands absorb floodwater and filter pollutants. ______ they provide habitat for hundreds of species.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","Instead,","Furthermore,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Habitat is another benefit added to those already listed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-566$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee had planned to vote in June. ______ new evidence emerged, and the vote was postponed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","For example,","Then,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Then' signals a later development that changed the plan.$q$),
  ($q$ei-567$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Early critics dismissed the composer's work as noise. ______ she is now regarded as one of the most influential figures of her era.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","Today,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The present regard contrasts with early dismissal; 'Today' marks the shift in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-568$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Social insects such as ants divide labor among specialized castes. ______ a single colony may include foragers, nurses, and soldiers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Meanwhile,","For instance,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence offers concrete examples of specialization.$q$),
  ($q$ei-569$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author concedes that her sample was small. ______ she argues that the consistency of the results justifies cautious optimism.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Previously,","Nonetheless,","Therefore,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$She admits a weakness but maintains her conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-570$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was designed to carry only light traffic. ______ it now supports thousands of heavy trucks each day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Nevertheless,","Likewise,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Heavy use contrasts with the original design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-571$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The recipe calls for butter. ______ vegans can substitute olive oil without altering the flavor much.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Alternatively,","As a result,","Previously,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Olive oil is offered as an alternative.$q$),
  ($q$ei-572$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Tom wanted to join the swim team. ______ he practiced every morning before school.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However","So","Instead","Otherwise"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Practicing is the natural result of his wanting to join.$q$),
  ($q$ei-573$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The movie started at seven. ______ we arrived ten minutes early to find seats.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Nevertheless,","Therefore,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Arriving early follows from the start time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-574$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Rainforests contain more than half of the world's plant and animal species. ______ they cover only a small part of its land area.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Likewise","Yet","Therefore"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The small land area contrasts with the large share of species.$q$),
  ($q$ei-575$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The study participants were asked to sleep only five hours a night. ______ their reaction times slowed noticeably after a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Alternatively,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slower reaction times were an effect of reduced sleep.$q$),
  ($q$ei-576$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city has invested heavily in public transit. ______ car ownership has begun to decline among young residents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Alternatively,","Nevertheless,","Previously,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The decline in car ownership is linked to the transit investment.$q$),
  ($q$ei-577$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some economists argue that automation will destroy jobs. ______ others point out that previous technological revolutions created new categories of work.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Likewise,","However,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second claim opposes the first.$q$),
  ($q$ei-578$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The first draft of the law was widely criticized as too vague. ______ the revised version includes precise definitions of each key term.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Alternatively,","Nevertheless,","By contrast,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The revision responds to the criticism, so 'Accordingly' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-579$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Ocean currents transport heat from the equator toward the poles. ______ without them, the climate of northern Europe would be far colder.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","In contrast,","Indeed,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Indeed' reinforces the importance described in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-580$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$At first, the villagers resisted the new road. ______ they came to appreciate how it connected them to nearby markets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","In contrast,","Meanwhile,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Eventually' marks the change in attitude over time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-581$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A lawyer must keep client information private. ______ there are rare exceptions, such as threats of serious harm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That said,","For example,","Likewise,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The exceptions qualify the rule.$q$),
  ($q$ei-582$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The soup was too hot to eat. ______ Ben waited a few minutes for it to cool.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead","However","Yet","So"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Waiting is a response to the soup being too hot.$q$),
  ($q$ei-583$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dolphins are mammals. ______ they breathe air and nurse their young.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Otherwise,","In contrast,","Specifically,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence gives specifics about being mammals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-584$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The first version of the software crashed frequently. ______ the update fixed most of the stability problems.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Likewise,","For instance,","Fortunately,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The update is a positive development after the problems.$q$),
  ($q$ei-585$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum is free on Sundays. ______ it draws its largest crowds then, often exceeding the building's capacity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Alternatively,","Predictably,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Large crowds are an expected consequence of free admission.$q$),
  ($q$ei-586$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The original manuscript was lost in a fire. ______ scholars rely on a single surviving copy made by a monk centuries later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Conversely,","Likewise,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reliance on a copy results from the loss of the original.$q$),
  ($q$ei-587$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Hummingbirds are small. ______ they are among the fastest animals, relative to body size, on Earth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet","Specifically","Thus","Likewise"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Their speed contrasts with their small size.$q$),
  ($q$ei-588$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Bamboo grows faster than almost any other plant. ______ it is considered a highly renewable material.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Previously,","In contrast,","Consequently,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Renewability follows from rapid growth.$q$),
  ($q$ei-589$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Okavango Delta is in Botswana.
• It is formed when a river flows into a desert.
• It supports large populations of elephants and hippos.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the delta's role in supporting wildlife. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Formed where a river flows into a desert, Botswana's Okavango Delta supports large populations of elephants and hippos.","Elephants and hippos are large animals.","The Okavango Delta is in Botswana.","Rivers sometimes flow into deserts."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This choice combines the delta's origin with its wildlife, directly meeting the goal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-590$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bats are the only mammals capable of true flight.
• Some bats can eat thousands of insects in a single night.
• This helps control pest populations.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why bats are beneficial to farmers. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Bats are the only mammals capable of true flight.","Many bats live in caves.","Some bats are very small.","Because some bats eat thousands of insects in a single night, they help control pest populations that threaten crops."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This choice connects insect-eating to pest control.$q$),
  ($q$ei-591$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece.
• They took place in Olympia every four years.
• Winners received olive wreaths.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how winners were honored. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["At the ancient Olympic Games, held every four years in Olympia, winners received olive wreaths.","Olympia was a place in Greece.","Games were held every four years.","The first Olympic Games were held in Greece."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice mentions the prize in context.$q$),
  ($q$ei-592$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Marie Tharp was an American geologist.
• In the 1950s she mapped the ocean floor.
• Her maps revealed a vast underwater mountain range.
• Many scientists initially doubted her interpretation of the data.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the obstacles Tharp faced. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["In the 1950s, Tharp mapped the ocean floor.","Marie Tharp was an American geologist.","Tharp's maps showed mountains.","Although Marie Tharp's maps of the ocean floor revealed a vast underwater mountain range, many scientists initially doubted her interpretation of the data."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This choice highlights the doubt she met despite her discovery.$q$),
  ($q$ei-593$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Kelp forests grow along cold coastlines.
• They provide shelter for fish and invertebrates.
• They also absorb carbon dioxide from the water.
• Many kelp forests have shrunk because of warming seas.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the benefits of kelp forests. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Many kelp forests have shrunk because of warming seas.","Kelp forests shelter fish and invertebrates and absorb carbon dioxide from the water.","Kelp forests grow along cold coastlines.","Kelp is a type of seaweed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$This choice states both benefits directly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-594$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The printing press was invented around 1440.
• It allowed books to be produced far faster than copying by hand.
• The price of books fell sharply.
• Literacy rates rose over the following centuries.$q$, $q$The student wants to show a chain of effects of the printing press. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Literacy is the ability to read.","The printing press was invented around 1440.","By allowing books to be made far faster than hand copying, the printing press, invented around 1440, lowered book prices and helped raise literacy over the following centuries.","Books were copied by hand before then."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice links invention to price drop to literacy.$q$),
  ($q$ei-595$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood through the gills.
• The third pumps blood to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the function of the octopus's hearts. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Octopuses are unusual animals.","Blood travels through the gills.","Octopuses have three hearts.","Of an octopus's three hearts, two pump blood through the gills and the third pumps it to the rest of the body."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice divides the hearts' functions clearly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-596$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.
• Different dynasties built and extended its sections.
• Some sections are made of stone; others are rammed earth.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the wall is not uniform. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Many dynasties ruled China.","Earth can be used for building.","Built and extended by different dynasties over many centuries, the Great Wall varies from section to section, some of stone and others of rammed earth.","The Great Wall of China is very long."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice emphasizes variation by linking different builders and materials.$q$),
  ($q$ei-597$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity.
• Their efficiency has improved steadily since the 1970s.
• Costs have dropped by about ninety percent in the last decade.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how affordable solar panels have become. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The cost of solar panels, which convert sunlight into electricity, has dropped by about ninety percent in the last decade.","Sunlight is a renewable resource.","Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity.","Efficiency has improved since the 1970s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice highlights the cost reduction as the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-598$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Dr. Rosalind Franklin was a chemist.
• Her X-ray images of DNA, taken in the early 1950s, revealed its helical structure.
• Her work was used by Watson and Crick without her full knowledge.
• She died in 1958, before the Nobel Prize for the discovery was awarded.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the lack of recognition Franklin received during her lifetime. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Rosalind Franklin was a chemist.","Although Franklin's X-ray images of DNA revealed its helical structure, her work was used without her full knowledge, and she died before the discovery earned a Nobel Prize.","Franklin took X-ray images of DNA in the early 1950s.","Watson and Crick won the Nobel Prize."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice combines her contribution with the missing credit.$q$),
  ($q$ei-599$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Axolotls are a type of salamander native to Mexico.
• They can regenerate lost limbs, organs, and parts of the brain.
• Scientists study them to understand tissue repair in humans.
• Wild populations are critically endangered.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why scientists study axolotls. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Wild axolotl populations are critically endangered.","Axolotls are native to Mexico.","Because axolotls can regenerate limbs, organs, and even parts of the brain, scientists study them to understand tissue repair in humans.","Axolotls are a type of salamander."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice links the ability to the research motivation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-600$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Silk Road was a network of trade routes linking East Asia and Europe.
• Merchants traded silk, spices, and paper along it.
• Ideas and religions also spread along its paths.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that the Silk Road's importance went beyond commerce. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Silk was valuable.","The Silk Road, a network of routes linking East Asia and Europe, carried not only silk, spices, and paper but also ideas and religions.","The Silk Road was a network of trade routes.","Merchants traded silk along it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice contrasts goods with ideas and religions to show breadth.$q$),
  ($q$ei-601$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers analyzed tree rings from ancient oaks.
• The rings show narrow growth during years of drought.
• Narrow rings cluster around 1100, matching records of famine.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the tree rings support historical records. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Famines occurred in the past.","Oaks can live for centuries.","Researchers analyzed tree rings.","Clusters of narrow tree rings around 1100, which indicate drought, match historical records of famine in the same period."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties the evidence to the historical records.$q$),
  ($q$ei-602$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The red panda lives in the Himalayas.
• It eats mainly bamboo.
• It spends most of its time in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe where the red panda spends its time. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The red panda eats bamboo.","Bamboo is a plant.","The red panda lives in the Himalayas.","The red panda, which eats mainly bamboo, spends most of its time in the trees of the Himalayas."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice answers the goal about location and habits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-603$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the world's oceans.
• Its deepest point, Challenger Deep, is nearly 11,000 meters below the surface.
• Only a few crewed expeditions have reached it.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how rarely the trench has been visited. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Challenger Deep is nearly 11,000 meters deep.","The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the oceans.","The ocean covers most of the planet.","Though Challenger Deep lies nearly 11,000 meters below the surface in the Mariana Trench, only a few crewed expeditions have ever reached it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice highlights how few expeditions have gone there.$q$),
  ($q$ei-604$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Maple syrup is made from the sap of maple trees.
• Sap is collected in late winter.
• About forty gallons of sap yield one gallon of syrup.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much sap is needed to make syrup. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Maple syrup is sweet.","Collected in late winter, maple sap must be boiled down at a ratio of about forty gallons to every gallon of syrup.","Maple trees grow in North America.","Sap is collected in late winter."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice emphasizes the ratio, as the goal requires.$q$),
  ($q$ei-605$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had killed bacteria in a dish.
• He identified the mold as Penicillium.
• The drug was not mass-produced until the 1940s.
• Penicillin has since saved millions of lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the gap between the discovery and its impact. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Fleming noticed in 1928 that Penicillium mold killed bacteria, but penicillin was not mass-produced until the 1940s, after which it saved millions of lives.","Mold can grow in dishes.","Penicillin saved millions of lives.","Fleming identified Penicillium."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice sets the 1928 discovery against the 1940s production and later impact.$q$),
  ($q$ei-606$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The monarch butterfly migrates up to 3,000 miles.
• It relies on milkweed as the only food for its caterpillars.
• Milkweed has declined across the Midwest due to herbicide use.
• Monarch numbers have fallen sharply.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a cause of the monarch's decline. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Milkweed grows in the Midwest.","Butterflies are insects.","Because herbicide use has reduced the milkweed on which monarch caterpillars depend, monarch numbers have fallen sharply.","The monarch migrates up to 3,000 miles."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice states the causal chain from herbicide to milkweed to monarchs.$q$),
  ($q$ei-607$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bees collect nectar from flowers.
• They turn it into honey.
• Honey is stored in the hive.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how honey is made. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Bees are insects.","Flowers produce nectar.","Bees collect nectar from flowers, turn it into honey, and store it in the hive.","Honey is sweet."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice describes the process in order.$q$),
  ($q$ei-608$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jazz began in New Orleans in the early twentieth century.
• It blended African rhythms with European harmonies.
• Improvisation became its defining feature.$q$, $q$The student wants to define jazz's origin and character. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["New Orleans is in Louisiana.","Jazz, which began in early-twentieth-century New Orleans by blending African rhythms with European harmonies, is defined by improvisation.","Jazz is popular worldwide.","Improvisation is spontaneous music."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice covers origin, blend, and defining feature.$q$),
  ($q$ei-609$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor.
• They support about a quarter of all marine species.
• Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching.
• Bleached corals often die.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the potential consequences of coral loss. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Coral reefs cover little of the ocean floor.","Because coral reefs, which cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, support about a quarter of marine species, the death of bleached corals threatens a disproportionate share of ocean life.","Rising temperatures cause bleaching.","Corals are animals."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice connects the reefs' small area to their outsized importance and the threat.$q$),
  ($q$ei-610$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was a major center of learning in the ancient world.
• It collected scrolls from across the Mediterranean.
• Scholars came to study there.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the library attracted scholars. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Because the Library of Alexandria collected scrolls from across the Mediterranean, scholars came to study at this major center of learning.","Scrolls were made of papyrus.","The library was in Egypt.","Alexandria was a city."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice links the collection to the scholars.$q$),
  ($q$ei-611$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Penguins are flightless birds.
• Their wings have evolved into flippers.
• This adaptation allows them to swim at speeds up to 15 miles per hour.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how flightlessness benefits penguins. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Flippers are not wings.","Penguins are birds.","Penguins live in cold places.","Although penguins cannot fly, their wings have evolved into flippers that let them swim at up to 15 miles per hour."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice shows the trade-off turning into an advantage.$q$),
  ($q$ei-612$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The park offers free yoga classes on Saturdays. ______ the classes fill up quickly, so arriving early is recommended.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Likewise,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The classes are free but crowded, so 'However' introduces the caveat.$q$),
  ($q$ei-613$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Studying abroad exposes students to new languages and customs. ______ many return with stronger independence and problem-solving skills.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Otherwise,","In addition,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Independence and problem-solving are further benefits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-614$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The lake looked calm from the shore. ______ beneath the surface, strong currents swirled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","In reality,","Similarly,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence contrasts appearance with reality.$q$),
  ($q$ei-615$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Initial reports suggested the vaccine was safe for all ages. ______ later trials found that certain groups needed lower doses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","However,","Therefore,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The later findings qualify the early claim.$q$),
  ($q$ei-616$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author describes the village in loving detail. ______ she never mentions the poverty that shaped daily life there.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Strikingly,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The omission is surprising given the detail, so 'Strikingly' fits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-617$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The device was designed for surgeons. ______ engineers soon realized it could also assist in deep-sea repairs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","Eventually,","Instead,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The new use was realized over time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-618$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Giraffes are the tallest land animals.
• Adult males can reach 18 feet.
• Their long necks help them reach leaves high in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how giraffes' height helps them eat. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Giraffes are tall.","Giraffes, which can reach 18 feet, use their long necks to reach leaves high in trees.","Trees have leaves.","Adult male giraffes are heavy."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice links height to feeding.$q$),
  ($q$ei-619$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tomatoes are botanically fruits.
• They are usually used in savory dishes.
• Many people think of them as vegetables.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why tomatoes cause confusion. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Tomatoes grow on vines.","Savory dishes are common.","Although tomatoes are botanically fruits, they are usually used in savory dishes, so many people think of them as vegetables.","Tomatoes are red."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice explains the source of confusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-620$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elephants live in herds led by an older female.
• The leader remembers locations of water.
• Herds follow her during droughts.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the herd leader's role. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Elephants live in herds.","An older female leads each elephant herd, remembering where to find water and guiding the herd during droughts.","Droughts are dry periods.","Female elephants are large."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice describes the leader's role directly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-621$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dust Bowl was a period of severe dust storms in the 1930s.
• It affected the Great Plains.
• Poor farming practices and drought caused it.
• Thousands of families left their farms.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the causes and consequences of the Dust Bowl. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The Dust Bowl was in the 1930s.","The Great Plains is a region.","Dust storms are windy.","Caused by poor farming practices and drought, the dust storms of the 1930s Dust Bowl drove thousands of Great Plains families from their farms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice states both cause and consequence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-622$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest is 29,032 feet tall.
• Climbers attempt it each spring.
• Altitude sickness is a serious risk.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the danger of climbing Everest. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Climbers who attempt Everest's 29,032 feet each spring face the serious risk of altitude sickness.","Spring is a season.","Climbers like challenges.","Everest is tall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice stresses the risk the climbers face.$q$),
  ($q$ei-623$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone was found in 1799.
• It bears the same text in three scripts.
• Scholars used it to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Rosetta Stone is significant. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The stone has writing on it.","Egypt has many monuments.","The Rosetta Stone was found in 1799.","Because the Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, bears the same text in three scripts, scholars could use it to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties the three scripts to decipherment.$q$),
  ($q$ei-624$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Compost is made from decaying organic matter.
• It enriches soil with nutrients.
• It also reduces the amount of waste sent to landfills.$q$, $q$The student wants to present two benefits of composting. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Landfills are large.","Composting, which turns decaying organic matter into material that enriches soil, also reduces the waste sent to landfills.","Compost is made at home.","Soil contains nutrients."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice presents both benefits.$q$),
  ($q$ei-625$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers flew their first powered airplane in 1903.
• The flight lasted twelve seconds.
• It covered 120 feet.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how modest the first flight was. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Airplanes have wings.","The Wright brothers' first powered flight in 1903 lasted just twelve seconds and covered only 120 feet.","The Wright brothers built airplanes.","Flight changed the world."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice highlights the small scale with 'just' and 'only.'$q$),
  ($q$ei-626$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Lightning heats the air around it to about 50,000 degrees Fahrenheit.
• This is hotter than the surface of the sun.
• The rapid heating produces thunder.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what causes thunder. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Lightning heats the surrounding air to about 50,000 degrees Fahrenheit, and the rapid heating produces thunder.","Lightning is bright.","Thunder is loud.","The sun is hot."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice explains the cause of thunder directly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-627$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Greenland sharks can live for more than 250 years.
• They grow only about one centimeter per year.
• Scientists estimate age by measuring eye lens proteins.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how scientists know the sharks' age. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Greenland sharks live in cold water.","Greenland is an island.","Because Greenland sharks grow only about a centimeter a year, scientists estimate their age, which can exceed 250 years, by measuring proteins in their eye lenses.","Sharks have lenses in their eyes."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice explains the method for age estimation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-628$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sleep helps the brain consolidate memories.
• Students who sleep well after studying perform better on tests.
• Many students stay up late to study.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that studying late may be counterproductive. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Tests measure learning.","Because sleep helps the brain consolidate memories, students who stay up late to study may perform worse than those who sleep well afterward.","Students study in the evening.","Sleep is important."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice draws the implication from the evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-629$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1912, the Titanic sank on its maiden voyage.
• Investigators later found that it carried too few lifeboats.
• Regulations then required lifeboats for every passenger.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the disaster changed regulations. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Lifeboats are small boats.","The Titanic sank on its maiden voyage.","Investigators look into accidents.","After investigators found that the Titanic, which sank in 1912, carried too few lifeboats, regulations were changed to require lifeboats for every passenger."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice links the investigation's finding to the rule change.$q$),
  ($q$ei-630$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus is the hottest planet in the solar system.
• Its atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide.
• The thick atmosphere traps heat.
• Surface temperatures reach about 900 degrees Fahrenheit.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Venus is hotter than Mercury despite being farther from the sun. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Venus, though farther from the sun than Mercury, is hotter because its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat, pushing surface temperatures to about 900 degrees Fahrenheit.","Venus is the hottest planet.","Mercury is small.","Venus has an atmosphere."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice gives the cause of the difference.$q$),
  ($q$ei-631$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers surveyed 2,000 commuters.
• Those who cycled to work reported lower stress levels.
• The researchers note that the survey does not prove cycling causes lower stress.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the finding with an appropriate caveat. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Researchers surveyed commuters.","In a survey of 2,000 commuters, cyclists reported lower stress levels, though the researchers caution that the survey does not prove cycling is the cause.","Stress is common.","Cycling is good exercise."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice states the finding and the caveat.$q$),
  ($q$ei-632$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon rainforest produces a significant share of the world's oxygen.
• Recent research shows that the forest consumes nearly as much oxygen as it produces.
• Its main value is in storing carbon.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Forests store carbon.","Although the Amazon is often credited with supplying much of the world's oxygen, research shows it consumes nearly as much as it produces; its main value lies in storing carbon.","The Amazon is large.","Oxygen is a gas."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice states the misconception and corrects it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-633$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mozart composed his first symphony at age eight.
• He wrote more than 600 works before he died at 35.
• His music is still widely performed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how productive Mozart was. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Mozart's music is still widely performed.","Symphonies are long.","Beginning with a symphony at age eight, Mozart wrote more than 600 works before dying at 35.","Mozart died at 35."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice highlights output in a short life.$q$),
  ($q$ei-634$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Seeds can travel by wind, water, or animals.
• Dandelion seeds have light, feathery parachutes.
• These parachutes carry them long distances on the wind.$q$, $q$The student wants to give an example of wind dispersal. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Seeds can travel by water.","Dandelion seeds, which have light, feathery parachutes, are carried long distances on the wind.","Wind is moving air.","Dandelions are weeds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice gives a specific example of wind dispersal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-635$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The city built a new bike lane downtown.
• Bike commuters increased by 25 percent in six months.
• Car traffic on the same street did not change.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the lane's effect on cycling. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The city built a lane.","Bikes are popular.","Car traffic did not change.","Six months after the city built a downtown bike lane, the number of bike commuters rose 25 percent."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice focuses on the cycling increase.$q$),
  ($q$ei-636$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Penguins huddle together in winter.
• The huddle keeps them warm.
• They take turns standing at the cold edge.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how huddling works. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Penguins stay warm in winter by huddling together and taking turns at the cold edge.","Penguins are birds.","Winter is cold.","Penguins live in Antarctica."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice describes the practice and its purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-637$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the late 1700s.
• Factories drew workers from the countryside to cities.
• Urban populations grew rapidly.
• Housing and sanitation lagged behind.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the problems caused by urban growth. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The Industrial Revolution began in Britain.","Cities have people.","Factories are buildings.","As factories drew workers from the countryside, Britain's cities grew faster than their housing and sanitation could handle."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties growth to inadequate infrastructure.$q$),
  ($q$ei-638$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.
• It is about the size of the United States.
• Parts of it receive almost no rain.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the Sahara's size and dryness. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Deserts are dry.","The Sahara, the largest hot desert in the world, is about the size of the United States, and parts of it receive almost no rain.","The United States is large.","The Sahara is in Africa."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice includes both size and dryness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-639$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A 2019 study found that urban trees lowered nearby temperatures by up to 5 degrees.
• The effect was strongest on streets with continuous tree cover.
• Cities with fewer trees recorded more heat-related illness.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue for planting more street trees. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Cities are warm.","Because urban trees can lower nearby temperatures by up to 5 degrees, especially along streets with continuous cover, cities with more trees may suffer less heat-related illness.","Trees have leaves.","A 2019 study measured temperatures."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice supports the argument with the findings.$q$),
  ($q$ei-640$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Spiders spin webs to catch insects.
• Webs are made of silk.
• Silk is produced in glands.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what spiders' webs are for. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Insects can fly.","Silk is made in glands.","Spiders have eight legs.","Spiders spin webs of silk to catch insects."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice states the purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-641$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The ancient Romans built roads across their empire.
• Many roads were built of layered stone and gravel.
• Some are still in use today.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the durability of Roman roads. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Built of layered stone and gravel, some Roman roads are still in use today.","Empires are large.","Romans built many roads.","Gravel is small stones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice emphasizes durability.$q$),
  ($q$ei-642$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybee colonies have declined in many regions.
• Pesticides, disease, and habitat loss are all blamed.
• Crops such as almonds depend on bee pollination.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the decline matters economically. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Habitat loss is common.","Because crops such as almonds depend on bee pollination, declines in honeybee colonies, linked to pesticides, disease, and habitat loss, threaten agriculture.","Almonds are nuts.","Honeybees make honey."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice connects the decline to crop production.$q$),
  ($q$ei-643$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The violin has four strings.
• They are tuned in perfect fifths.
• A bow of horsehair is drawn across them.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how a violin makes sound. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Horses have hair.","Perfect fifths are intervals.","A violinist produces sound by drawing a horsehair bow across the instrument's four strings, which are tuned in perfect fifths.","The violin is old."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice describes the sound production.$q$),
  ($q$ei-644$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Rainbows appear after rain.
• Sunlight passes through raindrops.
• The drops split the light into colors.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how rainbows form. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Rainbows are colorful.","Sunlight is bright.","Rainbows form when sunlight passes through raindrops, which split the light into colors.","Rain falls from clouds."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice states the mechanism.$q$),
  ($q$ei-645$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Writer Zora Neale Hurston collected folklore in the American South.
• She recorded stories, songs, and sayings.
• Her work preserved traditions that might otherwise have been lost.
• Her novel Their Eyes Were Watching God was published in 1937.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Hurston's work as a folklorist. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Hurston wrote a novel in 1937.","By recording stories, songs, and sayings across the American South, Zora Neale Hurston preserved folklore traditions that might otherwise have been lost.","The South is a region.","Folklore is a kind of story."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice focuses on her folklore collection and its value.$q$),
  ($q$ei-646$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The moon's gravity pulls on Earth's oceans.
• This causes tides.
• There are usually two high tides each day.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the cause of tides. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Oceans are large.","The moon's gravity pulls on Earth's oceans, causing the tides, with usually two high tides each day.","Tides can be high or low.","The moon orbits Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice states the cause and the pattern.$q$),
  ($q$ei-647$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea turtles return to the beach where they hatched to lay eggs.
• Scientists think they sense Earth's magnetic field.
• Experiments show turtles swim in the expected direction when the field is altered in a lab.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the evidence for the magnetic hypothesis. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Magnetic fields exist.","Sea turtles lay eggs.","Beaches are sandy.","Scientists hypothesize that sea turtles use Earth's magnetic field to return to their natal beaches, and lab experiments in which the field was altered changed the turtles' swimming direction in line with that idea."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice states the hypothesis and the supporting experiment.$q$),
  ($q$ei-648$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils.
• Archaeologists found edible honey in Egyptian tombs.
• Its low water content and acidity prevent bacteria from growing.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why honey lasts so long. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Honey is sweet.","Egyptian tombs are old.","Bacteria are small.","Honey never spoils because its low water content and acidity prevent bacteria from growing, which is why edible honey has been found in Egyptian tombs."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice gives the reason and the supporting example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-649$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Maria planted a vegetable garden.
• She grew tomatoes, beans, and squash.
• She shared the vegetables with her neighbors.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Maria's generosity. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Maria planted a garden.","Beans grow on vines.","Maria planted a vegetable garden of tomatoes, beans, and squash and shared the harvest with her neighbors.","Tomatoes are red."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice emphasizes sharing.$q$),
  ($q$ei-650$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The telegraph transmitted messages over wires in the 1840s.
• News that once took days could arrive in minutes.
• Newspapers began to cover distant events almost as they happened.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the telegraph changed journalism. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The 1840s were long ago.","Newspapers are printed daily.","Because the telegraph let news that once took days arrive within minutes, newspapers began covering distant events almost as they happened.","The telegraph used wires."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice shows the telegraph's effect on newspapers.$q$),
  ($q$ei-651$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Dr. Chen studies glaciers in Alaska.
• Her team measures how fast the ice is melting.
• Their data show a loss of 75 billion tons per year.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the team's key finding. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Dr. Chen studies glaciers.","Glaciers are made of ice.","Dr. Chen's team, which measures how fast Alaska's glaciers are melting, found a loss of 75 billion tons of ice per year.","Alaska is cold."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice states the finding clearly.$q$),
  ($q$ei-652$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The school started a recycling program.
• Students sort paper, plastic, and glass.
• The program has cut the school's waste in half.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the program's results. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Recycling is good.","Paper comes from trees.","Students sort items.","The school's recycling program, in which students sort paper, plastic, and glass, has cut its waste in half."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice highlights the result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-653$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The class read two novels this term. ______ they wrote a short essay comparing the main characters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Likewise,","Instead,","Afterward,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The essay followed the reading in time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-654$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Gold is a soft metal. ______ it is often mixed with other metals to make jewelry more durable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Therefore,","Likewise,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mixing with other metals is a result of gold's softness.$q$),
  ($q$ei-655$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Wolves hunt in packs. ______ mountain lions usually hunt alone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","For example,","Similarly,","By contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The hunting styles differ.$q$),
  ($q$ei-656$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The software update fixed several bugs. ______ it introduced a new problem with the login screen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Previously,","Likewise,","Unfortunately,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The new problem is a negative development after the fixes.$q$),
  ($q$ei-657$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics have long called the novel unreadable. ______ it has never been out of print, and each generation of readers rediscovers it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","For example,","Even so,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Continued readership contrasts with the critics' judgment.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Bioluminescence is the production of light by living organisms.<br>• Fireflies use bioluminescence to attract mates.<br>• The anglerfish uses a glowing lure to attract prey in the deep sea.<br>• The Hawaiian bobtail squid uses light from symbiotic bacteria to match moonlight from above, hiding its shadow from predators below.<br>• All three organisms produce light through a chemical reaction involving a molecule called luciferin.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize a similarity in how the three organisms produce light while noting a difference in how they use it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fireflies use bioluminescence to attract mates, anglerfish use a glowing lure to attract prey, and Hawaiian bobtail squid use light from bacteria to hide their shadows from predators below.","Fireflies, anglerfish, and Hawaiian bobtail squid all produce light through a chemical reaction involving luciferin, a process known as bioluminescence that occurs in many living organisms.","Like the anglerfish, which uses a glowing lure in the deep sea, the Hawaiian bobtail squid relies on light from symbiotic bacteria, which matches the moonlight shining down from above.","Although fireflies, anglerfish, and bobtail squid all produce light through a reaction involving luciferin, they use it for different purposes: attracting mates, luring prey, and hiding from predators."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal requires both a similarity (the shared chemical mechanism) and a difference (the various uses). Only the first choice states both. The second and fourth state only similarities, and the third states only differences between two organisms.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 2018, archaeologists reported findings from a site in the Olorgesailie Basin in southern Kenya.<br>• They found more than 500 stone tools.<br>• The tools were dated to about 320,000 years ago.<br>• Earlier tools at the site were large, heavy hand axes.<br>• The newer tools were smaller and more precisely shaped, and some were made from obsidian transported from more than 90 kilometers away.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how the newer tools differed from those made earlier at the site. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 2018, archaeologists reported finding more than 500 stone tools, dated to about 320,000 years ago, at a site in the Olorgesailie Basin in southern Kenya.","Some stone tools found at a site in southern Kenya were made from obsidian that had been transported to the site from more than 90 kilometers away.","The site's 320,000-year-old tools were smaller and more precisely shaped than its earlier hand axes, and some were made of obsidian from over 90 kilometers away.","Before about 320,000 years ago, people at a site in the Olorgesailie Basin made large, heavy hand axes, and more than 500 stone tools have been found there."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to describe how the newer tools differed from the older ones. Only the first choice contrasts the newer tools (smaller, precisely shaped, made of distant obsidian) with the earlier hand axes. The other choices describe the excavation or only one set of tools.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Octavia Butler (1947–2006) was an American science fiction author.<br>• Her novel Kindred (1979) follows a Black woman from 1970s California who is repeatedly transported to a Maryland plantation in the early 1800s.<br>• Butler described Kindred as "a kind of grim fantasy" rather than science fiction, since it offers no scientific explanation for the time travel.<br>• Kindred has sold more than half a million copies and is widely taught in high schools and colleges.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce Kindred to an audience unfamiliar with Octavia Butler and emphasize the novel's lasting popularity. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Octavia Butler, an American author who lived from 1947 to 2006, described her novel Kindred as \"a kind of grim fantasy\" because it gives no scientific explanation for time travel.","Octavia Butler's 1979 novel Kindred, which follows a modern Black woman transported to an antebellum plantation, has sold more than half a million copies and is widely taught in schools.","Kindred, a novel about a Black woman from 1970s California who is repeatedly transported to a Maryland plantation, was written by the science fiction author Octavia Butler.","Widely taught in high schools and colleges, Kindred has sold more than half a million copies since it was first published in 1979."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to introduce the novel to an unfamiliar audience and indicate its popularity. The first choice gives the author, title, date, and premise, and mentions its sales and use in schools. The others omit either the premise or the evidence of popularity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied two groups of honeybees.<br>• Group A was exposed to a common pesticide at levels typical of farm fields.<br>• Group B was not exposed.<br>• After four weeks, bees in Group A were 30% less likely to return to the hive after foraging.<br>• The researchers concluded that the pesticide impairs bees' navigation.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's conclusion and the evidence that supports it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Bees exposed to field levels of the pesticide were 30% less likely to return to the hive, leading researchers to conclude that it impairs navigation.","Researchers exposed one group of honeybees to a common pesticide at levels typical of farm fields for four weeks and compared it with a second group that was not exposed.","After four weeks, honeybees exposed to a common pesticide were 30% less likely than unexposed bees to return to the hive after foraging.","Researchers who studied two groups of honeybees concluded that a common pesticide, used at levels typical of farm fields, impairs bees' ability to navigate."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present the study's conclusion along with the evidence for it. Only the first choice states both the finding (30% less likely to return) and the conclusion (impaired navigation). The others describe the method without the result or conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Svalbard Global Seed Vault is located on an island in the Arctic Ocean, about 1,300 kilometers from the North Pole.<br>• It stores duplicate samples of seeds from gene banks around the world.<br>• The vault is built 120 meters inside a mountain.<br>• Permafrost keeps the seeds frozen even if the cooling system fails.<br>• In 2015, researchers withdrew seeds to replace collections lost in a gene bank damaged during the war in Syria.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide an example of the seed vault serving its intended purpose. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, which stores duplicate seed samples from gene banks around the world, is built 120 meters inside a mountain on an island in the Arctic Ocean.","Located about 1,300 kilometers from the North Pole, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault uses permafrost to keep seeds frozen even if its cooling system fails.","In 2015, a gene bank in Syria was damaged during the war there, and its collections were lost, prompting concern about the world's seed supply.","In 2015, researchers used duplicate seeds stored at the Svalbard Global Seed Vault to replace collections lost when a gene bank in Syria was damaged during the war there."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to give an example of the vault fulfilling its purpose. Only the first choice describes a specific instance (2015, Syria) in which stored seeds replaced lost collections. The others describe the vault's location or design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines.<br>• Their dense, tangled roots slow incoming waves.<br>• A 2020 study estimated that mangroves prevent more than $65 billion in flood damage worldwide each year.<br>• Mangroves store up to four times as much carbon per hectare as most tropical rainforests.<br>• About 35% of the world's mangroves have been lost since 1980, mainly to shrimp farms and coastal development.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the contrast between the value of mangrove forests and the rate at which they are disappearing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mangroves, whose dense roots slow incoming waves, prevent more than $65 billion in flood damage worldwide each year and store up to four times as much carbon per hectare as most rainforests.","About 35% of the world's mangroves, which grow along tropical coastlines, have been lost since 1980, mainly to shrimp farms and coastal development.","Mangroves provide valuable benefits—preventing more than $65 billion in flood damage each year and storing large amounts of carbon—yet about 35% of them have been lost since 1980.","Mangrove forests grow along tropical coastlines, where their dense, tangled roots slow incoming waves and help store large amounts of carbon."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal calls for highlighting the contrast between mangroves' value and their loss. Only the first choice includes both the benefits and the decline. The others give only benefits or only the loss.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Composer Hildegard of Bingen lived in Germany from 1098 to 1179.<br>• She was the abbess of a Benedictine convent.<br>• She wrote more than seventy liturgical songs.<br>• Her musical play Ordo Virtutum is one of the earliest known morality plays.<br>• Unlike most composers of her time, she is known by name; most medieval church music is anonymous.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how Hildegard of Bingen differed from most composers of her era. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hildegard of Bingen, who lived in Germany from 1098 to 1179, was the abbess of a Benedictine convent and wrote more than seventy liturgical songs.","Unlike the composers of most medieval church music, which is anonymous, Hildegard of Bingen is known by name.","Hildegard of Bingen's musical play Ordo Virtutum, written in twelfth-century Germany, is one of the earliest known morality plays.","Like many church musicians of her era, Hildegard of Bingen, a Benedictine abbess, composed liturgical songs—more than seventy in all."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to emphasize what made Hildegard unusual among medieval composers. Only the first choice explicitly contrasts her with typical, anonymous composers of the period.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Tardigrades are microscopic animals about 0.5 millimeters long.<br>• They can survive temperatures from −272°C to 150°C.<br>• In 2007, tardigrades were exposed to the vacuum of space for ten days aboard a satellite.<br>• Many survived and later reproduced normally.<br>• They survive extreme conditions by entering a dehydrated state called a tun.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how tardigrades survive extreme conditions and provide a striking example of their resilience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By entering a dehydrated state called a tun, tardigrades can survive extremes—many even survived ten days in the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades, microscopic animals about 0.5 millimeters long, can survive temperatures as low as −272°C and as high as 150°C.","In 2007, tardigrades were exposed to the vacuum of space for ten days aboard a satellite, and many of them later reproduced normally.","Tardigrades, which are only about 0.5 millimeters long, survive extreme conditions by entering a dehydrated state called a tun."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal requires both the mechanism (the tun state) and a dramatic illustration of survival. Only the first choice explains how they survive and gives the space experiment as evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The city of Medellín, Colombia, built a system of outdoor escalators in 2011.<br>• The escalators connect a hillside neighborhood, Comuna 13, to the city center.<br>• Before the escalators, residents had to climb the equivalent of a 28-story building to reach their homes.<br>• The trip now takes about six minutes.<br>• The escalators have become a tourist attraction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the escalators' impact on the daily lives of Comuna 13's residents. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Built in 2011, Medellín's outdoor escalators connect the hillside neighborhood of Comuna 13 to the city center and have since become a tourist attraction.","Before 2011, residents of Comuna 13, a hillside neighborhood in Medellín, had to climb the equivalent of a 28-story building to reach their homes.","Medellín's outdoor escalators, which connect Comuna 13 to the city center, have become a popular tourist attraction since they were built in 2011.","Medellín's outdoor escalators, built in 2011, cut what was once a climb equivalent to 28 stories into a six-minute ride for residents of Comuna 13."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to emphasize the escalators' practical benefit to residents. Only the first choice compares the old climb with the new six-minute ride. The others mention the escalators' existence, location, or tourist appeal.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researcher Mei Tanaka studied how children learn new words.<br>• She showed three-year-olds an unfamiliar object and named it once.<br>• A week later, 70% of the children correctly identified the object by name.<br>• When the object was named while the children were actively playing with it, 90% identified it correctly.<br>• Tanaka concluded that physical interaction strengthens word learning.$q$, $q$The student wants to compare the results of the two conditions in Tanaka's study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mei Tanaka showed three-year-olds an unfamiliar object and named it once, and a week later 70% of the children correctly identified the object by name.","Based on her study of how three-year-olds learn new words, Mei Tanaka concluded that physical interaction strengthens word learning.","Tanaka found that three-year-olds were more likely to remember an object's name a week later if they heard it while playing with the object (90%) than otherwise (70%).","When an object was named while children were actively playing with it, 90% of the three-year-olds in Tanaka's study identified it correctly a week later."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to compare the results of the two conditions. Only the first choice presents both percentages and identifies what distinguished the conditions.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Great Wall of China is not a single wall.<br>• It is a series of fortifications built over roughly two thousand years.<br>• The best-preserved sections were built during the Ming dynasty (1368–1644).<br>• A 2012 survey measured the total length of all sections at about 21,000 kilometers.<br>• Many earlier sections were made of rammed earth and have largely eroded.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception about the Great Wall of China. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The best-preserved sections of the Great Wall of China were built during the Ming dynasty, while many earlier sections, made of rammed earth, have largely eroded.","Rather than a single wall, the Great Wall of China is a series of fortifications built over some two thousand years, with a total length of about 21,000 kilometers.","A 2012 survey measured the total length of all sections of the Great Wall of China at about 21,000 kilometers, though many have eroded.","Built during the Ming dynasty (1368–1644), the best-preserved sections of the Great Wall of China are among the most visited."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to correct a misconception about the wall. Only the first choice directly states that it is not a single wall and gives supporting details.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Mariana Trench is the deepest known part of Earth's oceans.<br>• Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, is about 10,935 meters below sea level.<br>• Mount Everest's peak is about 8,849 meters above sea level.<br>• In 2019, explorer Victor Vescovo reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in a submersible.<br>• He found a plastic bag on the sea floor.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a comparison to help readers grasp the depth of the Challenger Deep. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Challenger Deep, at about 10,935 meters below sea level, is deeper than Mount Everest is tall: Everest's peak is about 8,849 meters above sea level.","The Mariana Trench, the deepest known part of Earth's oceans, has a deepest point called the Challenger Deep, which Victor Vescovo reached in 2019.","In 2019, explorer Victor Vescovo reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in a submersible and found a plastic bag on the sea floor.","Mount Everest's peak is about 8,849 meters above sea level, while the Mariana Trench is the deepest known part of Earth's oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to convey the depth of the trench by comparison with Everest. Only the first choice gives both measurements and states the comparison. The last choice mentions both but gives no depth for the trench, so no comparison can be made.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Artist Yayoi Kusama was born in Japan in 1929.<br>• She moved to New York in 1958.<br>• Her "Infinity Mirror Rooms" use mirrors and lights to create the illusion of endless space.<br>• In 2017, an exhibition of her Infinity Mirror Rooms at a Washington, D.C., museum drew more than 160,000 visitors.<br>• Visitors were limited to about thirty seconds inside each room.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe Kusama's Infinity Mirror Rooms and indicate their popularity. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Yayoi Kusama, who was born in Japan in 1929 and moved to New York in 1958, is known for her Infinity Mirror Rooms.","Visitors to a 2017 exhibition of Yayoi Kusama's Infinity Mirror Rooms in Washington, D.C., were limited to about thirty seconds in each room.","More than 160,000 visitors attended a 2017 exhibition in Washington, D.C., featuring the work of Yayoi Kusama, who was born in Japan in 1929.","Kusama's Infinity Mirror Rooms, which create an illusion of endless space, drew over 160,000 visitors to a 2017 exhibition."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to describe the rooms and show their popularity. Only the first choice describes what the rooms are and gives the attendance figure.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sea turtles return to the beaches where they hatched to lay their eggs.<br>• Scientists long wondered how they find these beaches after years at sea.<br>• A study by Kenneth Lohmann suggested that turtles detect the unique magnetic signature of their home beach.<br>• Each stretch of coastline has a slightly different magnetic field.<br>• Turtles may imprint on this signature as hatchlings.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a possible answer to the question of how sea turtles find their home beaches. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Scientists long wondered how sea turtles, which return to the beaches where they hatched to lay their eggs, are able to find those beaches after years at sea.","Kenneth Lohmann studied sea turtles, which return to the beaches where they hatched in order to lay their eggs.","Sea turtles may find home by sensing magnetic signatures: each coastline has a slightly different field, which hatchlings may learn.","Each stretch of coastline has a slightly different magnetic field, and sea turtles return to the beaches where they hatched to lay their eggs."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain a possible answer to the question of how turtles navigate. Only the first choice explains the magnetic-signature hypothesis.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Voynich manuscript is a handwritten book dated to the early fifteenth century.<br>• It is written in an unknown script.<br>• It contains illustrations of plants that do not match any known species.<br>• Linguists, cryptographers, and computer scientists have tried to decode it.<br>• No proposed decipherment has been widely accepted.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the Voynich manuscript has resisted decoding despite considerable effort. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Voynich manuscript, a handwritten book from the early fifteenth century, contains illustrations of plants that do not match any known species.","Despite efforts by linguists, cryptographers, and computer scientists, no proposed decipherment of the fifteenth-century Voynich manuscript has been widely accepted.","Linguists, cryptographers, and computer scientists have studied the Voynich manuscript, a book written in an unknown script in the early fifteenth century.","Written in an unknown script, the fifteenth-century Voynich manuscript is illustrated with drawings of plants unlike any known species."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to emphasize that the manuscript remains a mystery despite expert attempts to decode it. Only the first choice mentions the attempts and their lack of success.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two cities installed "cool roofs"—roofs painted white to reflect sunlight.<br>• In City A, cool roofs were installed on 10% of buildings.<br>• In City B, they were installed on 40% of buildings.<br>• Average summer temperatures dropped by 0.2°C in City A.<br>• Average summer temperatures dropped by 0.9°C in City B.$q$, $q$The student wants to suggest that the extent of cool-roof installation affected the amount of cooling. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Summer temperatures fell more in City B, where cool roofs covered 40% of buildings (0.9°C), than in City A, where they covered 10% (0.2°C).","Two cities installed cool roofs—roofs painted white to reflect sunlight—and average summer temperatures dropped in both City A and City B.","In City B, cool roofs were installed on 40% of buildings, and average summer temperatures there dropped by 0.9°C.","City A installed cool roofs on 10% of its buildings, while City B installed them on 40% of its buildings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to show that more cool roofs produced greater cooling. Only the first choice pairs each city's coverage with its temperature drop, revealing the relationship.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Japanese art of kintsugi repairs broken pottery with lacquer mixed with powdered gold.<br>• The cracks are highlighted rather than hidden.<br>• The practice is associated with the philosophy of wabi-sabi, which finds beauty in imperfection.<br>• Kintsugi may have begun in the fifteenth century.<br>• Repaired pieces are sometimes valued more highly than undamaged ones.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how kintsugi reflects a particular philosophy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kintsugi, a Japanese art that may have begun in the fifteenth century, repairs broken pottery using lacquer mixed with powdered gold.","Pottery repaired with kintsugi, which uses lacquer mixed with powdered gold, is sometimes valued more highly than pottery that was never broken.","The philosophy of wabi-sabi, which finds beauty in imperfection, is associated with many Japanese arts, including kintsugi, which may date to the fifteenth century.","Kintsugi, the Japanese art of repairing pottery with gold-flecked lacquer, highlights cracks rather than hiding them, reflecting a philosophy that finds beauty in imperfection."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain the connection between the technique and its underlying philosophy. Only the first choice describes the technique and links it to wabi-sabi's appreciation of imperfection.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested a new method of teaching fractions.<br>• Students in the experimental group used number lines.<br>• Students in the control group used pie diagrams.<br>• On a final test, the number-line group scored 15 points higher on average.<br>• The difference was largest on problems involving fractions greater than one.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the results of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers tested a new method of teaching fractions in which students in the experimental group used number lines while students in the control group used pie diagrams.","On a final test about fractions, students who used number lines answered problems involving fractions greater than one.","Students who learned fractions with number lines outperformed those who used pie diagrams by 15 points on average, with the largest advantage on fractions greater than one.","Students in a control group learned fractions with pie diagrams, a common method, while an experimental group tried a newer approach using number lines."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to present the study's results. Only the first choice reports the outcome, including the average difference and where it was largest.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ada Lovelace (1815–1852) was an English mathematician.<br>• She worked with Charles Babbage on his proposed Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer that was never built.<br>• In 1843, she published notes describing how the machine could calculate a sequence of numbers called Bernoulli numbers.<br>• These notes are often considered the first computer program.<br>• She also suggested the machine might one day compose music.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Ada Lovelace is often regarded as a pioneer of computer programming. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ada Lovelace, an English mathematician who lived from 1815 to 1852, worked with Charles Babbage on his proposed Analytical Engine.","Ada Lovelace's 1843 notes on how Babbage's Analytical Engine could calculate Bernoulli numbers are often considered the first computer program.","Ada Lovelace suggested that Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine, a mechanical computer that was never built, might one day compose music.","In 1843, Ada Lovelace published notes about Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine, a proposed mechanical computer that was never built."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why Lovelace is considered a pioneer of computing. Only the first choice describes her notes and their significance as the first program.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied the diets of urban and rural red foxes in England.<br>• Urban foxes got about 35% of their food from human sources, such as garbage and pet food.<br>• Rural foxes got about 6% of their food from human sources.<br>• Urban foxes had shorter, wider snouts than rural foxes.<br>• The researchers suggested that softer human food may reduce the need for long snouts used to catch prey.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the researchers' explanation for a physical difference between urban and rural foxes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Urban foxes, which ate more human food (35% vs. 6%), had shorter, wider snouts—possibly because soft human food reduces the need for long snouts.","Researchers studying red foxes in England found that urban foxes got about 35% of their food from human sources, compared with about 6% for rural foxes.","Urban red foxes in England had shorter, wider snouts than rural foxes and got about 35% of their food from garbage, pet food, and other human sources.","Researchers studying red foxes in England compared the diets of urban and rural foxes and also measured the length and width of their snouts."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal requires linking diet differences to physical differences. Only the first choice presents both the dietary difference and the snout difference, along with the proposed explanation connecting them.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The ancient city of Pompeii was buried by an eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE.<br>• Volcanic ash preserved buildings, frescoes, and even loaves of bread.<br>• Excavations began in 1748.<br>• About one-third of the city remains unexcavated.<br>• Archaeologists now prefer to leave some areas untouched so that future researchers, with better technology, can study them.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why much of Pompeii has not been excavated. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Pompeii, buried by an eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, has been excavated since 1748, revealing buildings, frescoes, and even loaves of bread.","Volcanic ash from the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE preserved Pompeii's buildings and frescoes, which archaeologists began excavating in 1748.","About one-third of Pompeii, which was buried by an eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, remains unexcavated today.","About a third of Pompeii remains unexcavated, partly because archaeologists want to leave areas for future researchers with better technology."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain why part of Pompeii remains unexcavated. Only the first choice gives the proportion unexcavated and the archaeologists' reason.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some spiders "balloon": they release silk threads and float into the air.<br>• Ballooning spiders have been found more than 4 kilometers above the ground.<br>• Scientists long assumed wind was responsible for lifting them.<br>• A 2018 study found that spiders also respond to Earth's electric field.<br>• In the study, spiders began ballooning in a lab with no wind when an electric field was switched on.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how a recent study challenged an earlier assumption about ballooning spiders. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ballooning spiders, which release silk threads and float into the air, have been found more than 4 kilometers above the ground.","Scientists long assumed that wind was responsible for lifting ballooning spiders, which have been found more than 4 kilometers above the ground.","Challenging the belief that wind alone lifts them, a 2018 study showed spiders ballooning in a windless lab when an electric field was switched on.","A 2018 study found that ballooning spiders, which release silk threads to float into the air, respond to Earth's electric field."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to show how new evidence challenged an earlier assumption. Only the first choice states the earlier belief and the study's contradicting evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The poet Pablo Neruda was born in Chile in 1904.<br>• His collection Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair was published in 1924, when he was nineteen.<br>• It became one of the best-selling books of poetry in the Spanish language.<br>• Neruda later wrote overtly political poetry.<br>• He received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1971.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how early in his life Neruda achieved success. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Pablo Neruda, who was born in Chile in 1904, wrote overtly political poetry later in his career and received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1971.","Neruda was only nineteen when he published Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair, which became one of the best-selling books of Spanish poetry.","Published in 1924, Pablo Neruda's Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair became one of the best-selling books of poetry in the Spanish language.","Pablo Neruda, a Chilean poet who received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1971, published Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair in 1924."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to emphasize Neruda's early success. Only the first choice highlights his age and the collection's sales, contrasting it with his much later Nobel Prize.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The European Union banned single-use plastic straws, plates, and cutlery in 2021.<br>• The ban aims to reduce ocean pollution.<br>• Single-use plastics make up about half of the litter found on European beaches.<br>• Businesses were allowed to sell existing stock after the ban took effect.<br>• Alternatives include products made of paper, bamboo, and wood.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the purpose of the ban and the problem it was meant to address. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["To reduce ocean pollution—single-use plastics make up about half of the litter on European beaches—the EU banned plastic straws, plates, and cutlery in 2021.","In 2021, the European Union banned single-use plastic straws, plates, and cutlery, though businesses were allowed to sell their existing stock.","Single-use plastics, which make up about half of the litter on European beaches, can be replaced by products made of paper, bamboo, and wood.","The European Union's 2021 ban on single-use plastic straws, plates, and cutlery encouraged alternatives made of paper, bamboo, and wood."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain the ban's purpose and the problem it addresses. Only the first choice gives both the purpose and the evidence of the problem.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Wildfire smoke contains fine particles called PM2.5.<br>• These particles can travel thousands of kilometers.<br>• In 2023, smoke from wildfires in Quebec, Canada, caused hazardous air quality in New York City.<br>• New York City is more than 600 kilometers from the fires.<br>• Health officials urged residents to stay indoors.$q$, $q$The student wants to use an example to illustrate how far wildfire smoke can travel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Wildfire smoke contains fine particles called PM2.5, which can travel thousands of kilometers from the fires that produce them.","In 2023, health officials urged New York City residents to stay indoors because of hazardous air quality caused by wildfire smoke.","In 2023, there were wildfires in Quebec, Canada, and the smoke they produced contained fine particles called PM2.5.","Smoke from 2023 Quebec wildfires caused hazardous air in New York City, more than 600 kilometers away."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to illustrate, with an example, the distance smoke can travel. Only the first choice gives a specific case with the distance involved.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers in Peru are studying a variety of potato called papa amarilla.<br>• It grows at altitudes above 3,500 meters.<br>• It is unusually resistant to frost.<br>• It is also more susceptible to a fungal disease called late blight than common varieties.<br>• Researchers hope to breed its frost resistance into other varieties.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe both an advantage and a disadvantage of papa amarilla. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Papa amarilla, a potato variety studied by researchers in Peru, grows at altitudes above 3,500 meters and is unusually resistant to frost.","Researchers in Peru hope to breed the unusual frost resistance of papa amarilla, which grows above 3,500 meters, into other potato varieties.","Papa amarilla, a potato variety grown above 3,500 meters in Peru, resists frost unusually well but is more susceptible to late blight than common varieties.","Papa amarilla is more susceptible to a fungal disease called late blight than common potato varieties grown at lower altitudes."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to present both a strength and a weakness of the variety. Only the first choice mentions frost resistance and susceptibility to late blight.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Nigerian author Chinua Achebe published Things Fall Apart in 1958.<br>• The novel depicts life in an Igbo village before and during British colonization.<br>• Achebe wrote it partly in response to European novels that portrayed Africans as lacking culture.<br>• It has been translated into more than fifty languages.<br>• It is one of the most widely read African novels.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what motivated Achebe to write Things Fall Apart. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe's 1958 novel about life in an Igbo village before and during British colonization, has been translated into more than fifty languages.","Achebe wrote Things Fall Apart (1958), a novel depicting an Igbo village before and during colonization, partly to counter European novels that portrayed Africans as lacking culture.","Nigerian author Chinua Achebe's Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, is one of the most widely read African novels and has been translated into more than fifty languages.","Set in an Igbo village before and during British colonization, Things Fall Apart was published in 1958 by the Nigerian author Chinua Achebe."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain Achebe's motivation for writing the novel. Only the first choice describes the novel and identifies what it was responding to.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A study compared two groups of hospital patients recovering from surgery.<br>• Patients in Group 1 had rooms with windows facing a grove of trees.<br>• Patients in Group 2 had rooms with windows facing a brick wall.<br>• Group 1 patients left the hospital about one day sooner on average.<br>• Group 1 patients also requested less pain medication.$q$, $q$The student wants to summarize the study's findings about the effect of a window view on recovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Surgical patients whose windows faced trees left the hospital about a day sooner and requested less pain medication than those whose windows faced a brick wall.","A study compared surgical patients whose hospital windows faced a grove of trees with patients whose windows faced a brick wall.","Researchers compared how much pain medication was requested by surgical patients whose windows faced trees and by those whose windows faced a brick wall.","Patients in Group 2, whose windows faced a brick wall, were compared with patients in Group 1, whose windows faced a grove of trees."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to summarize the study's findings comparing the two groups. Only the first choice states both outcomes and identifies both conditions.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The axolotl is a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City.<br>• Unlike most amphibians, it keeps its gills and lives in water its entire life.<br>• It can regrow lost limbs, parts of its heart, and even parts of its brain.<br>• Scientists study it to learn about tissue regeneration.<br>• It is critically endangered in the wild due to pollution and urban growth.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the axolotl's importance to science with its situation in the wild. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The axolotl, a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City, keeps its gills and lives in water its entire life, unlike most amphibians.","Scientists study the axolotl, which can regrow lost limbs and parts of its heart and brain, to learn about tissue regeneration.","The axolotl is critically endangered in the wild because of pollution and urban growth around the lakes near Mexico City.","Axolotls, which can regrow limbs and even parts of their brains, are valuable to science—yet critically endangered in the wild."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to contrast the axolotl's scientific value with its endangered status. Only the first choice includes both.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Hubble Space Telescope was launched in 1990.<br>• Its first images were blurry because of a flaw in its main mirror.<br>• In 1993, astronauts installed corrective optics during a spacewalk.<br>• After the repair, Hubble produced some of the sharpest images of space ever taken.<br>• Hubble has since been used in more than 19,000 published scientific papers.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the problem Hubble faced and how it was resolved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Launched in 1990, the Hubble Space Telescope has since been used in more than 19,000 published scientific papers.","In 1993, astronauts conducted a spacewalk to install corrective optics on the Hubble Space Telescope, which had been launched in 1990.","Hubble's first images were blurry due to a mirror flaw, but after astronauts added corrective optics in 1993, its images became exceptionally sharp.","After 1993, the Hubble Space Telescope produced some of the sharpest images of space ever taken and has been used in more than 19,000 papers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to describe the problem and how it was solved. Only the first choice includes the flaw, the repair, and the result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1970, a researcher placed a mirror in front of chimpanzees after marking their faces with red dye while they were unconscious.<br>• When they saw their reflections, the chimpanzees touched the marks on their own faces.<br>• This "mirror test" is used to assess self-recognition.<br>• Elephants, dolphins, and magpies have also passed the test.<br>• Most monkeys and dogs have not.$q$, $q$The student wants to indicate which animals have passed the mirror test and which generally have not. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1970, a researcher marked chimpanzees' faces with red dye and found that, when they saw their reflections, the chimpanzees touched the marks on their own faces.","The mirror test, which assesses self-recognition, has been passed by chimpanzees, elephants, dolphins, and magpies, but not by most monkeys and dogs.","The mirror test, first used with chimpanzees in 1970, assesses self-recognition by marking an animal's face and observing its response to a mirror.","Elephants, dolphins, and magpies, like the chimpanzees first tested in 1970, have passed the mirror test."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to indicate which animals have and have not passed the test. Only the first choice names both groups.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Bamboo is a type of grass.<br>• Some species can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day.<br>• Bamboo has a tensile strength comparable to that of steel.<br>• It regrows from its roots after being harvested, without replanting.<br>• In parts of Asia, it has been used for construction for thousands of years.$q$, $q$The student wants to make the case that bamboo is a sustainable building material. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because bamboo is as strong as steel in tension and regrows from its roots after harvest without replanting, it can be an appealing renewable building material.","Bamboo, a type of grass, can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day and has been used for construction in parts of Asia for thousands of years.","Some species of bamboo, a type of grass, can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day and regrow from their roots after being harvested.","Bamboo has a tensile strength comparable to that of steel and has been used for construction in parts of Asia for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to argue that bamboo is a sustainable building material. Only the first choice combines its strength (suitability for building) with its regrowth (sustainability).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers placed GPS trackers on 30 Arctic terns.<br>• Arctic terns breed in the Arctic and spend the northern winter near Antarctica.<br>• The average bird flew about 70,900 kilometers per year.<br>• Over a lifetime of about thirty years, a tern may fly the equivalent of three round trips to the Moon.<br>• The terns did not fly in a straight line but followed wind patterns in an S-shaped route.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the enormous distance an Arctic tern travels over its lifetime. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers placed GPS trackers on 30 Arctic terns, birds that breed in the Arctic and spend the northern winter near Antarctica.","Arctic terns, which breed in the Arctic and winter near Antarctica, follow an S-shaped route shaped by wind patterns rather than flying in a straight line.","Arctic terns live about thirty years and spend the northern winter near Antarctica, following wind patterns in an S-shaped route.","An Arctic tern flies about 70,900 kilometers a year, so over a thirty-year lifetime it may travel the equivalent of three round trips to the Moon."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to convey the immense distances terns travel. Only the first choice gives the annual distance and the vivid Moon comparison.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1955, Claudette Colvin, age fifteen, refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama.<br>• This happened nine months before Rosa Parks's more famous protest.<br>• Colvin was arrested.<br>• She later became a plaintiff in Browder v. Gayle, the case that ruled Montgomery's bus segregation unconstitutional.<br>• Her role was largely overlooked for decades.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Colvin's protest preceded Parks's but received far less attention. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1955, fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin was arrested after refusing to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama.","Claudette Colvin later became a plaintiff in Browder v. Gayle, the case that ruled bus segregation in Montgomery, Alabama, unconstitutional.","Claudette Colvin refused to give up her bus seat nine months before Rosa Parks did, yet her role was overlooked for decades.","Like Rosa Parks, Claudette Colvin refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, in 1955."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to emphasize that Colvin acted before Parks yet received little recognition. Only the first choice includes the timing relative to Parks and the decades of neglect.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers measured noise in 50 schools near a major airport.<br>• In schools under flight paths, aircraft noise exceeded 70 decibels about 40 times per day.<br>• Students in these schools scored lower on reading tests than students in quieter schools.<br>• After the airport moved its flight paths, reading scores in the affected schools improved within two years.<br>• Math scores showed no significant change.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the most compelling evidence that aircraft noise affects students' reading. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 50 schools near a major airport, aircraft noise exceeded 70 decibels about 40 times per day in the schools located under flight paths.","After the airport moved its flight paths away from certain schools, students' reading scores improved within two years, suggesting that aircraft noise had hindered reading.","After the airport moved its flight paths, math scores at the affected schools showed no significant change within two years.","Students at schools under the airport's flight paths, where noise often exceeded 70 decibels, scored lower on reading tests than students at quieter schools."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is the most compelling evidence that noise affects reading. Scores improving after the flight paths moved (with noise as the only change) is stronger evidence than a simple comparison between noisy and quiet schools, which could differ in other ways. The other choices give background or a result unrelated to reading.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The mantis shrimp has the most complex eyes known in the animal kingdom.<br>• Humans have three types of color receptors.<br>• Mantis shrimp have twelve to sixteen.<br>• However, tests show they are worse than humans at distinguishing between similar colors.<br>• Scientists think they may recognize colors quickly without comparing them, like a scanner.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a surprising finding about mantis shrimp vision. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite having twelve to sixteen types of color receptors to humans' three, mantis shrimp are worse than humans at distinguishing similar colors.","Mantis shrimp, which have the most complex eyes known in the animal kingdom, have twelve to sixteen types of color receptors.","Humans have three types of color receptors, and scientists think that mantis shrimp may recognize colors quickly without comparing them, like a scanner.","With twelve to sixteen types of color receptors, compared with three in humans, mantis shrimp have the most complex eyes known."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present the surprising finding. Only the first choice juxtaposes the large number of receptors with their poorer color discrimination.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ibn Battuta was a Moroccan scholar born in 1304.<br>• Over about thirty years, he traveled roughly 117,000 kilometers.<br>• He visited West Africa, the Middle East, India, and China.<br>• After returning home, he dictated an account of his travels called the Rihla.<br>• The Rihla is a key source on fourteenth-century societies.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the importance of the Rihla to modern historians. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ibn Battuta, a Moroccan scholar born in 1304, traveled roughly 117,000 kilometers over about thirty years, visiting West Africa, the Middle East, India, and China.","After traveling to West Africa, the Middle East, India, and China, Ibn Battuta returned home and dictated an account of his journeys called the Rihla.","Born in Morocco in 1304, Ibn Battuta spent about thirty years traveling roughly 117,000 kilometers.","Ibn Battuta's Rihla, his account of some thirty years of travel across Africa and Asia, remains a key source for historians of the fourteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to emphasize the Rihla's historical significance. Only the first choice identifies the book and explains its value to historians.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Dead Sea is a salt lake between Jordan and Israel.<br>• Its surface is about 430 meters below sea level, the lowest land elevation on Earth.<br>• Its water is about ten times saltier than the ocean.<br>• People float easily in it because of the high salt content.<br>• The lake's level has dropped more than a meter per year in recent decades, mainly because water from the Jordan River is diverted for agriculture.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Dead Sea has been shrinking. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Dead Sea, which lies between Jordan and Israel about 430 meters below sea level, is about ten times saltier than the ocean.","The level of the Dead Sea, the lowest land elevation on Earth, has dropped by more than a meter per year in recent decades.","The Dead Sea has dropped over a meter per year in recent decades, mainly because Jordan River water is diverted for agriculture.","Water from the Jordan River is diverted for agriculture in the region around the Dead Sea, a salt lake ten times saltier than the ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain a cause of the Dead Sea's decline. Only the first choice gives both the decline and its cause.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers gave participants a list of 20 words to memorize.<br>• Half the participants walked on a treadmill while studying; half sat still.<br>• Both groups were tested immediately and again one week later.<br>• Immediately after studying, both groups recalled about 12 words.<br>• A week later, the walking group recalled 9 words on average; the sitting group recalled 6.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the difference between the groups appeared only after a delay. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants who walked on a treadmill while studying a list of 20 words recalled about 12 words immediately afterward and 9 words a week later.","Although both groups initially recalled about 12 words, a week later participants who walked while studying recalled more (9) than those who sat still (6).","A week after studying a list of 20 words, participants who walked while studying recalled 9 words on average, while those who sat still recalled 6.","Researchers tested participants' memory of a list of 20 words both immediately after studying and one week later."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to show that the advantage of walking appeared only over time. Only the first choice contrasts the identical immediate results with the different results a week later.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Lichens are composite organisms made of a fungus and an alga or cyanobacterium.<br>• The fungus provides structure and protection.<br>• The alga or cyanobacterium produces food through photosynthesis.<br>• Lichens can grow on bare rock, tree bark, and even Antarctic stones.<br>• They are highly sensitive to air pollution and are used to monitor air quality.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a practical use humans have found for lichens. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because lichens are highly sensitive to air pollution, scientists use them to monitor air quality.","Lichens, which are made of a fungus and an alga or cyanobacterium, can grow on bare rock, tree bark, and even Antarctic stones.","In a lichen, the fungus provides structure and protection, while the alga or cyanobacterium produces food through photosynthesis.","Lichens are composite organisms that can grow in harsh places, from bare rock to Antarctic stones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain a practical use of lichens. Only the first choice describes their use in monitoring air quality and why they are suited to it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Oxford English Dictionary (OED) began in 1857.<br>• Its editors asked the public to send in examples of how words were used in books.<br>• Thousands of volunteers mailed in slips of paper with quotations.<br>• One of the most prolific contributors, William Chester Minor, sent in more than 10,000 slips.<br>• The first complete edition was published in 1928, seventy-one years after the project began.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the role that members of the public played in creating the OED. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Oxford English Dictionary, which began in 1857, took seventy-one years to complete, with its first full edition published in 1928.","William Chester Minor, one of the most prolific contributors to the Oxford English Dictionary, sent in more than 10,000 slips of paper with quotations.","The Oxford English Dictionary, begun in 1857, collected quotations showing how words had been used in books.","Thousands of volunteers mailed the OED quotations showing how words were used; one alone sent more than 10,000."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to emphasize the role of public volunteers. Only the first choice describes the volunteers' contributions and gives an example of their scale.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers placed cameras in a forest in Gabon.<br>• They recorded chimpanzees using stones to crack open hard-shelled nuts.<br>• Some chimpanzees carried stones more than 100 meters to nut trees.<br>• Young chimpanzees took up to seven years to master the technique.<br>• They learned by watching adults, especially their mothers.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how young chimpanzees learn to crack nuts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers using cameras in a forest in Gabon recorded chimpanzees using stones to crack open hard-shelled nuts.","Some chimpanzees in Gabon carried stones more than 100 meters to nut trees in order to crack open the nuts' hard shells.","Young chimpanzees learn to crack nuts by watching adults, especially their mothers, and can take up to seven years to master it.","Chimpanzees in Gabon use stones to crack open hard-shelled nuts, a technique that young chimpanzees take years to master."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to describe how young chimpanzees acquire the skill. Only the first choice explains the learning process and its duration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Trans-Siberian Railway connects Moscow and Vladivostok.<br>• It is about 9,289 kilometers long.<br>• It crosses eight time zones.<br>• Construction began in 1891 and the main line was completed in 1916.<br>• A nonstop trip takes about seven days.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the immense length of the Trans-Siberian Railway. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Trans-Siberian Railway, which connects Moscow and Vladivostok, was begun in 1891 and its main line completed in 1916.","About 9,289 kilometers long, the Trans-Siberian Railway crosses eight time zones and takes about seven days to ride.","Completed in 1916, the Trans-Siberian Railway connects Moscow and Vladivostok, a trip that takes about seven days nonstop.","The Trans-Siberian Railway, which connects Moscow in the west with Vladivostok in the east, was built between 1891 and 1916."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to convey the railway's length vividly. Only the first choice gives the length and illustrates it with the time zones and travel time.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some scientists want to bring back the woolly mammoth through genetic engineering.<br>• They plan to edit elephant DNA to include mammoth traits such as thick hair and fat.<br>• Supporters argue the animals could help restore Arctic grasslands.<br>• Critics argue the money would be better spent protecting living endangered species.<br>• Critics also note that elephants have long pregnancies, about 22 months.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the main argument on each side of the debate about reviving the woolly mammoth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Supporters say revived mammoths could help restore Arctic grasslands; critics say the money should protect living endangered species.","Some scientists plan to bring back the woolly mammoth by editing elephant DNA to include traits such as thick hair and fat.","Critics of reviving the woolly mammoth note that elephants, whose DNA would be edited to include mammoth traits, have pregnancies lasting about 22 months.","Supporters of reviving the woolly mammoth by editing elephant DNA argue that the animals could help restore Arctic grasslands."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present both sides of the debate. Only the first choice includes the supporters' and critics' main arguments.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Painter Amrita Sher-Gil was born in Budapest in 1913 to a Hungarian mother and an Indian father.<br>• She trained at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris.<br>• Her early paintings followed European academic conventions.<br>• After moving to India in 1934, she began painting rural Indian women in a flattened, brightly colored style influenced by Mughal miniatures.<br>• She died in 1941 at age twenty-eight.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how Sher-Gil's artistic style changed over her career. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Amrita Sher-Gil, born in Budapest in 1913 to a Hungarian mother and an Indian father, trained at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris.","After moving to India in 1934, Amrita Sher-Gil painted rural Indian women, continuing to work until her death in 1941 at age twenty-eight.","Amrita Sher-Gil's early paintings, made while she trained at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris, followed European academic conventions.","Sher-Gil moved from European academic conventions to a flat, vivid, Mughal-influenced style after settling in India in 1934."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to describe how her style changed. Only the first choice describes both the earlier European style and the later Mughal-influenced style. The others describe only her background, only her later subjects, or only her early style.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied 2,000 adults over ten years.<br>• Participants who ate nuts at least five times a week had a 20% lower risk of heart disease than those who rarely ate nuts.<br>• The nut-eaters also exercised more and smoked less on average.<br>• The researchers controlled for exercise and smoking in their analysis.<br>• Even after these adjustments, the nut-eaters had a 14% lower risk.$q$, $q$The student wants to address the possibility that the nut-eaters' lower risk was due only to their exercise and smoking habits. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Adults who ate nuts at least five times a week had a 20% lower risk of heart disease, though they also exercised more and smoked less on average.","In a ten-year study of 2,000 adults, people who ate nuts frequently had a lower risk of heart disease than those who rarely ate them.","Even after adjusting for exercise and smoking, researchers found that nut-eaters had a 14% lower risk of heart disease.","Because nut-eaters exercised more and smoked less, researchers adjusted their analysis of heart-disease risk to account for these differences."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to show that the benefit persisted after other factors were considered. Only the first choice reports the adjusted 14% figure along with the factors controlled. The second gives the unadjusted figure and leaves open whether exercise and smoking explain it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Atacama Desert in Chile is one of the driest places on Earth.<br>• Some weather stations there have never recorded rain.<br>• Its high altitude, dry air, and lack of light pollution make it ideal for astronomy.<br>• It hosts several major observatories, including ALMA, an array of 66 radio antennas.<br>• NASA has tested Mars rovers there because its soil resembles that of Mars.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the variety of scientific uses for the Atacama Desert. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Atacama Desert in Chile, where some weather stations have never recorded rain, is one of the driest places on Earth.","NASA tests Mars rovers in the Atacama's Mars-like soil, and its dry, dark skies host observatories such as ALMA.","With its high altitude, dry air, and lack of light pollution, the Atacama Desert hosts several major observatories, including ALMA.","ALMA, an array of 66 radio antennas, is one of several major observatories in Chile's extremely dry Atacama Desert."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to show two different ways scientists use the desert. Only the first choice mentions both rover testing and astronomy. The others focus on the desert's dryness or only on astronomy.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Inca built a road network called the Qhapaq Ñan.<br>• It extended about 40,000 kilometers across what is now Peru, Ecuador, Bolivia, Chile, Argentina, and Colombia.<br>• The Inca did not use wheeled vehicles.<br>• Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the roads.<br>• Chasquis could carry a message about 240 kilometers in a single day.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly messages could travel across the Inca Empire. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Without wheeled vehicles, the Inca relied on relay runners called chasquis, who could carry a message about 240 kilometers along the Qhapaq Ñan in a single day.","The Qhapaq Ñan, an Inca road network, extended about 40,000 kilometers across what are now six South American countries.","Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the Qhapaq Ñan, an Inca road network about 40,000 kilometers long.","The Inca, who did not use wheeled vehicles, built a road network that crossed what are now Peru, Ecuador, Bolivia, Chile, Argentina, and Colombia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize how quickly information moved. Only the first choice gives the speed of the chasquis (240 kilometers a day). The third mentions the runners but not their speed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two species of finches live on the same island.<br>• Species A has a large, thick beak.<br>• Species B has a small, narrow beak.<br>• During droughts, only large, hard seeds remain available.<br>• In a 1977 drought, Species A's population fell by 20%, while Species B's fell by 85%.$q$, $q$The student wants to suggest that beak shape influenced the finches' survival during the drought. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Two finch species on the same island differ in their beaks: Species A has a large, thick beak, while Species B has a small, narrow one.","In a 1977 drought on the island, the population of Species B, which has a small, narrow beak, fell by 85%.","Because only large, hard seeds remain available during droughts, both finch species on the island lost population during a drought in 1977.","During a 1977 drought, when only large, hard seeds remained, the thick-beaked Species A declined far less (20%) than the narrow-beaked Species B (85%)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to suggest that beak shape affected survival. Only the first choice links each species' beak type to its population loss under drought conditions.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sound travels about 343 meters per second in air.<br>• It travels about 1,480 meters per second in water.<br>• It travels about 5,960 meters per second in steel.<br>• Sound travels faster in materials whose particles are more tightly bound.<br>• Before modern communication, people sometimes put an ear to a railroad track to hear an approaching train.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why putting an ear to a railroad track could help someone detect an approaching train. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sound travels about 343 meters per second in air, about 1,480 meters per second in water, and about 5,960 meters per second in steel.","Before modern communication, people sometimes put an ear to a railroad track to listen for trains, since sound travels through materials.","Since sound moves far faster in steel (5,960 m/s) than in air (343 m/s), a train can be heard through the rails first.","Sound travels faster in materials whose particles are tightly bound, such as steel, than in materials such as air or water."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain why listening to a rail works. Only the first choice connects the speed difference in steel and air to the practice of listening to tracks.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mary Anning (1799–1847) was a fossil collector in Lyme Regis, England.<br>• She discovered the first complete plesiosaur skeleton in 1823.<br>• Scientists of her time often published her findings without crediting her.<br>• As a woman, she was barred from joining the Geological Society of London.<br>• In 2010, the Royal Society named her one of the ten British women who most influenced the history of science.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the way Anning was treated in her lifetime with the recognition she later received. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mary Anning, a fossil collector from Lyme Regis, discovered the first complete plesiosaur skeleton in 1823, when she was in her twenties.","Barred from the Geological Society and often uncredited in life, Anning was honored by the Royal Society in 2010.","In 2010, the Royal Society named Mary Anning, who discovered the first complete plesiosaur skeleton, one of ten influential British women in science.","Scientists of Mary Anning's time often published her findings without credit, and she was barred from the Geological Society of London."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to contrast her treatment during her life with her later recognition. Only the first choice includes both. The third and fourth each give only one side.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In a study, participants were asked to remember a seven-digit number.<br>• While holding the number in mind, they were offered a choice between fruit salad and chocolate cake.<br>• Participants remembering the number chose cake 63% of the time.<br>• A control group asked to remember a two-digit number chose cake 41% of the time.<br>• Researchers suggested that mental strain reduces self-control.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's findings and the conclusion the researchers drew from them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["People holding seven digits in mind chose cake 63% of the time versus 41% for two digits, suggesting strain weakens self-control.","In a study, participants remembering a seven-digit number were offered a choice between fruit salad and chocolate cake.","Researchers who studied people choosing between fruit salad and chocolate cake suggested that mental strain may reduce self-control.","Participants who were asked to remember a seven-digit number chose chocolate cake over fruit salad 63% of the time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present the evidence and conclusion together. Only the first choice gives both groups' results and the researchers' interpretation. The fourth gives one group's result without the comparison.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The ancient Egyptians built the Great Pyramid of Giza around 2560 BCE.<br>• It was the tallest human-made structure in the world for about 3,800 years.<br>• It was originally covered in polished white limestone casing stones.<br>• Most of the casing stones were removed in the 1300s CE and reused in buildings in Cairo.<br>• Today, the pyramid's exposed core blocks give it a stepped appearance.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Great Pyramid looks different today than it did when it was completed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Built around 2560 BCE, the Great Pyramid of Giza was the tallest human-made structure in the world for about 3,800 years.","The Great Pyramid of Giza was originally covered in polished white limestone casing stones, which made it the tallest human-made structure for centuries.","In the 1300s CE, builders in Cairo reused stones taken from the Great Pyramid of Giza, which had been built around 2560 BCE.","The Great Pyramid's stepped look today results from the removal of most of its smooth white limestone casing in the 1300s CE for use in Cairo's buildings."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain why the pyramid looks different today. Only the first choice links the removal of the casing stones to its current stepped appearance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested whether dogs understand human pointing.<br>• Dogs were shown two cups, one of which hid food; a person pointed to the correct cup.<br>• Adult dogs chose the correct cup about 70% of the time.<br>• Eight-week-old puppies with little human contact also chose correctly about 70% of the time.<br>• Researchers concluded that the ability may be largely inherited rather than learned.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the evidence that led the researchers to their conclusion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Adult dogs shown two cups, one hiding food, chose the cup that a person pointed to about 70% of the time.","Researchers tested whether dogs and young puppies could find food hidden under one of two cups when a person pointed to it.","Puppies with little human contact read pointing as well as adult dogs (about 70%), so the ability may be largely inherited.","Researchers concluded that dogs' ability to understand human pointing may be largely inherited rather than learned through experience."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to present the evidence for the conclusion. Only the first choice explains that inexperienced puppies performed as well as adults, which is what supports the claim that the ability is inherited.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Great Molasses Flood occurred in Boston on January 15, 1919.<br>• A storage tank holding about 8.7 million liters of molasses burst.<br>• A wave of molasses up to 7.6 meters high swept through the streets at about 56 kilometers per hour.<br>• Twenty-one people were killed.<br>• Investigators found that the tank had been poorly built and never properly tested.$q$, $q$The student wants to identify the cause of the Great Molasses Flood. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["On January 15, 1919, a wave of molasses up to 7.6 meters high swept through Boston's streets at about 56 kilometers per hour.","The 1919 Great Molasses Flood, which killed twenty-one people in Boston, occurred because a poorly built, never properly tested storage tank burst.","In 1919, a storage tank in Boston burst and released about 8.7 million liters of molasses, killing twenty-one people.","Investigators studied the Great Molasses Flood, in which a storage tank holding 8.7 million liters of molasses burst in Boston in 1919."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to identify the cause of the disaster. Only the first choice explains why the tank burst (poor construction and lack of testing).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Bees and other pollinators are needed for about 75% of the world's food crop species.<br>• Almonds depend entirely on honeybee pollination.<br>• California produces about 80% of the world's almonds.<br>• Each spring, beekeepers truck about 2 million hives to California's almond orchards.<br>• That is more than two-thirds of all commercial honeybee hives in the United States.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the scale of the effort required to pollinate California's almond crop. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Almond pollination requires about 2 million trucked-in hives each spring—over two-thirds of all U.S. commercial hives.","Bees and other pollinators are needed for about 75% of the world's food crop species, including almonds, which depend entirely on honeybees.","California produces about 80% of the world's almonds, a crop that depends entirely on pollination by honeybees.","Each spring, beekeepers transport hives to California, which produces about 80% of the world's almonds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to convey the scale of the almond pollination effort. Only the first choice includes the number of hives and their share of all U.S. hives.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The aye-aye is a lemur found only in Madagascar.<br>• It taps on tree bark with a long, thin middle finger.<br>• It listens for hollow spaces made by insect larvae.<br>• It then gnaws a hole and uses its finger to pull out the larvae.<br>• Woodpeckers find food in a similar way on other continents, but there are no woodpeckers in Madagascar.$q$, $q$The student wants to draw a comparison between the aye-aye and woodpeckers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The aye-aye, a lemur found only in Madagascar, taps on tree bark with a long, thin middle finger to locate insect larvae.","After locating larvae by tapping on bark, the aye-aye gnaws a hole in the tree and pulls the larvae out with its finger.","Woodpeckers, which do not live in Madagascar, find food on other continents by tapping on trees to locate insect larvae.","Madagascar has no woodpeckers, but the aye-aye finds larvae the same way: by tapping on bark."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to compare the aye-aye with woodpeckers. Only the first choice links the two, noting that the aye-aye uses a similar method where woodpeckers are absent.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Architect Zaha Hadid was born in Baghdad in 1950.<br>• In 2004, she became the first woman to win the Pritzker Architecture Prize.<br>• Her buildings are known for sweeping curves and the absence of right angles.<br>• For years, many of her designs were considered impossible to build.<br>• Advances in computer modeling eventually allowed many of them to be constructed.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how technology helped make Hadid's designs a reality. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Zaha Hadid, who was born in Baghdad in 1950, became the first woman to win the Pritzker Architecture Prize in 2004.","Zaha Hadid's buildings, known for their sweeping curves and absence of right angles, earned her the Pritzker Architecture Prize in 2004.","Hadid's curving designs, once deemed impossible to build, became buildable thanks to advances in computer modeling.","For years, many of Zaha Hadid's designs, such as those with sweeping curves, were considered impossible to build."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain how technology affected her career. Only the first choice links computer modeling to the eventual construction of her designs.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Doppler effect is the change in frequency of a wave as its source moves.<br>• As an ambulance approaches, its siren sounds higher in pitch.<br>• As it moves away, the siren sounds lower.<br>• Astronomers use the Doppler effect with light.<br>• Light from galaxies moving away from Earth shifts toward red, which shows that the universe is expanding.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Doppler effect helps astronomers learn about the universe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Doppler effect explains why an ambulance siren sounds higher in pitch as it approaches and lower as it moves away.","Astronomers apply the Doppler effect to light: redshifted light from receding galaxies shows the universe is expanding.","The Doppler effect, which is the change in a wave's frequency as its source moves, can be observed in both sound and light.","Light from galaxies moving away from Earth shifts toward red, a phenomenon that astronomers have studied extensively."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how astronomers use the Doppler effect to learn about the universe. Only the first choice links the effect to redshift and the conclusion that the universe is expanding.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Bajau people live in coastal Southeast Asia.<br>• Many Bajau free-dive for fish, spending up to five hours a day underwater.<br>• A 2018 study found that the Bajau have spleens about 50% larger than those of a nearby group that does not dive.<br>• The spleen releases oxygen-rich red blood cells during dives.<br>• The researchers identified a gene variant linked to larger spleens that is common among the Bajau.$q$, $q$The student wants to present evidence that the Bajau have a physical adaptation suited to their diving lifestyle. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Bajau, who free-dive for hours daily, have spleens about 50% larger than those of a nearby non-diving group.","The Bajau, who live in coastal Southeast Asia, free-dive for fish and may spend up to five hours a day underwater.","The spleen releases oxygen-rich red blood cells during dives, and a gene variant linked to larger spleens is common among some groups.","Researchers studying the Bajau and a nearby group that does not dive identified a gene variant linked to spleen size."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present evidence of a physical adaptation to diving. Only the first choice compares the Bajau's spleen size with that of a non-diving group and connects it to diving.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Volunteers in a citizen-science project counted birds in their backyards each February.<br>• In 2023, more than 500,000 people participated.<br>• They submitted about 380,000 checklists from 190 countries.<br>• Scientists use the data to track changes in bird populations.<br>• Data from past counts helped document the spread of the Eurasian collared-dove across North America.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide a specific example of how the project's data have been useful to scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 2023, more than 500,000 people in 190 countries took part in a citizen-science project in which they counted backyard birds.","Each February, volunteers in a citizen-science project count the birds in their backyards and submit checklists to scientists.","Volunteers submitted about 380,000 checklists from 190 countries for a 2023 bird count used by scientists to track populations.","Past counts by the project's volunteers helped scientists document the Eurasian collared-dove's spread across North America."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to give a specific example of how scientists have used the data. Only the first choice describes such a case (the collared-dove's spread).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Pantanal in South America is the world's largest tropical wetland.<br>• It covers about 150,000 square kilometers.<br>• It has one of the highest concentrations of jaguars in the world.<br>• In 2020, fires burned about 30% of the Pantanal.<br>• Scientists estimate that about 17 million vertebrate animals died in the fires.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the severity of the 2020 fires in the Pantanal. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Pantanal, the world's largest tropical wetland, covers about 150,000 square kilometers and is home to many jaguars.","The Pantanal in South America has one of the highest concentrations of jaguars in the world, and fires burned parts of it in 2020.","In 2020, fires burned about 30% of the Pantanal, the world's largest tropical wetland, killing an estimated 17 million vertebrate animals.","Covering about 150,000 square kilometers, the Pantanal in South America is the largest tropical wetland on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to emphasize the severity of the 2020 fires. Only the first choice gives both the extent of the burned area and the number of animals killed.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Novelist Kazuo Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki, Japan, in 1954.<br>• His family moved to England when he was five.<br>• His novel The Remains of the Day (1989) is narrated by an English butler.<br>• Never Let Me Go (2005) is narrated by a young woman in a boarding school with a dark secret.<br>• Both narrators gradually reveal truths they have avoided facing.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the narrators of the two novels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kazuo Ishiguro, born in Nagasaki in 1954, wrote The Remains of the Day, which is narrated by an English butler.","Ishiguro's butler and boarding-school narrators share a habit: both slowly reveal truths they have avoided facing.","The Remains of the Day (1989) is narrated by an English butler, while Never Let Me Go (2005) is narrated by a young woman at a boarding school.","Kazuo Ishiguro's novel Never Let Me Go, published in 2005, is narrated by a young woman at a boarding school with a dark secret."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to identify a similarity between the novels' narrators. Only the first choice states what the narrators have in common. The third choice describes only how they differ.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers placed small cameras on the backs of emperor penguins.<br>• The penguins dove to depths of more than 500 meters.<br>• During dives, their heart rates dropped from about 70 beats per minute to as low as 6.<br>• This slowing reduced the amount of oxygen their bodies used.<br>• It allowed them to stay underwater for more than 20 minutes.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how emperor penguins are able to remain underwater for long periods. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Slowing their hearts from about 70 to as few as 6 beats per minute saves oxygen, letting penguins dive for over 20 minutes.","Emperor penguins fitted with small cameras dove to depths of more than 500 meters and stayed underwater for more than 20 minutes.","During dives, emperor penguins' heart rates dropped from about 70 beats per minute to as low as 6, researchers found.","Researchers placed small cameras on the backs of emperor penguins, which dove to depths of more than 500 meters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain how penguins can stay underwater so long. Only the first choice links the slowing heart rate to conserving oxygen and to the length of dives.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Rosetta Stone was discovered in Egypt in 1799.<br>• It contains the same decree in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek.<br>• Scholars could already read ancient Greek.<br>• In 1822, Jean-François Champollion used the Greek text to help decipher the hieroglyphs.<br>• Before this, hieroglyphs had been unreadable for more than a thousand years.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Rosetta Stone was key to deciphering hieroglyphs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Rosetta Stone, discovered in Egypt in 1799, contains a decree written in hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek scripts.","In 1822, Jean-François Champollion deciphered Egyptian hieroglyphs, which had been unreadable for more than a thousand years.","Scholars who studied the Rosetta Stone after its discovery in 1799 could already read ancient Greek, one of its three scripts.","Since the stone repeated its decree in readable Greek, Champollion could use it to decipher hieroglyphs in 1822."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain why the stone was useful for deciphering hieroglyphs. Only the first choice connects the readable Greek text to Champollion's decipherment.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A city introduced a fee of $15 for cars entering its downtown during peak hours.<br>• In the first year, traffic downtown fell by about 18%.<br>• Average bus speeds increased by 10%.<br>• Revenue from the fee, about $500 million, was used to improve public transit.<br>• Some downtown business owners initially opposed the fee, fearing fewer customers.$q$, $q$The student wants to present evidence that the fee reduced traffic congestion. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A city introduced a $15 fee for cars entering downtown at peak hours, despite initial opposition from some business owners.","Revenue from the city's $15 downtown entry fee, about $500 million, was used to improve public transit.","After the city began charging cars $15 to enter downtown at peak hours, traffic fell about 18% and bus speeds rose 10% in the first year.","Some downtown business owners initially opposed the city's $15 peak-hour fee for cars, fearing it would reduce customers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to show the fee's effect on traffic. Only the first choice gives the traffic and bus-speed results.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Scientists sequenced the genome of the coelacanth, a fish once thought extinct.<br>• Coelacanths were known only from fossils until a living one was caught off South Africa in 1938.<br>• Their genes have changed more slowly than those of most other vertebrates.<br>• Coelacanths have fleshy, lobed fins with bones similar to those in limbs.<br>• They are more closely related to land animals than most fish are.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the coelacanth is of interest to scientists studying how land animals evolved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Scientists who sequenced the coelacanth's genome found that its genes have changed more slowly than those of most other vertebrates.","Coelacanths have limb-like bones in their lobed fins and are closer relatives of land animals than most fish are.","The coelacanth, a fish that was once thought to be extinct, has been studied by scientists who sequenced its genome.","Until a living coelacanth was caught off South Africa in 1938, scientists knew the fish only from fossils and believed it extinct."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why the coelacanth interests scientists who study the evolution of land animals. Only the first choice mentions the limb-like fins and the close relationship to land animals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Phillis Wheatley was born in West Africa around 1753.<br>• She was kidnapped and enslaved in Boston as a child.<br>• In 1773, she published Poems on Various Subjects, Religious and Moral.<br>• It was the first book of poetry published by an African American.<br>• Some colonists doubted she had written the poems, so a panel of prominent Bostonians examined her and signed a statement confirming her authorship.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize an obstacle Wheatley faced in having her authorship accepted, along with the significance of her book. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Wheatley's 1773 book, the first by an African American poet, appeared only after Boston leaders vouched for her authorship.","Phillis Wheatley, born in West Africa around 1753, was kidnapped and enslaved in Boston as a child.","Phillis Wheatley published Poems on Various Subjects, Religious and Moral in 1773, after being enslaved in Boston as a child.","Some colonists doubted that Phillis Wheatley had written the poems in Poems on Various Subjects, Religious and Moral."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to show the obstacles Wheatley faced in being recognized as an author. Only the first choice includes both her achievement and the examination she had to undergo.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Traditional concrete production releases large amounts of carbon dioxide.<br>• Cement production accounts for about 8% of global carbon dioxide emissions.<br>• A startup has developed concrete that absorbs carbon dioxide as it hardens.<br>• Its concrete stores about 25 kilograms of carbon dioxide per cubic meter.<br>• The new concrete costs about the same as traditional concrete.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that the new concrete could be a practical alternative to traditional concrete. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cement production accounts for about 8% of global carbon dioxide emissions, so traditional concrete production releases large amounts of carbon dioxide.","A startup's concrete stores about 25 kilograms of carbon dioxide per cubic meter as it hardens.","A startup has developed a new concrete that costs about the same as traditional concrete, which is made using cement.","Unlike traditional concrete, whose cement accounts for about 8% of global CO₂ emissions, a startup's new concrete absorbs CO₂ as it hardens—and costs about the same."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to emphasize that the new concrete is both environmentally better and affordable. Only the first choice contrasts its carbon absorption with traditional concrete's emissions and notes its comparable cost.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied how long it takes people to recognize faces.<br>• Participants were shown photos for different lengths of time.<br>• With photos shown for 100 milliseconds, participants recognized familiar faces 90% of the time.<br>• With photos shown for 33 milliseconds, recognition dropped to 60%.<br>• Recognition of unfamiliar faces was below 50% at both durations.$q$, $q$The student wants to compare how well participants recognized familiar faces versus unfamiliar faces. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants recognized familiar faces 90% of the time when photos were shown for 100 milliseconds but only 60% when shown for 33 milliseconds.","Researchers showed participants photos of familiar and unfamiliar faces for 100 milliseconds and for 33 milliseconds.","Familiar faces were recognized at 90% and 60% rates, but unfamiliar faces stayed below 50% at both durations.","Recognition of unfamiliar faces was below 50% whether photos were shown for 100 milliseconds or 33 milliseconds."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to compare familiar and unfamiliar faces. Only the first choice gives results for both types. The second compares durations rather than familiarity.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The longest-living known vertebrate is the Greenland shark.<br>• Researchers estimated ages by radiocarbon dating proteins in the sharks' eye lenses.<br>• The oldest shark studied was estimated to be about 392 years old.<br>• Greenland sharks grow about 1 centimeter per year.<br>• They do not reach reproductive maturity until about age 150.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how slowly Greenland sharks grow and mature. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers estimated the ages of Greenland sharks by radiocarbon dating proteins in the lenses of their eyes.","Greenland sharks grow only about a centimeter a year and do not mature until about 150, and one was estimated to be some 392 years old.","The Greenland shark, the longest-living known vertebrate, was studied by researchers using radiocarbon dating.","Using radiocarbon dating of eye-lens proteins, researchers estimated that the oldest Greenland shark they studied was about 392 years old."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to emphasize the shark's slow pace of life. Only the first choice mentions the slow growth and very late maturity, along with its great age.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Henrietta Lacks died of cervical cancer in 1951.<br>• Doctors took samples of her cancer cells without her knowledge or consent.<br>• Her cells, called HeLa cells, were the first human cells to survive and reproduce indefinitely in a lab.<br>• HeLa cells were used to develop the polio vaccine and in thousands of other studies.<br>• Her family did not learn about the cells until the 1970s.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight both the scientific importance of HeLa cells and the ethical concerns surrounding them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Taken without consent in 1951, HeLa cells helped create the polio vaccine, yet Lacks's family learned of them only in the 1970s.","HeLa cells, taken from Henrietta Lacks in 1951, were the first human cells to survive and reproduce indefinitely in a lab.","HeLa cells have been used in thousands of studies, including research that led to the polio vaccine.","Doctors took samples of Henrietta Lacks's cancer cells in 1951 without her knowledge or consent."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to highlight both the scientific value and the ethical problem. Only the first choice includes the cells' contributions and the lack of consent and disclosure.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested a new antibiotic on mice infected with drug-resistant bacteria.<br>• Mice receiving the antibiotic had a 95% survival rate.<br>• Mice receiving no treatment had a 10% survival rate.<br>• The antibiotic has not yet been tested in humans.<br>• Researchers expect human trials to begin in three years.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the antibiotic's promising results while acknowledging a limitation of the research. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mice infected with drug-resistant bacteria and given a new antibiotic had a 95% survival rate, compared with 10% for untreated mice.","Researchers expect human trials of a new antibiotic, which was tested on mice infected with drug-resistant bacteria, to begin in three years.","A new antibiotic tested on mice infected with drug-resistant bacteria has not yet been tested in humans.","The antibiotic raised infected mice's survival to 95% (versus 10% untreated) but has not yet been tested in humans."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to present the promising result while noting a limitation. Only the first choice includes both. The second gives only the result.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Gothic cathedrals in Europe used flying buttresses.<br>• A flying buttress is an external arch that transfers the weight of the roof to a pier outside the building.<br>• Earlier churches needed thick walls to support the roof.<br>• Flying buttresses allowed walls to be thinner and taller.<br>• Thinner walls could contain large stained-glass windows.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how flying buttresses made large stained-glass windows possible. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A flying buttress is an external arch that transfers the weight of a cathedral's roof to a pier outside the building.","Gothic cathedrals, with their large stained-glass windows, used flying buttresses, unlike earlier churches, which had thick walls.","Flying buttresses moved the roof's weight to outside piers, allowing thinner walls with room for large stained-glass windows.","Earlier churches needed thick walls to support their roofs, while Gothic cathedrals had thinner, taller walls."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain how flying buttresses made large windows possible. Only the first choice traces the chain from transferred weight to thinner walls to room for windows.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The first successful human heart transplant was performed in 1967 in South Africa.<br>• The patient lived for eighteen days.<br>• Early transplant patients often died because their immune systems rejected the new organ.<br>• The drug cyclosporine, approved in 1983, suppressed rejection.<br>• After cyclosporine, one-year survival rates for heart transplant patients rose above 80%.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how cyclosporine changed outcomes for heart transplant patients. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The first successful human heart transplant was performed in South Africa in 1967, and the patient lived for eighteen days.","Organ rejection killed many early heart transplant patients, but after cyclosporine (1983), one-year survival topped 80%.","Cyclosporine, a drug approved in 1983, suppresses the immune system's rejection of a transplanted organ.","Since the first heart transplant in 1967, survival rates for heart transplant patients have risen above 80%."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how a drug changed outcomes. Only the first choice connects the problem (rejection), the drug, and the improved survival.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A 2016 study examined three groups of commuters.<br>• Those who walked or cycled to work reported the highest satisfaction with their commutes.<br>• Those who took public transit reported moderate satisfaction.<br>• Those who drove reported the lowest satisfaction.<br>• Commute length had less effect on satisfaction than mode of travel.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the study's finding about which factor most affected commuters' satisfaction. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In a 2016 study, travel mode affected commuters' satisfaction more than commute length did.","A 2016 study found that commuters who walked or cycled to work were the most satisfied with their commutes.","Commuters who drove to work reported the lowest satisfaction, while those who took public transit reported moderate satisfaction.","A 2016 study examined the satisfaction of commuters who walked or cycled, took public transit, or drove to work."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize the study's finding about mode versus length. Only the first choice includes that comparison along with the ranking.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Japanese artist Katsushika Hokusai created the woodblock print The Great Wave off Kanagawa around 1831.<br>• It was part of a series called Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji.<br>• Thousands of copies were printed.<br>• After Japan opened to trade in the 1850s, the print became popular in Europe.<br>• It influenced European artists such as Claude Monet and Vincent van Gogh.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the influence of The Great Wave off Kanagawa beyond Japan. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Around 1831, Hokusai created The Great Wave off Kanagawa as part of his series Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji.","Thousands of copies of Hokusai's The Great Wave off Kanagawa, a woodblock print from around 1831, were printed.","The Great Wave off Kanagawa, a print in Hokusai's series Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji, is one of the most famous Japanese artworks.","Hokusai's The Great Wave became popular in Europe after the 1850s and influenced Monet and van Gogh."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to describe the print's impact outside Japan. Only the first choice describes its popularity in Europe and its influence on European artists.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sleep researchers studied the effects of a 20-minute afternoon nap.<br>• Participants who napped scored 34% higher on an alertness test than those who did not.<br>• Participants who napped for 60 minutes woke feeling groggy.<br>• This grogginess, called sleep inertia, occurs when a person wakes from deep sleep.<br>• Deep sleep typically begins after about 30 minutes.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why a shorter nap may be more beneficial than a longer one. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants who took a 20-minute afternoon nap scored 34% higher on an alertness test than participants who did not nap.","Grogginess after waking from deep sleep, which typically begins after about 30 minutes, is called sleep inertia.","Twenty-minute nappers gained 34% in alertness, but 60-minute nappers, likely reaching deep sleep, woke groggy.","Researchers compared the effects of afternoon naps lasting 20 minutes with those of naps lasting 60 minutes."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain why a shorter nap may be better than a longer one. Only the first choice contrasts both nap lengths and links grogginess to deep sleep.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The ancient Greek Antikythera mechanism was found in a shipwreck in 1901.<br>• It dates to about 100 BCE.<br>• It contains at least thirty interlocking bronze gears.<br>• It could predict eclipses and track the positions of the Sun and Moon.<br>• No device of comparable complexity is known until mechanical clocks appeared in Europe more than a thousand years later.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far ahead of its time the Antikythera mechanism was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Found in a shipwreck in 1901, the Antikythera mechanism contains at least thirty interlocking bronze gears.","Nothing as complex as the Antikythera mechanism (c. 100 BCE) is known for over a thousand years after it.","The Antikythera mechanism, which dates to about 100 BCE, could predict eclipses and track the positions of the Sun and Moon.","Mechanical clocks appeared in Europe more than a thousand years after the Antikythera mechanism was made around 100 BCE."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to emphasize how advanced the device was for its time. Only the first choice states both what it did and that nothing comparable existed for more than a millennium.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied eye contact between dogs and their owners.<br>• When dogs and owners gazed at each other, levels of the hormone oxytocin rose in both.<br>• Oxytocin is associated with bonding between mothers and infants.<br>• Wolves raised by humans did not show the same oxytocin increase.<br>• The researchers suggested that this bonding mechanism developed during dog domestication.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the researchers concluded that the bonding mechanism developed during domestication. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Dogs and owners, but not human-raised wolves, showed oxytocin rises during mutual gaze, pointing to domestication.","When dogs and their owners gazed at each other, levels of oxytocin, a hormone associated with bonding, rose in both.","Oxytocin, a hormone associated with bonding between mothers and infants, rose in dogs and their owners during eye contact.","Researchers who studied dogs and wolves raised by humans examined whether eye contact affected oxytocin levels."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain why researchers linked the effect to domestication. Only the first choice includes the comparison with wolves, which is the evidence for that conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Composer Fanny Mendelssohn (1805–1847) wrote more than 460 pieces of music.<br>• Her father told her that music could be a career for her brother Felix but only an "ornament" for her.<br>• Some of her songs were published under Felix's name.<br>• In 2010, her Easter Sonata, long attributed to Felix, was identified as her work.<br>• Her music is now regularly performed and recorded.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast how Fanny Mendelssohn's work was credited in the past with how it is regarded today. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fanny Mendelssohn wrote more than 460 pieces, though her father considered music only an \"ornament\" for her.","Some of Fanny Mendelssohn's songs were published under the name of her brother, the composer Felix Mendelssohn.","In 2010, a sonata that had long been attributed to Felix Mendelssohn was identified as the work of his sister Fanny.","Long credited partly to her brother—her Easter Sonata was identified as hers only in 2010—Fanny's music is now widely performed."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to contrast the past misattribution of her work with her present recognition. Only the first choice includes both.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1854, cholera killed more than 600 people in London's Soho district.<br>• Physician John Snow mapped the locations of deaths.<br>• Most deaths clustered around a public water pump on Broad Street.<br>• Snow persuaded officials to remove the pump's handle.<br>• New cases soon declined, supporting Snow's theory that cholera spread through contaminated water.$q$, $q$The student wants to summarize how John Snow's investigation supported his theory about cholera. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1854, cholera killed more than 600 people in London's Soho district, where John Snow was a physician.","John Snow persuaded officials in 1854 to remove the handle of a public water pump on Broad Street in London's Soho district.","Snow mapped Soho's 1854 cholera deaths, found them clustered at one pump, and saw cases fall once its handle was removed.","John Snow, a physician in London, believed that cholera spread through contaminated water rather than through the air."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to summarize how Snow's investigation supported his theory. Only the first choice includes his method (mapping), his finding (clustering), and the result of his intervention.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Oak trees produce acorns in highly variable amounts.<br>• In "mast years," all the oaks in a region produce enormous crops at the same time.<br>• In other years, they produce very few acorns.<br>• Scientists think mast years overwhelm seed-eating animals, so some acorns survive to grow.<br>• Mast years also cause spikes in mouse populations, which can increase the number of ticks carrying Lyme disease.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a way in which mast years can affect human health. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In mast years, all the oaks in a region produce enormous crops of acorns at the same time, overwhelming seed-eating animals.","Mast years can have effects far beyond oaks: abundant acorns cause mouse populations to spike, which can increase ticks carrying Lyme disease.","Scientists think oaks produce huge acorn crops in mast years so that seed-eating animals are overwhelmed and some acorns survive.","Oak trees produce acorns in highly variable amounts, with enormous crops in mast years and very few in other years."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to describe a consequence of mast years for human health. Only the first choice traces the link from acorns to mice to Lyme-disease ticks.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Hedy Lamarr (1914–2000) was a Hollywood film star.<br>• During World War II, she co-invented a "frequency-hopping" system with composer George Antheil.<br>• It was designed to keep enemies from jamming radio-guided torpedoes.<br>• The U.S. Navy did not adopt it during the war.<br>• Frequency hopping later became a foundation for technologies such as Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the unexpected and lasting significance of Lamarr's invention. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lamarr, a film star, co-invented frequency hopping; ignored by the Navy, it later underpinned Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.","During World War II, Hollywood star Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil co-invented a frequency-hopping system for torpedoes.","Frequency hopping, which was designed to keep enemies from jamming radio-guided torpedoes, became a foundation for Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.","The U.S. Navy did not adopt the frequency-hopping system that Hedy Lamarr co-invented with George Antheil during World War II."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize the surprising long-term importance of an invention by someone known for something else. Only the first choice identifies her fame, the invention's early neglect, and its later impact.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers grew two groups of tomato plants in a greenhouse.<br>• One group was exposed to recordings of caterpillars chewing leaves.<br>• The other group was exposed to silence.<br>• Later, when real caterpillars were introduced, the first group produced more defensive chemicals.<br>• Researchers concluded that plants may respond to sounds associated with threats.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the key result of the experiment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers grew two groups of tomato plants, exposing one to recordings of caterpillars chewing leaves and the other to silence.","Researchers concluded from a greenhouse experiment with tomato plants that plants may respond to sounds associated with threats.","When caterpillars were introduced to a greenhouse, tomato plants produced defensive chemicals to protect their leaves.","When real caterpillars arrived, plants that had heard chewing recordings made more defensive chemicals than plants kept in silence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to present the key result. Only the first choice compares the two groups' responses to real caterpillars.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers compared two methods of studying for a vocabulary test.<br>• Group 1 reread the word list four times.<br>• Group 2 read the list once and then quizzed themselves three times.<br>• One week later, Group 1 remembered 36% of the words.<br>• Group 2 remembered 61% of the words.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the study's results to recommend one study method over the other. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers compared two groups: Group 1 reread a vocabulary list four times, while Group 2 read it once and then quizzed themselves three times before the test.","One week after studying, students who had reread a vocabulary list four times remembered 36% of the words.","Self-quizzing beat rereading: a week later, students who quizzed themselves recalled 61% of words versus 36%.","Researchers compared rereading and self-quizzing, two common methods that students use to study vocabulary."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to recommend one method based on the results. Only the first choice compares the outcomes of the two methods.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The kākāpō is a flightless parrot native to New Zealand.<br>• It is nocturnal and can live for up to 90 years.<br>• By 1995, only 51 kākāpō remained.<br>• Conservationists moved all of them to predator-free islands and monitored every nest.<br>• By 2022, the population had grown to 252.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the success of conservation efforts for the kākāpō. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The kākāpō, a flightless and nocturnal parrot native to New Zealand, can live for up to 90 years.","Moving every remaining kākāpō to predator-free islands and monitoring each nest helped the population grow from 51 in 1995 to 252 by 2022.","By 1995, only 51 kākāpō—flightless, nocturnal parrots native to New Zealand that can live for up to 90 years—remained, and conservationists moved them to islands.","Conservationists moved all of New Zealand's kākāpō, a flightless parrot species, to islands free of predators."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to show that conservation efforts worked. Only the first choice links the efforts to the population increase.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Bread mold, Penicillium, and mushrooms are all fungi.<br>• Fungi were once classified as plants.<br>• Unlike plants, fungi cannot make their own food through photosynthesis.<br>• Fungi absorb nutrients from their surroundings.<br>• Genetic studies show that fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why fungi are no longer classified as plants. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though once classified as plants, fungi cannot photosynthesize and are genetically closer to animals than to plants.","Fungi, including bread mold, Penicillium, and mushrooms, absorb nutrients from their surroundings, and they were once classified as plants by scientists.","Fungi such as mushrooms and bread mold were once classified as plants, the organisms that make food by photosynthesis.","Bread mold, Penicillium, and mushrooms are all fungi, which absorb nutrients from their surroundings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain why fungi are no longer considered plants. Only the first choice gives both reasons: they don't photosynthesize and they're genetically closer to animals.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 1960, Jane Goodall began studying chimpanzees in Gombe, Tanzania.<br>• She observed a chimpanzee stripping leaves from a twig and using it to fish termites from a mound.<br>• At the time, toolmaking was considered a defining trait of humans.<br>• Her mentor, Louis Leakey, responded: "Now we must redefine tool, redefine man, or accept chimpanzees as humans."<br>• Goodall's research continued for more than sixty years.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Goodall's observation was significant. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1960, Jane Goodall began studying chimpanzees in Gombe, Tanzania, where she observed one stripping leaves from a twig to fish for termites; her research lasted sixty years.","Jane Goodall observed a chimpanzee in Gombe, Tanzania, using a twig to fish termites from a mound.","At the time Jane Goodall began her research in 1960, toolmaking was considered a defining trait of humans.","Goodall's 1960 observation of a chimpanzee shaping a twig to fish for termites challenged the belief that only humans make tools."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain the significance of her observation. Only the first choice connects what she saw to the challenge it posed to prevailing beliefs.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Sahara Desert covers about 9 million square kilometers.<br>• About 6,000 to 11,000 years ago, it was much wetter, a period called the African Humid Period.<br>• During this time, it had lakes, grasslands, and animals such as hippos and giraffes.<br>• Rock art from the period shows people herding cattle and swimming.<br>• Shifts in Earth's orbit are thought to have caused the change to desert.$q$, $q$The student wants to use evidence left by ancient people to show that the Sahara was once wetter. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Sahara Desert, which today covers about 9 million square kilometers, once had lakes, grasslands, and animals such as hippos and giraffes during the African Humid Period.","Shifts in Earth's orbit are thought to have changed the Sahara from a wetter landscape into the desert it is today.","Rock art showing people swimming and herding cattle reflects a time, 6,000 to 11,000 years ago, when the Sahara held lakes and grasslands.","About 6,000 to 11,000 years ago, the Sahara Desert was much wetter than it is today."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to use evidence from human artifacts to show the Sahara was once wetter. Only the first choice connects the rock art to the wetter period.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Engineers designed a building in Zimbabwe called the Eastgate Centre.<br>• Its ventilation system was inspired by termite mounds.<br>• Termite mounds keep a stable internal temperature using a network of air channels.<br>• The Eastgate Centre has no conventional air conditioning.<br>• It uses about 10% of the energy of similar-sized buildings for climate control.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a practical benefit of the Eastgate Centre's nature-inspired design. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Eastgate Centre, a building in Zimbabwe, was designed without a conventional air-conditioning system.","Inspired by termite mounds, the Eastgate Centre's ventilation uses about 10% of the climate-control energy of similar buildings.","Termite mounds use a network of air channels to keep their internal temperature stable.","Engineers designed Zimbabwe's Eastgate Centre, which has no conventional air conditioning, with a ventilation system inspired by the air channels in termite mounds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to show the practical benefit of imitating nature. Only the first choice links the termite-inspired design to its energy savings.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Two researchers studied the effect of class size on student achievement.<br>• Researcher A found that students in classes of 15 scored higher than those in classes of 25.<br>• Researcher B found no significant difference between classes of 22 and 25.<br>• Researcher A's study included 11,000 students over four years.<br>• Researcher B's study included 800 students over one year.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the two findings along with details that might explain why they differ. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researcher A, studying 11,000 students over four years, found an advantage for classes of 15 over 25; Researcher B, studying 800 students for one year, found none between 22 and 25.","Researcher A found that students in classes of 15 scored higher than students in classes of 25.","Researchers A and B both studied how class size affects student achievement; Researcher A found that classes of 15 outscored classes of 25, while B found no significant difference.","Researcher B found no significant difference in achievement between students in classes of 22 and classes of 25."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain why the studies' different conclusions might not conflict. The first choice shows that they examined different class sizes and differed in scale, which could account for the different results.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sign languages are full languages with their own grammar.<br>• American Sign Language (ASL) and British Sign Language (BSL) are not mutually intelligible.<br>• ASL is more closely related to French Sign Language.<br>• A French teacher, Laurent Clerc, helped found the first American school for the deaf in 1817.<br>• ASL developed from a mix of French Sign Language and local sign systems.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a surprising fact about the history of American Sign Language. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["American Sign Language and British Sign Language are both full languages with their own grammar, and each is used by deaf communities in a country where English is spoken.","Laurent Clerc, a teacher from France, helped found the first American school for the deaf in 1817.","ASL developed from a mix of French Sign Language and local sign systems and has its own grammar.","Despite English being spoken in both countries, ASL is unrelated to BSL; it descends partly from French Sign Language, brought by teacher Laurent Clerc in 1817."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain a surprising fact about ASL's origins. Only the first choice highlights the surprise (ASL unrelated to BSL despite shared English) and explains the French connection.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 2004, researchers found the fossil of Tiktaalik roseae in the Canadian Arctic.<br>• It lived about 375 million years ago.<br>• It had gills and scales like a fish.<br>• It also had a flexible neck and sturdy fins with wrist-like bones.<br>• Scientists consider it a transitional form between fish and four-legged land animals.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why scientists consider Tiktaalik a transitional form. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tiktaalik roseae, which lived about 375 million years ago, was found in the Canadian Arctic in 2004.","Tiktaalik roseae, found in the Canadian Arctic in 2004 and dated to about 375 million years ago, had sturdy fins with wrist-like bones and a flexible neck.","With fish-like gills and scales but also a neck and wrist-like fin bones, Tiktaalik shows features of both fish and land animals.","Scientists studying a 375-million-year-old fossil found in 2004 consider Tiktaalik a transitional form."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain why Tiktaalik is considered transitional. Only the first choice shows it has both fish and land-animal features. The last choice states the conclusion without the evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers examined the effect of houseplants on indoor air quality.<br>• In a sealed chamber, plants removed some pollutants over 24 hours.<br>• Typical homes exchange their indoor air with outdoor air several times a day.<br>• Researchers calculated that a home would need 10 to 1,000 plants per square meter to match the air cleaning of normal ventilation.<br>• They concluded that houseplants have little measurable effect on air quality in real homes.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why results from sealed-chamber experiments are misleading about houseplants in real homes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In a sealed chamber, houseplants removed some pollutants over 24 hours, while typical homes exchange their indoor air with outdoor air several times a day through ventilation.","Though plants cleaned air in sealed chambers, homes would need 10 to 1,000 per square meter to match normal ventilation, so plants do little in real homes.","Researchers examined how houseplants affect indoor air quality, including in sealed chambers.","Typical homes exchange their indoor air with outdoor air several times a day through normal ventilation."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why lab results don't apply to homes. Only the first choice contrasts the lab result with the number of plants needed in a real home.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Poet Emily Dickinson (1830–1886) wrote nearly 1,800 poems.<br>• Fewer than a dozen were published during her lifetime.<br>• Editors of those poems often changed her punctuation, especially her frequent dashes.<br>• After her death, her sister found her manuscripts.<br>• A 1955 edition by Thomas H. Johnson was the first to restore her original punctuation.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how readers eventually came to see Dickinson's poems with her original punctuation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Dickinson's distinctive dashes, often removed by early editors, weren't restored in print until Thomas H. Johnson's 1955 edition.","Emily Dickinson wrote nearly 1,800 poems, but fewer than a dozen were published during her lifetime, and her sister found her manuscripts only after her death in 1886.","After Emily Dickinson's death in 1886, her sister found the manuscripts of her nearly 1,800 poems.","Editors who published Emily Dickinson's poems during her lifetime worked on fewer than a dozen of them."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain how readers came to see her poems as she wrote them. Only the first choice describes the editors' changes and the later restoration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Earth's magnetic field has reversed many times.<br>• During a reversal, magnetic north and south switch places.<br>• The last full reversal occurred about 780,000 years ago.<br>• Evidence comes from volcanic rocks, which record the direction of the magnetic field when they cool.<br>• Reversals take an average of about 7,000 years to complete.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how scientists know that Earth's magnetic field has reversed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Earth's magnetic field has reversed many times, most recently about 780,000 years ago.","During a reversal of Earth's magnetic field, magnetic north and south switch places, a process that takes about 7,000 years and last occurred about 780,000 years ago.","The last full reversal of Earth's magnetic field occurred about 780,000 years ago and took thousands of years.","Volcanic rocks record the magnetic field's direction as they cool, preserving evidence of past reversals."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain how scientists know about past reversals. Only the first choice describes the evidence (volcanic rocks) and how it records the field.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The ancient city of Great Zimbabwe was built between the 11th and 15th centuries.<br>• Its stone walls were built without mortar.<br>• Some walls are 11 meters high.<br>• Gold, Chinese porcelain, and Persian pottery have been found at the site.<br>• European colonial officials in the 1800s falsely claimed it had been built by outsiders, not by Africans.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide evidence that Great Zimbabwe had trade connections with distant regions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Great Zimbabwe, built between the 11th and 15th centuries, has mortarless stone walls up to 11 meters high, and gold has been found among the ruins of the site.","European officials in the 1800s falsely claimed that Great Zimbabwe, with its mortarless walls, was built by outsiders.","Finds of Chinese porcelain and Persian pottery at Great Zimbabwe point to trade ties reaching far beyond Africa.","Gold has been found at Great Zimbabwe, a city built between the 11th and 15th centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to provide evidence of long-distance trade. Only the first choice cites goods from China and Persia and draws that conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Velcro was invented by Swiss engineer George de Mestral.<br>• In 1941, after a walk, he noticed burrs stuck to his dog's fur.<br>• Under a microscope, he saw that the burrs had tiny hooks.<br>• He spent years developing a fastener with hooks on one strip and loops on another.<br>• Velcro was patented in 1955.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how an observation of nature led to the invention of Velcro. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Swiss engineer George de Mestral patented Velcro in 1955, having spent years developing a fastener with hooks on one strip and loops on another strip.","George de Mestral modeled Velcro's hook-and-loop design on the tiny hooks he saw on burrs stuck to his dog's fur.","In 1941, George de Mestral noticed burrs stuck to his dog's fur after taking a walk in Switzerland.","Velcro, patented in 1955, has hooks on one strip and loops on another strip."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how nature inspired the invention. Only the first choice connects the burrs' hooks to Velcro's design.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers measured the stress hormone cortisol in hospital nurses.<br>• Nurses working 12-hour shifts had higher average cortisol levels than nurses working 8-hour shifts.<br>• Nurses working 12-hour shifts reported higher job satisfaction.<br>• They worked fewer days per week.<br>• They said the extra days off helped them recover.$q$, $q$The student wants to present both a drawback and an advantage of 12-hour shifts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although 12-hour-shift nurses had higher cortisol, they reported greater job satisfaction, citing extra days off.","Nurses working 12-hour shifts had higher average cortisol levels than nurses working 8-hour shifts.","Nurses working 12-hour shifts worked fewer days per week and said the extra days off helped them recover.","Researchers measured cortisol, a stress hormone, in nurses working 12-hour and 8-hour shifts."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present a complicated picture with a downside and an upside. Only the first choice includes both the higher stress and the higher satisfaction.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The ocean absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide humans release.<br>• This makes seawater more acidic.<br>• Since the 1800s, ocean acidity has increased by about 30%.<br>• Acidic water makes it harder for oysters, clams, and corals to build shells and skeletons.<br>• Some oyster hatcheries in the Pacific Northwest now add chemicals to buffer the water.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how ocean acidification is affecting a human industry. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The ocean absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide humans release, which has made seawater about 30% more acidic since the 1800s and harder for corals to build skeletons.","Acidic water makes it harder for oysters, clams, and corals to build their shells and skeletons.","Since the 1800s, ocean acidity has increased by about 30% as seawater absorbs carbon dioxide.","Ocean acidification, which hampers shell-building, has led some Pacific Northwest oyster hatcheries to buffer their water chemically."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to show how acidification affects human activity. Only the first choice describes a human response (hatcheries buffering water) to its effects.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan grew up in India and had little formal training.<br>• In 1913, he sent a letter with pages of theorems to G. H. Hardy at Cambridge.<br>• Hardy recognized his genius and invited him to England.<br>• Ramanujan produced nearly 3,900 results, many without proofs.<br>• Some of his results are still being proved and applied today, including in physics.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Ramanujan's lasting influence on mathematics. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1913, Srinivasa Ramanujan, who grew up in India with little formal training, sent a letter with pages of theorems to G. H. Hardy, who invited him to Cambridge.","G. H. Hardy recognized Ramanujan's genius and invited him to England from India.","Ramanujan, largely self-taught, produced nearly 3,900 results, some of which mathematicians are still proving and applying today.","Srinivasa Ramanujan, who grew up in India, had little formal training in mathematics."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to emphasize his lasting influence. Only the first choice notes that his work is still being proved and applied.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• A small town replaced its traffic lights at a busy intersection with a roundabout.<br>• In the three years before, there were 41 crashes, 9 with injuries.<br>• In the three years after, there were 30 crashes, 1 with injuries.<br>• Roundabouts force drivers to slow down.<br>• They eliminate high-speed, right-angle collisions.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the effect of the roundabout on safety and explain why it occurred. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In the three years after the town replaced its traffic lights with a roundabout, there were 30 crashes at the intersection.","After the roundabout replaced traffic lights, injury crashes fell from 9 to 1, likely because roundabouts slow drivers and prevent right-angle collisions.","A small town replaced traffic lights at a busy intersection with a roundabout, which forces drivers to slow down.","Before the town replaced its traffic lights with a roundabout, there were 41 crashes at the intersection over three years, 9 of which caused injuries, compared with 30 afterward."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to show both the result and a reason for it. Only the first choice gives the drop in injury crashes and the explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Jazz musician Mary Lou Williams (1910–1981) was a pianist, composer, and arranger.<br>• She wrote and arranged music for Duke Ellington and Benny Goodman.<br>• In the 1940s, she mentored younger musicians such as Thelonious Monk and Dizzy Gillespie.<br>• Her apartment in Harlem was a gathering place for bebop musicians.<br>• She later composed sacred jazz works, including a jazz Mass.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Williams's influence on the development of bebop. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Mary Lou Williams shaped bebop by mentoring Monk and Gillespie and hosting young musicians in her Harlem apartment.","Mary Lou Williams wrote and arranged music for well-known bandleaders, including Duke Ellington and Benny Goodman.","Mary Lou Williams, a pianist, composer, and arranger who wrote for Duke Ellington and Benny Goodman, later composed sacred jazz works, including a jazz Mass.","In the 1940s, the Harlem apartment of jazz pianist Mary Lou Williams was well known among musicians."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize her role in the development of bebop. Only the first choice describes her mentoring of bebop musicians and her role as host.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested the effect of background music on reading comprehension.<br>• Participants read passages in silence, with instrumental music, or with music that had lyrics.<br>• Scores were similar for silence and instrumental music.<br>• Scores were 12% lower with music that had lyrics.<br>• Researchers suggested that lyrics compete with reading for language processing.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain which kind of background music affected reading comprehension and why. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants read passages in silence, with instrumental music, or with music that had lyrics.","Reading comprehension scores were similar whether participants read in silence or with instrumental music playing.","Researchers suggested that song lyrics may compete with reading for the brain's language processing.","Only music with lyrics hurt reading scores (by 12%), perhaps because lyrics compete with reading for language processing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain which type of music affected comprehension and why. Only the first choice identifies the type, the effect, and the proposed reason.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Spider silk is stronger by weight than steel.<br>• It is also highly elastic.<br>• Spiders cannot be farmed in large numbers because they are territorial and cannibalistic.<br>• Scientists have inserted spider-silk genes into silkworms.<br>• The resulting fibers can be produced on existing silk farms.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how scientists have tried to overcome an obstacle to producing spider silk. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Spider silk, which is stronger by weight than steel and highly elastic, would be valuable, but spiders are territorial and cannibalistic and can't be farmed in large numbers.","Spiders are territorial and cannibalistic, so they cannot be farmed in large numbers for their silk.","Since spiders can't be farmed, scientists put spider-silk genes into silkworms, whose fibers can be produced on existing silk farms.","Scientists have inserted spider-silk genes into silkworms, which are raised on silk farms."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain how scientists addressed a problem. Only the first choice presents both the problem (spiders can't be farmed) and the solution.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 2015, a team in Kenya found stone tools dated to 3.3 million years ago.<br>• The site is called Lomekwi 3.<br>• Before this discovery, the oldest known stone tools were about 2.6 million years old.<br>• The earliest known members of the genus Homo date to about 2.8 million years ago.<br>• The tools may have been made by an earlier hominin species.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Lomekwi 3 tools challenge the idea that the first stone tools were made by members of the genus Homo. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At a site in Kenya called Lomekwi 3, a team found stone tools dated to 3.3 million years ago.","The 3.3-million-year-old Lomekwi 3 tools predate the earliest known Homo by about 500,000 years, suggesting an earlier species made them.","In 2015, a team at a site in Kenya called Lomekwi 3 found stone tools dated to 3.3 million years ago; before that, the oldest known tools were about 2.6 million years old.","The earliest known members of the genus Homo date to about 2.8 million years ago."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why the find challenged the idea that Homo made the first tools. Only the first choice compares the tools' age with the earliest Homo fossils.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Bats are the only mammals capable of true flight.<br>• Bats can carry viruses that make other mammals sick without becoming ill themselves.<br>• Flying raises bats' body temperatures to about 40°C.<br>• Some scientists think this daily "fever" helps bats' immune systems control viruses.<br>• Bats also have unusually long lifespans for their size.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a hypothesis about why bats can carry viruses without becoming ill. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some scientists think the fever-like body temperature bats reach in flight helps them tolerate viruses that sicken other mammals.","Bats, the only mammals capable of true flight, have unusually long lifespans for their size.","Bats can carry viruses that make other mammals sick, and their body temperature rises to about 40°C when they fly.","Bats' immune systems are of interest to scientists because bats carry viruses that sicken other mammals."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present a hypothesis linking flight and viral tolerance. Only the first choice states the proposed connection. The third mentions both facts but doesn't connect them.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Novelist Gabriel García Márquez was born in Colombia in 1927.<br>• One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967) follows seven generations of the Buendía family.<br>• The novel blends realistic detail with fantastic events, such as a character ascending to heaven while folding laundry.<br>• This style is known as magical realism.<br>• The novel has sold more than 50 million copies.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the style of magical realism using an example from the novel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Gabriel García Márquez, born in Colombia in 1927, published One Hundred Years of Solitude in 1967.","Gabriel García Márquez's One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967), which follows seven generations of the Buendía family, has sold more than 50 million copies.","Magical realism is a literary style associated with Gabriel García Márquez and his 1967 novel.","One Hundred Years of Solitude exemplifies magical realism, mixing realistic detail with fantastic events like a woman rising to heaven while folding laundry."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to illustrate magical realism with an example from the novel. Only the first choice defines the style and gives a specific example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tracked carbon storage in two types of forest.<br>• Old-growth forests had trees averaging 300 years old.<br>• Young plantation forests had trees averaging 30 years old.<br>• Old-growth forests stored about 2.5 times as much carbon per hectare.<br>• Plantation trees grew faster and absorbed carbon more quickly each year.$q$, $q$The student wants to present an advantage of each type of forest in relation to carbon. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers compared old-growth forests, with trees averaging 300 years old, to young plantation forests.","Old-growth forests, whose trees average 300 years old, stored about 2.5 times as much carbon per hectare as plantations.","Plantation trees absorb carbon faster each year, but old-growth forests store about 2.5 times as much carbon per hectare.","Plantation forests, with trees averaging 30 years old, grew faster than old-growth forests did."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to present an advantage of each forest type. Only the first choice gives the plantations' faster absorption and the old-growth forests' greater storage.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In the 1930s, the U.S. Great Plains suffered severe dust storms.<br>• Farmers had plowed up native grasses whose roots held the soil.<br>• A drought dried the exposed soil.<br>• Winds carried the soil hundreds of kilometers.<br>• The Soil Conservation Service, created in 1935, promoted planting trees as windbreaks and plowing along contours.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe both the causes of the dust storms and the measures taken to prevent them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In the 1930s, after farmers plowed up native grasses and a drought dried the soil, winds carried soil from the U.S. Great Plains hundreds of kilometers during severe dust storms.","After plowing removed grass roots and drought dried the soil, the Soil Conservation Service promoted windbreaks and contour plowing to hold it in place.","Farmers on the Great Plains had plowed up native grasses before a drought dried out the exposed soil.","The Soil Conservation Service, created in 1935, was a response to severe dust storms on the Great Plains."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to describe causes and responses. Only the first choice includes both the causes (plowing, drought) and the remedies (windbreaks, contour plowing).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Octopuses have about 500 million neurons.<br>• About two-thirds of these neurons are in their arms, not their central brain.<br>• An octopus arm can continue to react to touch after being severed.<br>• Arms can explore and grasp objects without direction from the brain.<br>• Researchers describe octopus intelligence as "distributed."$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what researchers mean when they describe octopus intelligence as "distributed". Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["With two-thirds of their neurons in their arms, octopuses have arms that can explore and grasp objects without direction from the brain.","Octopuses have about 500 million neurons, a large number for an invertebrate animal.","Researchers describe the intelligence of octopuses, which have about 500 million neurons, as \"distributed,\" and a severed arm can even continue to react to touch.","An octopus arm can continue to react to touch even after it has been severed from the body."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to explain what "distributed" intelligence means. Only the first choice explains that neurons are concentrated in the arms and that arms act independently.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The London Underground opened in 1863.<br>• It was the world's first underground railway.<br>• Early trains were pulled by steam locomotives.<br>• Smoke filled the tunnels, and some stations had openings to the street to let it escape.<br>• Electric trains were introduced in 1890 on a deeper line.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a problem with the early Underground and how technology later addressed it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The London Underground, which opened in 1863, was the world's first underground railway.","The London Underground, which opened in 1863 as the world's first underground railway, used steam locomotives until electric trains were introduced in 1890 on a deeper line.","In 1890, electric trains were introduced on a deeper line of the London Underground, which opened in 1863.","Steam trains on the early Underground filled tunnels with smoke, so some stations had openings to let it out—a problem electric trains, from 1890, avoided."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to describe a problem with early trains and how it was later addressed. Only the first choice includes both the smoke problem and the electric solution.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researcher Nalini Nadkarni studies forest canopies.<br>• She found that some trees grow roots from their branches into the soil that collects in the canopy.<br>• This canopy soil forms from decomposing mosses and other plants.<br>• The aerial roots let trees absorb nutrients from high above the ground.<br>• Her findings showed that canopies are complex ecosystems, not just collections of leaves.$q$, $q$The student wants to present one of Nadkarni's findings and explain its broader significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Nalini Nadkarni studies forest canopies, where soil forms from decomposing mosses and other plants and where some trees absorb nutrients from high above the ground.","Canopy soil, which forms from decomposing mosses and other plants, collects high above the forest floor.","Nadkarni found trees growing roots from branches into canopy soil, showing that canopies are complex ecosystems.","Researcher Nalini Nadkarni found that some trees absorb nutrients from high above the ground."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to present a finding and its broader significance. Only the first choice includes the aerial roots and what they reveal about canopies.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Indian Ocean tsunami of 2004 killed more than 225,000 people.<br>• There was no tsunami warning system in the Indian Ocean at the time.<br>• The Pacific Ocean had had a warning system since 1949.<br>• In 2006, an Indian Ocean warning system began operating.<br>• It uses seismic sensors and ocean buoys to detect tsunamis.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a change that followed the 2004 tsunami and the gap it addressed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami killed more than 225,000 people, and in 2006 a warning system using seismic sensors and ocean buoys began operating in the Indian Ocean.","In 2004, the Indian Ocean, unlike the Pacific, had no tsunami warning system; one was established in 2006 using seismic sensors and buoys.","The Pacific Ocean has had a tsunami warning system since 1949, which uses sensors to detect tsunamis.","A tsunami warning system that began operating in the Indian Ocean in 2006 uses seismic sensors and ocean buoys."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how the 2004 disaster led to change. Only the first choice describes the missing system and the new system created afterward.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested whether honeybees can understand zero.<br>• Bees were trained to choose the image with fewer dots.<br>• When then shown an image with no dots, bees chose it over images with one or more dots.<br>• Understanding zero as a number was once thought to be limited to humans and a few primates.<br>• Bee brains contain fewer than one million neurons.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize why the finding about honeybees is surprising. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Bees, with fewer than a million neurons, treated an empty image as \"fewer\" than one dot—suggesting an understanding of zero once thought limited to primates.","Honeybees, whose brains contain fewer than one million neurons, were trained to choose the image with fewer dots and were then shown an image containing no dots at all.","Understanding zero as a number was once thought to be limited to humans and a few other primates.","Bee brains, which contain fewer than one million neurons, are much smaller than primate brains."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize how surprising the finding is. Only the first choice combines the bees' small brains, their behavior, and the earlier belief about zero.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some cities are adding "green roofs"—rooftops covered with plants.<br>• Green roofs absorb rainwater, reducing runoff into storm drains.<br>• They also insulate buildings, lowering heating and cooling costs.<br>• Installing a green roof costs about twice as much as a conventional roof.<br>• Green roofs can last up to twice as long as conventional roofs.$q$, $q$The student wants to respond to the concern that green roofs are too expensive. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Green roofs, which are rooftops covered with plants, absorb rainwater and reduce runoff into city storm drains.","Some cities are adding green roofs, which insulate buildings and lower their heating and cooling costs.","Installing a green roof, a rooftop covered with plants, costs about twice as much as a conventional roof.","Green roofs cost about twice as much to install, but they can last twice as long and cut heating and cooling costs."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to address the cost objection. Only the first choice acknowledges the higher cost and offers financial counterpoints.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Sculptor Augusta Savage (1892–1962) was a leading artist of the Harlem Renaissance.<br>• In 1923, she was rejected from a summer art program in France because she was Black.<br>• She publicly protested the decision.<br>• She later founded the Savage Studio of Arts and Crafts in Harlem.<br>• Her students included painters Jacob Lawrence and Gwendolyn Knight.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Savage's role as a teacher of other artists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sculptor Augusta Savage was a leading artist of the Harlem Renaissance who lived from 1892 to 1962.","Augusta Savage, a sculptor of the Harlem Renaissance who was rejected from a program in France in 1923 because she was Black, later founded a studio in Harlem.","Augusta Savage nurtured the next generation of artists, teaching Jacob Lawrence and Gwendolyn Knight at her Harlem studio.","Augusta Savage founded the Savage Studio of Arts and Crafts in Harlem after being rejected from a program in France."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to emphasize her influence as a teacher. Only the first choice names her notable students.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Mississippi River delta is losing land.<br>• Since the 1930s, Louisiana has lost about 5,000 square kilometers of coastal land.<br>• Levees built to prevent flooding also stop the river from depositing sediment on the delta.<br>• Without new sediment, the land sinks and erodes.<br>• Engineers are building channels to divert sediment-rich water back into the wetlands.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how engineers are trying to address a cause of land loss in the delta. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Since the 1930s, Louisiana has lost about 5,000 square kilometers of coastal land in the Mississippi River delta, where levees were built to prevent flooding.","Because levees block the sediment that rebuilds the delta, engineers are building channels to send sediment-rich water back into the wetlands.","Levees built along the Mississippi River prevent flooding in the communities along its banks.","Without new sediment, the land in the Mississippi River delta sinks and erodes over time."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain the engineers' solution and the problem it addresses. Only the first choice connects levees to the loss of sediment and describes the diversion channels.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Japanese bullet train, or Shinkansen, began service in 1964.<br>• Its trains reach speeds of up to 320 kilometers per hour.<br>• In nearly 60 years of operation, no passenger has died in a derailment or collision.<br>• Its trains have an average delay of under one minute.<br>• Earthquake sensors automatically stop trains when tremors are detected.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the safety record of the Shinkansen. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In nearly 60 years, no Shinkansen passenger has died in a derailment or collision, aided by sensors that stop trains during earthquakes.","Japan's Shinkansen, which began service in 1964 and reaches speeds of up to 320 kilometers per hour, has trains with an average delay of under one minute.","The trains of the Shinkansen, Japan's bullet train system, have an average delay of under one minute.","Earthquake sensors on Japan's Shinkansen automatically stop trains when they detect tremors."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to emphasize the system's safety record. Only the first choice gives the record and a safety feature contributing to it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied whether handwriting notes helps learning more than typing.<br>• Students who typed notes recorded more words, often copying lectures nearly verbatim.<br>• Students who wrote by hand recorded fewer words but summarized in their own words.<br>• On conceptual questions, the handwriting group scored higher.<br>• On factual questions, the groups scored about the same.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's findings and a possible explanation for them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Students who typed their notes recorded more words than students who wrote by hand, often copying lectures nearly verbatim.","Researchers compared students who took notes by hand with those who typed, testing both conceptual and factual understanding.","On factual questions, students who typed notes and students who wrote them by hand scored about the same.","Handwriting students, forced to summarize rather than copy, outscored typists on conceptual questions, though the groups tied on factual ones."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to present the findings with an explanation. Only the first choice gives the conceptual advantage, the tie on factual questions, and the reason (summarizing).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Nobel Prize–winning chemist Marie Curie discovered polonium and radium.<br>• She named polonium after Poland, her homeland.<br>• Poland was not an independent country at the time; it was divided among Russia, Prussia, and Austria.<br>• Curie hoped the name would draw attention to Poland's lack of independence.<br>• Poland regained independence in 1918.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the political meaning behind the name Curie chose for polonium. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie, a Nobel Prize–winning chemist from Poland, discovered both polonium and radium, and Poland regained its independence in 1918.","Poland, Marie Curie's homeland, regained its independence in 1918 after being divided among Russia, Prussia, and Austria.","Curie named polonium after Poland, then divided among three empires, hoping to draw attention to its lack of independence.","Marie Curie discovered polonium and named it after Poland, the country where she was born."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain the political significance of the name. Only the first choice includes Poland's divided status and Curie's intention.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Some desert beetles in Namibia collect water from fog.<br>• Their backs have bumps that attract water droplets and grooves that repel water.<br>• Droplets grow on the bumps and roll down the grooves into the beetles' mouths.<br>• Engineers have used this design to create fog-harvesting nets.<br>• In some villages, such nets collect hundreds of liters of water a day.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how a feature of the Namibian beetle has been applied to benefit people. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some desert beetles in Namibia collect water from fog: droplets form on bumps on their backs and roll down water-repelling grooves into the beetles' mouths.","Engineers have copied the Namibian beetle's water-attracting bumps and water-repelling grooves to make nets that collect hundreds of liters of water daily.","Droplets grow on bumps on a Namibian beetle's back and roll down grooves into its mouth.","In some villages, fog-harvesting nets collect hundreds of liters of water each day for residents."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how the beetle has benefited humans. Only the first choice links the beetle's design to the nets and their output.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied children's willingness to share.<br>• Children who were given a puppet to care for shared more stickers afterward than those who were not.<br>• The effect lasted only for the session in which children cared for the puppet.<br>• Children who watched an adult share showed no change.<br>• Researchers suggested that caregiving experiences may temporarily increase generosity.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a key finding of the study while noting a limitation of that finding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Caring for a puppet increased children's sharing, but only during that session—suggesting caregiving may boost generosity temporarily.","Children who watched an adult share stickers showed no change in their own willingness to share.","Researchers studied whether caring for a puppet changed children's willingness to share stickers, and they also tested children who watched an adult share.","Children who were given a puppet to care for shared more stickers afterward than children who were not."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to present a finding along with its limitation. Only the first choice includes the increase in sharing and the fact that it didn't last.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Hurricanes draw energy from warm ocean water.<br>• They usually weaken over land.<br>• In 2017, Hurricane Harvey stalled over Houston for four days.<br>• It dropped more than 1,500 millimeters of rain in some areas.<br>• Scientists found that warmer Gulf of Mexico waters made the rainfall about 15% heavier.$q$, $q$The student wants to present evidence of a link between ocean temperatures and Hurricane Harvey's rainfall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 2017, Hurricane Harvey stalled over Houston for four days, dropping more than 1,500 millimeters of rain in places.","Hurricanes, which draw their energy from warm ocean water, usually weaken once they move over land.","Hurricane Harvey, which struck Houston in 2017, drew energy from warm ocean water in the Gulf of Mexico.","Scientists found that warmer Gulf waters made Hurricane Harvey's record rainfall over Houston about 15% heavier."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to show a connection between warm water and rainfall. Only the first choice states the scientists' finding that warmer water increased Harvey's rainfall.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers in Kenya tested whether beehive fences could protect farms from elephants.<br>• Elephants avoid bees, which can sting them around their eyes and trunks.<br>• Farms with beehive fences had 80% fewer elephant raids than unfenced farms.<br>• Farmers also earned income by selling honey from the hives.<br>• Beehive fences cost less than electric fences.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize two benefits that beehive fences provided to farmers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Elephants avoid bees, which can sting them around their eyes and trunks, so beehives can deter them.","Researchers in Kenya tested whether beehive fences could protect farms from raids by elephants.","Beehive fences cut elephant raids by 80% and also gave farmers honey to sell.","Beehive fences cost less than electric fences and use bees that elephants try to avoid."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to emphasize two benefits for farmers. Only the first choice includes both the reduction in raids and the honey income.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Amazon River carries about 20% of all river water that flows into the world's oceans.<br>• It has no bridges across its main channel.<br>• Much of the river runs through sparsely populated rainforest.<br>• Its riverbanks shift frequently due to seasonal flooding.<br>• Ferries and boats carry people and goods across the river.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Amazon River has no bridges across its main channel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Amazon River carries about 20% of all river water that flows into the world's oceans.","The Amazon has no bridges across its main channel, partly because it crosses sparsely populated land and its banks shift with seasonal floods.","Ferries and boats carry people and goods across the Amazon River, which carries about 20% of all river water flowing into the oceans and runs through sparsely populated rainforest.","The Amazon River's banks shift frequently because of the seasonal flooding that occurs there."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why there are no bridges. Only the first choice gives reasons for the absence of bridges.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In medieval Europe, books were copied by hand.<br>• Copying a single Bible could take a scribe more than a year.<br>• Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press with movable metal type around 1440.<br>• His press could produce about 3,600 pages a day.<br>• By 1500, an estimated 20 million books had been printed in Europe.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the speed of hand copying with that of Gutenberg's press. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Where a scribe might need a year to copy one Bible, Gutenberg's press could print about 3,600 pages a day.","Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press with movable metal type around 1440.","By 1500, an estimated 20 million books had been printed in Europe, which had once relied on scribes.","In medieval Europe, books were copied by hand, so producing a book required a great deal of time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to contrast the speed of the two methods. Only the first choice compares the time required for hand copying with the press's daily output.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The axial tilt of Earth is about 23.5 degrees.<br>• This tilt causes the seasons.<br>• Mars has a similar tilt, about 25 degrees, so it also has seasons.<br>• A Martian year lasts about 687 Earth days.<br>• As a result, each Martian season lasts roughly twice as long as an Earth season.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a similarity and a difference between seasons on Earth and on Mars. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Earth's axial tilt of about 23.5 degrees causes the planet's seasons.","Mars has an axial tilt of about 25 degrees, similar to Earth's tilt of about 23.5 degrees—the tilt that is responsible for Earth's seasons.","A year on Mars lasts about 687 Earth days, much longer than a year on Earth.","Like Earth, Mars has seasons because of its tilt, but with a 687-day year, each Martian season lasts about twice as long."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to give both a similarity and a difference. Only the first choice states that both planets have tilt-driven seasons and that Martian seasons are longer.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers tested drivers in a simulator.<br>• Drivers talking on a hands-free phone missed twice as many traffic signals as undistracted drivers.<br>• Drivers talking with a passenger did not show this effect.<br>• Passengers tended to stop talking when traffic became difficult.<br>• People on the phone could not see the traffic and kept talking.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why talking on a hands-free phone affected drivers differently than talking with a passenger. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Drivers talking on a hands-free phone missed twice as many traffic signals as undistracted drivers in a simulator.","Drivers talking with a passenger in a simulator did not miss more traffic signals than undistracted drivers did.","Unlike phone callers, passengers can see traffic and pause when driving gets hard, which may be why only hands-free calls doubled missed signals.","Researchers tested drivers in a simulator and found that those talking on a hands-free phone missed twice as many traffic signals as undistracted drivers did."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain why the two kinds of conversation had different effects. Only the first choice gives the explanation (passengers adjust to traffic).$q$),
  ($q$ei-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Nazca Lines are large designs etched into the desert in southern Peru.<br>• They were made between 500 BCE and 500 CE.<br>• Some depict animals, such as a monkey and a hummingbird, and are hundreds of meters long.<br>• The designs are best seen from the air or nearby hills.<br>• The region's dry, windless climate has preserved them for centuries.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Nazca Lines have survived for so long. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Nazca Lines, made between 500 BCE and 500 CE, are large designs etched into the desert in southern Peru.","The Nazca Lines have survived for centuries because the region's dry, windless climate has preserved them.","Some Nazca Lines depict animals, such as a monkey and a hummingbird, and are hundreds of meters long.","The Nazca Lines, which include designs of animals, are best seen from the air or from nearby hills."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why the lines still exist. Only the first choice gives the reason for their preservation.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied how much water two crops need.<br>• Producing one kilogram of rice requires about 2,500 liters of water.<br>• Producing one kilogram of millet requires about 1,500 liters.<br>• Millet tolerates drought and poor soil.<br>• India declared 2023 the International Year of Millets to encourage its cultivation.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that millet is better suited than rice to regions with limited water. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Millet needs about 1,000 fewer liters of water per kilogram than rice and tolerates drought, making it suited to water-scarce regions.","Producing one kilogram of rice requires about 2,500 liters of water, and in 2023, which India declared the International Year of Millets, farmers were encouraged to grow millet.","India declared 2023 the International Year of Millets to encourage farmers to grow the crop.","Millet, a crop that tolerates drought and poor soil, was the focus of the International Year of Millets."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to argue that millet suits dry regions. Only the first choice compares water needs and notes drought tolerance.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers measured how long people could hold a plank exercise.<br>• Participants who were told the exercise would last 60 seconds held it longer than those not given a time.<br>• Participants who were told it would last 30 seconds stopped soon after 30 seconds.<br>• The researchers concluded that expectations affect physical endurance.<br>• All participants were similarly fit.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the researchers' conclusion and the evidence that supports it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Participants told that a plank would last 60 seconds held it longer than those given no time, while those told it would last 30 seconds stopped soon after 30 seconds.","Researchers measured how long participants, all similarly fit, could hold a plank exercise.","Participants who were told that the plank would last 30 seconds stopped soon after that time had passed.","Because similarly fit participants stopped near whatever time they expected, researchers concluded that expectations shape endurance."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to present the conclusion with its supporting evidence. Only the first choice notes that participants were equally fit (ruling out fitness) and that they stopped near their expected times.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Ancient Polynesian navigators sailed thousands of kilometers across the Pacific.<br>• They did not use compasses or charts.<br>• They read the stars, ocean swells, and the flight paths of birds.<br>• In 1976, the canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional methods.<br>• The voyage helped disprove claims that Polynesians had settled islands by accident.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the historical significance of the Hōkūleʻa voyage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ancient Polynesian navigators sailed thousands of kilometers without compasses or charts, reading the stars, ocean swells, and the flight paths of birds instead.","Polynesian navigators read the stars, ocean swells, and the flight paths of birds to find their way.","The 1976 voyage of Hōkūleʻa, guided only by traditional methods, helped show that Polynesians settled distant islands deliberately.","In 1976, the canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti across thousands of kilometers of ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to explain the significance of the Hōkūleʻa voyage. Only the first choice links it to disproving the accidental-settlement claim.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The quagga was a type of zebra with stripes only on its front half.<br>• It lived in South Africa and was hunted to extinction by 1883.<br>• DNA studies showed the quagga was a subspecies of the plains zebra.<br>• The Quagga Project has bred plains zebras with fewer stripes.<br>• After several generations, some foals closely resemble quaggas.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why scientists believed they could re-create the quagga's appearance through breeding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The quagga, a zebra with stripes only on its front half, lived in South Africa until 1883.","Because the extinct quagga was a plains-zebra subspecies, breeders have produced quagga-like foals from plains zebras with fewer stripes.","The Quagga Project has bred plains zebras with fewer stripes over several generations, and some of the resulting foals now closely resemble the extinct quagga.","DNA studies showed that the quagga, which was hunted to extinction by 1883, was a type of zebra."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain why the breeding project is possible. Only the first choice connects the DNA finding (quagga as plains-zebra subspecies) to the project's approach.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Researchers studied the sleep of college students during exams.<br>• Students who slept fewer than six hours before an exam scored 10% lower on average.<br>• Many students believed that staying up late to study would improve their scores.<br>• Students who slept at least eight hours scored highest.<br>• Sleep helps the brain consolidate memories.$q$, $q$The student wants to challenge a common belief among students about studying late. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Contrary to many students' belief, those who slept fewer than six hours before exams scored 10% lower on average.","Students who slept at least eight hours before an exam scored highest, researchers found.","Researchers studied the sleep of college students during exam periods to learn how it affected scores.","Sleep helps the brain consolidate memories, which is important for students preparing for exams."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is to challenge a common belief. Only the first choice states the belief and the evidence against it.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Venus flytrap is native to a small area of North and South Carolina.<br>• It grows in nutrient-poor soil.<br>• It catches insects to obtain nitrogen.<br>• Its trap closes only if a trigger hair is touched twice within about 20 seconds.<br>• This prevents the plant from wasting energy on raindrops or debris.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Venus flytrap avoids closing on things other than prey. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Venus flytrap, native to a small area of the Carolinas, catches insects to get nitrogen missing from its soil, closing its trap when a trigger hair is touched.","The Venus flytrap grows in nutrient-poor soil in a small area of North and South Carolina.","The Venus flytrap's trap closes when a trigger hair is touched, allowing it to catch insects.","By closing only when a trigger hair is touched twice within about 20 seconds, the flytrap avoids wasting energy on raindrops or debris."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal is to explain how the flytrap avoids false triggers. Only the first choice describes the two-touch mechanism and its purpose.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• In 2014, a landslide in Washington State destroyed the community of Oso.<br>• Heavy rain had saturated the hillside.<br>• The hill had experienced smaller landslides for decades.<br>• A 1999 report had warned of the risk of a large failure.<br>• After the disaster, the state increased funding for landslide mapping.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the disaster had been anticipated but that action was taken only afterward. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 2014, after heavy rain saturated a hillside that had experienced smaller landslides for decades, a landslide destroyed the community of Oso in Washington State.","The hillside near Oso, Washington, had experienced smaller landslides for decades before 2014.","Although a 1999 report had warned of the danger at Oso, it was only after the 2014 disaster that the state increased funding for landslide mapping.","A report written in 1999 warned of the risk of a large landslide on the hill near Oso, Washington."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal is to show that warnings preceded the disaster and that action followed only afterward. Only the first choice includes both.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Galápagos marine iguana is the only lizard that forages in the sea.<br>• It eats algae growing on underwater rocks.<br>• It swallows a great deal of salt while feeding.<br>• Special glands near its nose remove excess salt.<br>• It expels the salt by sneezing.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the marine iguana copes with a challenge created by its diet. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Galápagos marine iguana is the only lizard in the world that forages for food in the sea.","Marine iguanas, which swallow salt while eating underwater algae, rid themselves of it through nasal glands and by sneezing.","Galápagos marine iguanas, the only lizards that forage in the sea, eat algae growing on underwater rocks and have special glands near their noses.","Marine iguanas of the Galápagos have special glands near their noses."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal is to explain how the iguana deals with a challenge of its diet. Only the first choice includes both the challenge (swallowing salt) and the solution.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$For decades, ecologists assumed that the decline of the region's songbirds was driven mainly by the loss of breeding habitat in North American forests. A 2019 tracking study complicated this picture: many of the birds were dying not in their summer ranges but during migration and in their wintering grounds in Central America. ______ conservation efforts focused only on protecting northern forests were unlikely, on their own, to reverse the decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final sentence states a conclusion that follows from the study's finding that birds were dying elsewhere. "Accordingly" signals that the conclusion is a logical consequence. "Nevertheless" signals contrast, "Similarly" signals a parallel point, and "For instance" introduces an example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The novelist's early books were praised for their lush, elaborate sentences, some of which ran for more than a page. Her later work, written after she began teaching high school English, is strikingly different. ______ her final novel contains hardly a sentence longer than fifteen words.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Regardless,","Meanwhile,","Indeed,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The final sentence strengthens the claim that her later work is "strikingly different" by giving an emphatic illustration. "Indeed" signals that the sentence reinforces and intensifies the previous point. "However" would signal a contrast with the previous sentence, which is not present.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Most species of bats navigate by echolocation, emitting high-pitched calls and listening for the echoes. Fruit bats of the genus Pteropus, ______ rely largely on their keen eyesight and sense of smell to find the ripe fruit they eat.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["as a result,","in addition,","by contrast,","for example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence presents fruit bats as an exception to the echolocation described for most species. "By contrast" correctly signals this difference. The other options signal cause, addition, or example.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The proposed dam would provide electricity for nearly a million homes and protect downstream towns from seasonal floods. ______ it would also flood a valley that contains some of the oldest rock art in the region, a fact that has led archaeologists to oppose the project.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus","Yet","Likewise,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence presents a drawback that stands in tension with the benefits listed in the first. "Yet" signals this contrast. "Thus" signals a result, "Likewise" a similarity, and "In other words" a restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$To test whether crows can recognize individual human faces, researchers wore a particular mask while trapping and banding several birds. Years later, crows in the area still scolded and dive-bombed anyone wearing that mask, even though most of them had never been trapped themselves. ______ the birds seemed to have learned to fear the mask from other crows.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","On the other hand,","Even so,","Second,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The last sentence restates and interprets the observation in the previous sentence: crows that had never been trapped still reacted to the mask, meaning they learned from others. "In other words" signals this clarifying restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The first commercial jet airliner entered service in 1952 and could carry thirty-six passengers. ______ within two years, several of the planes had broken apart in midair, and investigators eventually traced the failures to metal fatigue around the aircraft's square windows.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Similarly,","In summary,","Tragically, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence describes a disastrous turn that contrasts with the milestone described in the first. "Tragically, however" captures both the contrast and the tone. "Moreover" adds a similar point, "Similarly" draws a parallel, and "In summary" introduces a conclusion.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Many plants defend themselves against insects with chemical weapons rather than thorns or tough leaves. ______ when caterpillars begin chewing on a tomato plant, the plant produces compounds that interfere with the insects' digestion and releases airborne signals that prompt neighboring plants to do the same.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","For example,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence gives a specific case of a plant using chemical defenses, illustrating the general claim in the first sentence. "For example" signals this.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The museum's new climate-control system was designed to keep humidity in the galleries between 45 and 55 percent. ______ it held humidity at exactly 50 percent for the first six months, until a faulty sensor caused levels to spike during an unusually wet August.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","Initially,","Nonetheless,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence describes how the system performed at first, before a later problem. "Initially" signals the first stage of a sequence that is later interrupted. The other choices signal contrast or similarity, which do not fit.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The candidate's campaign had raised more money than any other in the state's history, and early polls showed her with a comfortable lead. ______ she lost the election by nearly eight percentage points, a result that analysts attributed to low turnout among her supporters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Still,","Hence,","Likewise,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The loss contradicts what the fundraising and early polls would lead one to expect. "Still" signals that the outcome occurred despite those advantages. "Hence" signals a result, "Likewise" a similarity, and "Specifically" an elaboration.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$Critics have argued that the new prize rewards commercial success rather than literary merit. ______ several of this year's finalists were best-selling novels, but the winning book—a slim collection of experimental poems—had sold fewer than two thousand copies before the announcement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","In addition,","In other words,","Granted,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence concedes part of the critics' point (some finalists were best sellers) before undercutting it (the winner was not). "Granted" signals such a concession. The other choices signal a result, an addition, or a restatement.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that a mold contaminating one of his bacterial cultures had killed the bacteria around it. He published his observation the following year, but he was unable to purify the active substance in large quantities. ______ it was not until the early 1940s that a team at Oxford developed a way to produce penicillin as a practical drug.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In comparison,","Even so,","As a result,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Because Fleming could not purify the substance, a practical drug had to wait for later work. "As a result" signals this cause-and-effect relationship. "Even so" would wrongly suggest that the delay happened despite his difficulty.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The language has no word that corresponds exactly to the English word "blue." ______ it has separate words for the light blue of the sky and the dark blue of deep water, and speakers treat these as two distinct colors, much as English speakers treat red and pink.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Rather,","Likewise,","Ultimately,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence corrects a possible misunderstanding created by the first: the language doesn't lack words for blue; it divides blue into two colors. "Rather" signals this corrective alternative.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The researchers had planned to collect soil samples from all forty sites in the national park. ______ flooding closed the park's southern roads for most of the summer, and they were able to reach only twenty-six sites.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Accordingly,","Moreover,","In fact,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains how the plan was disrupted, which contrasts with the original intention. "However" signals this contrast.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Romans built many of their harbors with a type of concrete that has survived two thousand years of pounding waves. Modern concrete, ______ often begins to crumble after only a few decades of exposure to seawater.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["for example,","in turn,","as such,","by comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence contrasts modern concrete with the durable Roman concrete. "By comparison" signals this contrast. "For example" would present modern concrete as an example of the Roman concrete's durability.$q$),
  ($q$ei-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The startup's founders believed that customers would pay a premium for groceries delivered within fifteen minutes. ______ they raised more than $100 million to build a network of small warehouses in dense urban neighborhoods.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["On the contrary,","Similarly,","To that end,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence describes an action taken in order to achieve the goal implied in the first (fast delivery). "To that end" signals an action taken for a stated purpose.$q$)
on conflict (id) do update set
  module = excluded.module,
  domain = excluded.domain,
  skill = excluded.skill,
  difficulty = excluded.difficulty,
  passage = excluded.passage,
  prompt = excluded.prompt,
  choices = excluded.choices,
  answer = excluded.answer,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;
